(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e4b.cc947c3634.js"]={"44":[{"id":"sdl44_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":1,"stem":"Which portion of the nephron contains the organic acid secretory systems that deliver many diuretics into the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"Medullary thick ascending limb","B":"Early distal convoluted tubule","C":"Cortical collecting duct","D":"Inner medullary collecting duct","E":"Proximal tubule S2 segment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Organic acid secretory systems are located in the middle third of the straight part of the proximal tubule, the S2 segment. They secrete organic acids such as uric acid, NSAIDs, diuretics, and antibiotics from the blood into the luminal fluid, which helps deliver most diuretics to the luminal side of the tubule where they act. Organic base systems for creatinine and choline lie in the S1 and S2 segments. The thick ascending limb and the collecting ducts are sites where diuretics act from the lumen, not where the organic acid secretory systems sit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: organic acid secretion in the proximal tubule delivers diuretics to the lumen, and NSAIDs and probenecid compete for weak acid secretion. COMLEX: a patient on a loop diuretic who starts an NSAID may respond less well to the diuretic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 and Obj 3 — clearance ratios distinguish net secretion from net reabsorption, and endogenous markers estimate GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of plasma solutes accumulates almost in proportion to nephron loss because their excretion depends largely on glomerular filtration?","choices":{"A":"Sodium and chloride","B":"Creatinine and urea","C":"Phosphate and urate","D":"Glucose and amino acids","E":"Calcium and magnesium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Creatinine and urea depend largely on glomerular filtration for excretion and are not reabsorbed as avidly as electrolytes, so they accumulate almost in proportion to the number of nephrons lost. Plasma sodium and chloride stay nearly constant even with severe GFR reduction because tubular reabsorption falls, and phosphate, urate, and hydrogen ion stay near normal until GFR falls below 20 to 30 percent of normal and then rise less than proportionately. Glucose and amino acids are normally almost fully reabsorbed in the proximal tubule rather than excreted by filtration, and calcium and magnesium are regulated electrolytes rather than filtration-dependent wastes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: at steady state creatinine excretion equals production, so plasma creatinine rises as GFR falls. COMLEX: rising creatinine and urea are the laboratory markers used to follow loss of kidney function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 and Obj 3 — clearance ratios distinguish net secretion from net reabsorption, and endogenous markers estimate GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":1,"stem":"By which process is intravenous mannitol mainly eliminated from the body?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular filtration","B":"Proximal tubular secretion","C":"Hepatic metabolism","D":"Biliary excretion","E":"Distal tubular secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mannitol is not metabolized and is excreted by glomerular filtration within 30 to 60 minutes, without any important tubular reabsorption or secretion. It is poorly absorbed from the gut, so systemic effects require intravenous administration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: because mannitol depends on filtration for excretion, a low GFR leaves it in the blood and risks volume overload. COMLEX: mannitol is used cautiously even in mild renal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 and Obj 3 — clearance ratios distinguish net secretion from net reabsorption, and endogenous markers estimate GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone released after diuretic-induced volume depletion increases K+ secretion by the collecting duct?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid hormone","B":"Glucagon","C":"Calcitonin","D":"Aldosterone","E":"Thyroxine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diuretics cause total body volume depletion, which stimulates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to make more aldosterone. Aldosterone increases ENaC number, Na+/K+-ATPase activity, and K+ channel number in principal cells, so K+ is secreted and lost in the urine. Increased tubular flow adds to the K+ loss. Parathyroid hormone, glucagon, calcitonin and thyroxine are not the volume-driven hormone that adds ENaC, pumps and K+ channels to principal cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop and thiazide diuretics cause hypokalemia through higher distal flow and aldosterone. COMLEX: heart failure patients on loop diuretics need careful monitoring, because hypokalemia raises arrhythmia risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 and SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — regulation of principal cell K+ secretion, and how a nonreabsorbed luminal solute alters proximal water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which diuretic class keeps water in the lumen with a nonreabsorbed solute, rather than by inhibiting one transporter?","choices":{"A":"Loop diuretics","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Osmotic diuretics","D":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","E":"Potassium-sparing diuretics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Osmotic diuretics are freely filtered but not reabsorbed, so they act as an effective osmole that keeps water in the tubule, with the strongest effect in the proximal tubule and the same mechanism throughout the nephron. Loop diuretics block NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, thiazides block the Na+/Cl- symporter in the distal tubule, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act in the proximal tubule, and K+-sparing agents act in the collecting duct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mannitol acts throughout the nephron with no single transporter target. COMLEX: mannitol is mainly used for elevated intracranial or intraocular pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 and SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — regulation of principal cell K+ secretion, and how a nonreabsorbed luminal solute alters proximal water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Which force drives K+ secretion across the luminal membrane of collecting duct principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic gradient from tubular water reabsorption","B":"Proton gradient from intercalated cell H+ secretion","C":"Calcium gradient from paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption","D":"Lumen-negative potential from Na+ reabsorption","E":"Hydrostatic gradient from increased tubular flow rate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the collecting duct Na+ is reabsorbed and K+ is secreted. Na+ reabsorption generates an electrical gradient that makes the lumen negative with respect to the cell, and this drives K+ secretion. ENaC blockers reduce this gradient by making the lumen more positive, which lowers K+ secretion and spares K+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lumen-negative potential from ENaC-mediated Na+ entry explains why K+-sparing diuretics conserve K+. COMLEX: K+-sparing diuretics are used as adjuncts to K+-wasting diuretics to avoid hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 and SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — regulation of principal cell K+ secretion, and how a nonreabsorbed luminal solute alters proximal water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter couples Na+ entry to H+ secretion during proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter","B":"Apical Na+/Cl- symporter","C":"Basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase","D":"Apical H+/K+ ATPase","E":"Apical Na+/H+ antiporter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCO3- reabsorption begins with an Na+/H+ antiporter that pumps sodium into the proximal tubule cell and hydrogen ions into the lumen. Luminal H+ combines with filtered HCO3- to form H2CO3, carbonic anhydrase converts it to CO2 and water, and the CO2 diffuses into the cell. Because bicarbonate reabsorption is linked to Na+ reabsorption through this antiporter, blocking carbonic anhydrase reduces both. Na+/K+/2Cl- and Na+/Cl- cotransport belong to the thick ascending limb and early distal tubule, the Na+/K+ ATPase is basolateral and does not secrete H+, and the H+/K+ ATPase is in alpha-intercalated cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide reduces Na+ and HCO3- reabsorption because the two are linked through the Na+/H+ antiporter. COMLEX: the effect is a mild diuresis, because other transporters compensate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 and SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation mechanics and the consequences of defective proximal reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal tubular disorder is mimicked by treatment with a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 RTA","B":"Type 4 RTA","C":"Type 2 RTA","D":"Gitelman syndrome","E":"Bartter syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower serum pH and produce a drug-induced proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis with hypokalemia, caused by the tubule inability to reabsorb filtered HCO3-. Urine becomes more alkaline than normal because of the extra bicarbonate. Gitelman and Bartter syndromes mimic thiazide and loop diuretics and cause metabolic alkalosis, not acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists proximal RTA (type 2) as an adverse effect of acetazolamide. COMLEX: acetazolamide causes acidosis, and its dose must be reduced in renal insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 and SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation mechanics and the consequences of defective proximal reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which electrolyte abnormality accompanies the metabolic acidosis produced by acetazolamide?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalcemia","B":"Hypernatremia","C":"Hypermagnesemia","D":"Hypokalemia","E":"Hyperphosphatemia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors induce a metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia, a drug-induced proximal type 2 RTA. Potassium is excreted along with the bicarbonate that the tubule cannot reabsorb. The acidosis is paired with potassium loss, not with high calcium, sodium, magnesium or phosphate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hypokalemia with proximal RTA among the adverse effects of acetazolamide. COMLEX: the alkaline urine can promote calcium phosphate stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 and SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation mechanics and the consequences of defective proximal reabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The acidosis is paired with po","B":"The acidosis is paired with po","C":"The acidosis is paired with po","E":"The acidosis is paired with po"}},{"id":"sdl44_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism lowers intraocular pressure when a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor treats open-angle glaucoma?","choices":{"A":"Decreased aqueous humor formation by the ciliary body","B":"Increased outflow through the trabecular meshwork","C":"Increased outflow through the uveoscleral pathway","D":"Osmotic movement of water out of the vitreous body","E":"Constriction of the pupil to open the drainage angle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ciliary body makes aqueous humor, a solution of mainly Na+ and HCO3-, using CO2 and intracellular carbonic anhydrase to form HCO3-. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease HCO3- production, which reduces aqueous humor production and lowers intraocular pressure. Topical agents such as dorzolamide and brinzolamide act locally. Increasing trabecular or uveoscleral outflow and pupillary constriction are actions of other glaucoma drugs, the cholinomimetics and prostaglandin analogs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide, dorzolamide, brinzolamide, and methazolamide all treat glaucoma by reducing aqueous humor production. COMLEX: glaucoma is the most common use of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors overall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation depends on intracellular carbonic anhydrase","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Increasing trabecular or uveos"}},{"id":"sdl44_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure produces the fluid whose formation carbonic anhydrase inhibitors reduce in order to lower intracranial pressure?","choices":{"A":"Ciliary body","B":"Arachnoid granulations","C":"Trabecular meshwork","D":"Choroid plexus","E":"Subarachnoid space"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The choroid plexus generates cerebrospinal fluid. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors inhibit intracellular carbonic anhydrase, reduce bicarbonate production, and so reduce CSF production and intracranial pressure in idiopathic intracranial hypertension. The ciliary body makes aqueous humor and the trabecular meshwork drains it, while arachnoid granulations and the subarachnoid space reabsorb and hold CSF rather than produce it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide is used for idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri). COMLEX: this condition has raised intracranial pressure of unknown cause, with no tumor, infection, or structural defect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation depends on intracellular carbonic anhydrase","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":1,"stem":"By which mechanism does acetazolamide prevent altitude sickness?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate retention that dampens breathing","B":"Carbon dioxide retention that raises oxygen tension","C":"Metabolic acidosis that drives hyperventilation","D":"Pulmonary vasodilation that improves gas exchange","E":"Blood volume expansion that raises oxygen delivery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypoxemia at altitude causes hyperventilation, CO2 loss, and respiratory alkalosis, with headache and nausea. Acetazolamide blocks carbonic anhydrase in the kidney, increases bicarbonate excretion, and induces metabolic acidosis, which causes compensatory hyperventilation that increases arterial oxygen. Its benefit comes from the acid-base effect that drives breathing, not from pulmonary vasodilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists altitude sickness as an acetazolamide indication because it offsets respiratory alkalosis. COMLEX: patients with a history of altitude sickness take the drug when they ascend to high elevation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal bicarbonate reclamation depends on intracellular carbonic anhydrase","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main clinical indication for mannitol?","choices":{"A":"Chronic heart failure with edema","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","C":"Prevention of altitude sickness","D":"Recurrent calcium kidney stones","E":"Elevated intracranial pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The most important use of mannitol is to reduce elevated intracranial pressure from CNS infection, trauma, or brain tumors, by drawing water out of the brain into the blood. It is occasionally used for intraocular pressure in acute angle-closure glaucoma. It is contraindicated in congestive heart failure. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and calcium stones from hypercalciuria are thiazide indications, and altitude sickness is prevented with acetazolamide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists elevated intracranial or intraocular pressure and drug overdose among the uses of mannitol. COMLEX: it is usually given in an intensive care setting with close monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — luminal osmotic agents such as mannitol and their effect on tubular water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":1,"stem":"Which property allows mannitol to act as an osmotic diuretic?","choices":{"A":"Free filtration with poor tubular reabsorption","B":"Active secretion into the proximal tubule","C":"Reabsorption with Na+ in the proximal tubule","D":"Metabolism to an osmotically active product","E":"Binding to the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mannitol diffuses poorly across cell membranes but is filtered freely across the glomerulus and is not reabsorbed, so it raises tubular fluid osmolality and keeps water in the tubule. The increased osmotic pressure prevents filtered water from being reabsorbed, and sodium and other solutes follow water into the urine. Binding Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransport is how loop diuretics act, not mannitol, which works by osmotic effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic diuretics need filtration to reach the lumen, and with a severely reduced GFR the agent cannot be excreted into the urine. COMLEX: the effect is strongest in the proximal tubule but occurs throughout the nephron.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — luminal osmotic agents such as mannitol and their effect on tubular water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":1,"stem":"At which nephron segment is the effect of osmotic diuretics most marked?","choices":{"A":"Thick ascending limb","B":"Proximal tubule","C":"Distal convoluted tubule","D":"Cortical collecting duct","E":"Connecting tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The effect of osmotic diuretics is most marked in the proximal tubule, although the same process occurs throughout all segments of the nephron. Loop diuretics act at the thick ascending limb, thiazides at the distal tubule, and K+-sparing diuretics in the collecting duct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mannitol acts at the proximal tubule and in the nephron generally, unlike transporter-specific diuretics. COMLEX: it is not a treatment for essential hypertension or heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — luminal osmotic agents such as mannitol and their effect on tubular water and sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2) takes oral acetazolamide at the standard dose. After two weeks he reports tingling in his fingers and ringing in his ears, and his serum bicarbonate is 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which mechanism best explains his increased drug exposure?","choices":{"A":"Increased glomerular filtration of the drug","B":"Increased hepatic first-pass extraction of the drug","C":"Increased distal tubular reabsorption of the drug","D":"Reduced proximal tubular secretion of the drug","E":"Reduced intestinal absorption of the drug"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acetazolamide is excreted by secretion in the proximal tubule S2 segment, so dosing must be reduced in renal insufficiency, and failing secretion raises drug exposure. Paresthesias and tinnitus are recognized adverse effects of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. Acetazolamide also causes NaHCO3 excretion and metabolic acidosis, and it may accumulate in renal failure, leading to nervous system toxicity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs eliminated by tubular secretion accumulate when nephron mass is lost. COMLEX: reduce the dose of acetazolamide in renal insufficiency, because it may accumulate in renal failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — saturable carrier-mediated secretory pathways and the PAH titration curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with chronic kidney disease has a GFR that has fallen from 120 to 30 mL/min. He receives a drug at an unchanged constant rate. The drug is freely filtered, is not metabolized, and is neither secreted nor reabsorbed by the tubules. Compared with baseline, what will its steady-state plasma concentration be?","choices":{"A":"Twofold higher","B":"Fourfold higher","C":"Eightfold higher","D":"Equal to baseline","E":"One-half of baseline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For a substance that is only filtered, the excretion rate equals GFR times plasma concentration, as for creatinine. At steady state the excretion rate must equal the input rate, so the plasma concentration rises until the reduced GFR again clears the same amount. A fall in GFR from 120 to 30 mL/min is a fourfold reduction, so the plasma concentration rises fourfold. Twofold or eightfold rises do not match a fourfold fall in GFR, and the level rises rather than falls when filtration drops.","boardPrep":"Step 1: creatinine excretion equals GFR times plasma creatinine, and its plasma level rises until excretion again matches production. COMLEX: drugs handled mainly by filtration need dose reduction as GFR falls.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — the clearance equation and how plasma concentration and urine flow determine excretion of a filtered solute","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Twofold or eightfold rises do "}},{"id":"sdl44_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure and advanced chronic kidney disease (eGFR 15 mL/min/1.73 m2) needs a diuretic whose elimination depends least on renal function. Which drug best fits?","choices":{"A":"Torsemide","B":"Furosemide","C":"Acetazolamide","D":"Mannitol","E":"Hydrochlorothiazide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Torsemide is mainly eliminated by the liver, whereas furosemide is mainly eliminated by the kidney through glomerular filtration and tubular secretion, and bumetanide is eliminated half by each. Acetazolamide is excreted by proximal tubular secretion, mannitol by glomerular filtration, and thiazides are secreted by the organic acid secretory system in the proximal tubule. Mannitol is also contraindicated in congestive heart failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: torsemide is the loop diuretic with mainly hepatic elimination. COMLEX: loop diuretic half-life depends on renal function, and loop diuretics should not be used in anuric patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Acetazolamide is excreted by p","E":"Acetazolamide is excreted by p"}},{"id":"sdl44_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure takes furosemide and now has a serum K+ of 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) with metabolic alkalosis. Which mechanism best explains his potassium loss?","choices":{"A":"Direct blockade of K+ reabsorption in the proximal tubule","B":"Suppressed aldosterone release during volume contraction","C":"Higher distal flow and aldosterone increasing K+ secretion","D":"Inhibited H+/K+ ATPase in intercalated cells","E":"Insulin release that shifts K+ from cells into plasma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loop diuretics reach the collecting duct with more sodium and water, and volume depletion stimulates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Both events increase K+ and H+ secretion in the collecting duct, causing hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. The loop itself has little net K+ transport because K+ reabsorbed through NKCC2 is recycled by an apical K+ channel. Loop diuretics do not inhibit the intercalated cell H+/K+ ATPase, and insulin shifts K+ into cells, not out into plasma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: agents upstream of the collecting duct increase distal delivery, so they waste K+. COMLEX: hypokalemia greatly increases digoxin toxicity in heart failure patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — principal cell K+ secretion and the factors that regulate it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with hypertension takes hydrochlorothiazide, and his serum K+ is 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). His physician adds triamterene to the regimen. At which site does triamterene act to reduce his urinary K+ loss?","choices":{"A":"Cortical collecting duct","B":"Thick ascending limb","C":"Early distal tubule","D":"Proximal convoluted tubule","E":"Thin descending limb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"K+-sparing diuretics act in the principal cells of the collecting duct, the final portion of the nephron. Triamterene blocks ENaC, which makes the lumen more positive and lowers K+ secretion. Hydrochlorothiazide acts earlier, in the distal convoluted tubule, and is combined with a K+-sparing agent so that the potassium loss is offset. The thick ascending limb is the loop diuretic site and the thin descending limb lacks the principal cell ENaC that triamterene blocks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides waste K+, while ENaC blockers and aldosterone antagonists conserve it at the collecting duct. COMLEX: fixed combinations of a thiazide with triamterene are used to limit hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — electrogenic K+ secretion through apical ROMK and BK channels and the systemic factors that regulate it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old volunteer receives an investigational diuretic. Urinary Na+ excretion rises, urinary K+ excretion falls, and the drug shows no binding to steroid hormone receptors. Which mechanism most likely accounts for these findings?","choices":{"A":"Blockade of the NKCC2 cotransporter in thick limb cells","B":"Blockade of the Na+/Cl- symporter in distal tubule cells","C":"Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in proximal tubule cells","D":"Retention of an unreabsorbed osmotic solute in the lumen","E":"Blockade of luminal Na+ channels in principal cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A diuretic that raises Na+ excretion while lowering K+ excretion is K+-sparing. Because it does not act on hormone receptors, it must directly block ENaC in collecting duct principal cells, which prevents Na+ reabsorption and makes the lumen more positive, lowering K+ secretion. Loop and thiazide diuretics and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors raise urinary K+ excretion, and osmotic diuretics increase all urinary electrolytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: amiloride and triamterene block ENaC, and spironolactone and eplerenone block the aldosterone receptor. COMLEX: both K+-sparing subclasses can cause hyperkalemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — RAAS and aldosterone effects on renal sodium retention","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loop and thiazide diuretics an","B":"Loop and thiazide diuretics an","D":"Loop and thiazide diuretics an"}},{"id":"sdl44_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with idiopathic intracranial hypertension started oral acetazolamide two days ago. Blood pH is 7.31 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), serum HCO3- is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), Na+ is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), Cl- is 113 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and urine pH is 7.2. Which mechanism best explains the alkaline urine despite her acidemia?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone excess driving distal H+ secretion","B":"Filtered bicarbonate escaping proximal reabsorption","C":"Ketoacid excretion that buffers urinary protons","D":"Urease-producing bacteria generating ammonia","E":"Diarrheal bicarbonate loss with renal compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibition prevents conversion of luminal H2CO3 to CO2 and water, so filtered HCO3- stays in the tubule and is excreted with Na+. This makes the urine more alkaline than normal while the plasma HCO3- falls, producing a type 2 (proximal) RTA-like acidosis. Her anion gap is 139 - 113 - 15 = 11 mEq/L, which is normal and consistent with bicarbonate loss. Aldosterone stimulates distal H+ secretion, which would acidify the urine rather than alkalinize it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide causes bicarbonaturia, alkaline urine, and a normal anion gap acidosis. COMLEX: use urine pH and serum K+ together to classify a renal tubular acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — urine pH and serum K+ in the differential of renal tubular acidoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man taking acetazolamide for altitude prophylaxis has Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 3.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 111 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and he has no diarrhea. Which best describes his acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap acidosis from ketoacid accumulation","B":"High anion gap acidosis from lactate accumulation","C":"Normal anion gap acidosis from renal HCO3- loss","D":"Normal anion gap acidosis from diarrheal HCO3- loss","E":"High anion gap acidosis from toxic alcohol ingestion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"His anion gap is 138 - 111 - 15 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, so this is a hyperchloremic normal anion gap acidosis. Acetazolamide blocks proximal HCO3- reabsorption, so bicarbonate is lost in the urine along with K+, a drug-induced proximal type 2 RTA. High anion gap acidoses from ketoacids, lactate, or toxic alcohols would show a gap above normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RTA and diarrhea cause normal anion gap acidosis, whereas the MUDPILES causes raise the gap. COMLEX: acetazolamide is a reversible cause of hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — normal versus high anion gap metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman takes acetazolamide and has a serum K+ of 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) with a serum HCO3- of 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). She has no vomiting or diarrhea. Which mechanism best explains her hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced K+ reabsorption by the thick ascending limb","B":"Urinary K+ loss with the unreabsorbed bicarbonate","C":"Reduced aldosterone release lowering K+ secretion","D":"Blocked apical K+ channels in principal cells","E":"Increased proximal K+ secretion through the Na+/H+ antiporter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause a proximal type 2 RTA with hypokalemia, and potassium is excreted along with the bicarbonate that cannot be reabsorbed. Volume contraction raises rather than lowers aldosterone, and enhanced K+ reabsorption would raise rather than lower the serum K+. Blocking apical K+ channels in principal cells would reduce K+ secretion and raise serum K+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hypokalemia with proximal RTA among the adverse effects of acetazolamide. COMLEX: check serum K+ and bicarbonate in patients on long-term acetazolamide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — how tubular flow, luminal anions, and aldosterone regulate principal cell K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with obesity has daily headaches and bilateral papilledema. MRI is normal, and lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 36 cm H2O with normal CSF composition. Acetazolamide is started. Which effect underlies the expected benefit?","choices":{"A":"Greater CSF reabsorption by arachnoid granulations","B":"Osmotic movement of water out of brain tissue","C":"Cerebral vasoconstriction to lower blood volume","D":"Diuresis that reduces total body water","E":"Less bicarbonate production and reduced CSF secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is elevated intracranial pressure of unknown cause. The choroid plexus generates CSF, and acetazolamide inhibits intracellular carbonic anhydrase, reducing bicarbonate production, CSF production, and intracranial pressure. The diuretic effect of acetazolamide is weak and is not the reason for its benefit. Acetazolamide does not increase CSF reabsorption, draw water osmotically out of the brain as mannitol does, or constrict cerebral vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide is a treatment for idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri). COMLEX: the goal is to prevent permanent brain damage from persistently high intracranial pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — intracellular carbonic anhydrase and cellular bicarbonate transport mechanics","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Acetazolamide does not increas","C":"Acetazolamide does not increas","D":"The diuretic effect of acetazo"}},{"id":"sdl44_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma has an intraocular pressure of 32 mm Hg and starts oral acetazolamide. Transport of which pair of ions by the ciliary epithelium drives the aqueous humor formation that the drug reduces?","choices":{"A":"Sodium and bicarbonate","B":"Potassium and bicarbonate","C":"Sodium and phosphate","D":"Potassium and chloride","E":"Calcium and phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ciliary body epithelium uses CO2 and intracellular carbonic anhydrase to form HCO3-, and active transport of the newly formed HCO3- together with Na+ into the posterior chamber drives the osmotic water movement that forms aqueous humor. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease HCO3- production, which reduces aqueous humor formation by the ciliary body and lowers intraocular pressure. Potassium, chloride, phosphate, and calcium transport are not the carbonic anhydrase dependent step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower intraocular pressure by decreasing aqueous humor production. COMLEX: topical dorzolamide and brinzolamide avoid renal and systemic effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — intracellular carbonic anhydrase and cellular bicarbonate transport mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl44_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with severe COPD and chronic CO2 retention is in the intensive care unit after pneumonia. His serum HCO3- is 38 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and attempts to wean him from the ventilator have failed because of a weak respiratory drive. His team adds acetazolamide. Which effect underlies the expected benefit?","choices":{"A":"Bronchodilation from reduced airway carbonic anhydrase","B":"Lower serum HCO3- that strengthens respiratory drive","C":"Diuresis that clears pulmonary edema fluid","D":"Reduced cerebrospinal fluid production in the brain","E":"Reduced CO2 production by peripheral tissues"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor brick notes that these drugs may be used in some intensive care patients with COPD to help transition them off ventilators: blocking proximal tubular carbonic anhydrase causes bicarbonaturia, which lowers the compensatory high serum bicarbonate and increases the patient's respiratory drive. These drugs are not bronchodilators, their diuretic effect is too weak for use in fluid overload, reduced CSF production is the basis for their use in idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and they do not lower tissue CO2 production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the same bicarbonaturia and acidosis underlie their use against altitude sickness and their off-label use in central sleep apnea. COMLEX: acetazolamide produces a drug-induced proximal (type 2) RTA with hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — proximal carbonic anhydrase inhibition and metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"These drugs are not bronchodil","D":"These drugs are not bronchodil"}},{"id":"sdl44_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with traumatic brain injury and oliguric acute kidney injury (urine output 100 mL/day, creatinine 5.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL)) receives intravenous mannitol for elevated intracranial pressure. Six hours later she has dyspnea, bibasilar crackles, and peripheral edema. Which mechanism best explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"Mannitol-induced aldosterone release causing sodium retention","B":"Mannitol-induced ADH release causing free water retention","C":"Direct depression of myocardial contractility by mannitol","D":"Mannitol binding of plasma proteins lowering oncotic pressure","E":"Unexcreted mannitol draws cell water into the plasma volume"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mannitol depends on glomerular filtration to be excreted. With a severely reduced GFR it cannot be excreted into the urine, so it stays in the blood, and water is drawn from the intracellular regions into the extracellular fluid. The resulting volume overload and plasma volume expansion produce pulmonary or peripheral edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists pulmonary edema among the adverse effects of mannitol, and anuria is a contraindication. COMLEX: severe renal impairment with low urine output is a contraindication to osmotic diuretics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — clinical volume expansion states and fluid shifts between compartments","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl44_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man with severe traumatic brain injury has received repeated intravenous mannitol doses for elevated intracranial pressure. He now has a heart rate of 122/min, blood pressure of 84/48 mm Hg, dry mucous membranes, and a urine output above 250 mL/h. Which complication best explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Volume overload from retained unexcreted mannitol","B":"Pulmonary edema from acute cardiac decompensation","C":"Volume depletion from excessive osmotic diuresis","D":"Hyperkalemia from excessive distal potassium retention","E":"Metabolic acidosis from proximal bicarbonate wasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mannitol has powerful diuretic qualities, so patients are at risk for severe volume depletion, with tachycardia, hypotension, and signs of poor perfusion. Serum electrolytes may change, leading to hypokalemia or hyponatremia. Volume overload occurs in patients with poor kidney function who cannot excrete the drug, which does not fit his high urine output. Metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate wasting is an effect of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, not of mannitol. Pulmonary edema is a mannitol risk when fluid is retained, but his hypotension, dry mucosa and brisk urine output show depletion, and the expected K+ change is hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists dehydration and hypo- or hypernatremia among the adverse effects of mannitol. COMLEX: fluid status and electrolytes must be monitored closely during osmotic diuretic therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — clinical volume contraction states","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Volume overload occurs in pati","D":"Pulmonary edema is a mannitol "}},{"id":"sdl44_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 25 percent has a large intracranial mass with elevated intracranial pressure, and the team plans mannitol. Which complication is of greatest concern?","choices":{"A":"Ototoxicity from rapid intravenous bolus dosing","B":"Hyperkalemic type 4 renal tubular acidosis","C":"Hyperuricemia with recurrent acute gout flares","D":"Pulmonary edema from intravascular volume expansion","E":"Calcium phosphate stones from alkaline urine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mannitol raises serum osmolality and expands the plasma volume, and in congestive heart failure the increased plasma volume can exacerbate pulmonary edema, so heart failure is a contraindication. Ototoxicity is a loop diuretic effect, type 4 RTA follows K+-sparing diuretics, hyperuricemia occurs with thiazide and loop diuretics, and alkaline-urine stones follow carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists heart failure and anuria as contraindications to mannitol. COMLEX: congestive heart failure, severe renal impairment with low urine output, and severe dehydration are the main contraindications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — integrated hemodynamic control of body sodium and volume","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ototoxicity is a loop diuretic","C":"Ototoxicity is a loop diuretic"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl44_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Clearance Principles & Pharmacokinetics in Renal Impairment","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with chronic kidney disease and an estimated GFR of 25 mL/min/1.73 m2 (reference range: above 90 mL/min/1.73 m2) needs an aminoglycoside for sepsis. The drug is cleared mainly by glomerular filtration. Which dosing change best prevents toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Larger loading dose than usual","B":"Shorter interval between doses","C":"Longer interval between doses","D":"Substitution of a secreted drug","E":"Unchanged dose and schedule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aminoglycosides are cleared mainly by glomerular filtration, so their clearance falls with GFR, prolonging half-life and causing accumulation. Maintenance doses are reduced or the dosing interval is lengthened in proportion to the fall in GFR. A larger loading dose than usual increases peak exposure without fixing accumulation. A shorter interval increases accumulation. Substitution of a secreted drug does not help, because tubular secretion also falls in renal impairment. An unchanged dose and schedule leads to toxicity. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 44, pharmacokinetics in renal impairment); Katzung Ch. 3 (dosing in renal disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when GFR falls, filtered drugs accumulate, so reduce the dose or lengthen the interval in proportion to creatinine clearance. COMLEX: aminoglycoside accumulation risks nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 3 — clearance links GFR to drug elimination","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl44_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Diuretic Foundations & Potassium-Wasting Mechanics","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman starts furosemide for heart failure edema. After 2 weeks her serum K+ is 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Through which apical channels is most of the extra K+ secreted into her urine?","choices":{"A":"NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb","B":"ENaC in principal cells","C":"ROMK in principal cells","D":"H+/K+-ATPase in intercalated cells","E":"NCC in the distal convoluted tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Upstream diuretics deliver more Na+ and fluid to the collecting duct. Principal cells take up the Na+ through ENaC, which makes the lumen more negative, and K+ is then secreted through apical ROMK (and BK) channels, while high flow keeps washing the K+ away. NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb is the drug's target and reabsorbs K+ rather than secreting it. ENaC carries Na+ into the cell; it creates the driving force but does not secrete K+. H+/K+-ATPase in intercalated cells reabsorbs K+. NCC in the distal convoluted tubule carries Na+ and Cl-, not K+. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 44, potassium-wasting mechanics); Katzung Ch. 15 (diuretic-induced hypokalemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop and thiazide diuretics waste K+ because more distal Na+ and flow drive ROMK secretion, worsened by secondary aldosteronism. COMLEX: monitor potassium and consider a potassium-sparing agent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 4 — hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis complicates loop and thiazide diuretics","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"ENaC carries Na+ into the cell"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl44_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma starts oral acetazolamide and has a brisk diuresis that wanes by day 4. His serum HCO3- is now 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which mechanism best explains the waning diuresis?","choices":{"A":"Induction of carbonic anhydrase","B":"Loss of drug secretion by OATs","C":"Tolerance at the ciliary body","D":"Faster hepatic drug clearance","E":"Smaller filtered HCO3- load"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acetazolamide causes bicarbonaturia and a hyperchloremic acidosis. As plasma HCO3- falls, the filtered HCO3- load falls until it matches the reduced proximal reabsorptive capacity, so the diuresis is self-limited after several days. Induction of carbonic anhydrase is not the described mechanism. Loss of drug secretion by OATs is wrong, because proximal secretion keeps delivering acetazolamide to its luminal target. Increased NaCl reabsorption by downstream segments also contributes. Tolerance at the ciliary body concerns the eye, not the diuresis. Faster hepatic clearance is wrong, because acetazolamide is renally secreted. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 44, drug-induced type 2 RTA and self-limited diuresis); Katzung Ch. 15 (carbonic anhydrase inhibitors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CA inhibitors cause a self-limited bicarbonate diuresis, alkaline urine, and normal anion gap acidosis. COMLEX: expect hypokalemia from increased distal Na+ delivery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 3 — type 2 RTA and its causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl44_q4","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Proximal Tubule CA Inhibition & Acidosis","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure has received high-dose furosemide for a week. His edema persists, and his serum HCO3- is 38 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) with an arterial pH of 7.52 (reference range: 7.35–7.45). Which added drug best lowers his serum HCO3- while continuing diuresis?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide","B":"Acetazolamide","C":"Hydrochlorothiazide","D":"Mannitol","E":"Triamterene"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acetazolamide blocks proximal carbonic anhydrase, so filtered HCO3- escapes reabsorption and is excreted, correcting a metabolic alkalosis while producing a natriuresis. The lecture lists metabolic alkalosis as an indication. More furosemide would worsen the contraction alkalosis. Hydrochlorothiazide also causes hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Mannitol causes osmotic diuresis without correcting alkalosis and expands plasma volume, which is risky in heart failure. Triamterene reduces further K+ and H+ loss but does not correct an established alkalosis or add useful natriuresis. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 44 and SDL 48, CAI indications including metabolic alkalosis); Katzung Ch. 15 (acetazolamide for metabolic alkalosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide treats metabolic alkalosis by causing bicarbonaturia. COMLEX: loop diuretic contraction alkalosis in heart failure is a classic setting for adding acetazolamide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 4 — loop diuretics cause hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"More furosemide would worsen t"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl44_q5","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Extrarenal CAI Therapeutic Indications","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman takes acetazolamide for 2 days before climbing to 4,200 meters. Before ascent she has her blood and urine tested. Which laboratory pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low urine pH, low serum HCO3-","B":"Low urine pH, high serum HCO3-","C":"High urine pH, high serum HCO3-","D":"High urine pH, low serum HCO3-","E":"Normal urine pH, normal serum HCO3-"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acetazolamide blocks proximal HCO3- reclamation, so HCO3- spills into the urine and raises urine pH while serum HCO3- falls, producing a mild metabolic acidosis. That acidosis drives hyperventilation at altitude and prevents acute mountain sickness. Low urine pH with low serum HCO3- fits an acidosis with intact distal acidification, not bicarbonaturia. High urine pH with high serum HCO3- fits metabolic alkalosis. Low urine pH with high serum HCO3- fits the paradoxical aciduria of volume contraction. Normal values ignore the drug's effect. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 44, extrarenal CAI indications and altitude sickness); Katzung Ch. 15 (acetazolamide).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide causes bicarbonaturia (alkaline urine) and a metabolic acidosis that stimulates ventilation at altitude. COMLEX: start prophylaxis before ascent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 3 — CA inhibition blocks proximal HCO3- reclamation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Low urine pH with low serum HC","B":"Low urine pH with high serum H","C":"High urine pH with high serum "}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl44_q6","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Osmotic Diuresis & Plasma Expansion Hazards","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has sudden eye pain and blurred vision, and her intraocular pressure is 52 mm Hg (reference range: 10–21 mm Hg). Intravenous mannitol is given while she awaits laser treatment. Which mechanism best explains how mannitol lowers her eye pressure?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of ciliary carbonic anhydrase","B":"Pupillary constriction opening the angle","C":"Increased trabecular outflow of humor","D":"Osmotic water shift from the vitreous","E":"Reduced choroidal blood flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mannitol stays in the extracellular fluid and raises plasma osmolality, drawing water out of tissues, including the vitreous, into the vascular bed, which lowers intraocular pressure in acute angle-closure glaucoma. Inhibition of ciliary carbonic anhydrase is how acetazolamide and dorzolamide lower pressure. Pupillary constriction is the action of cholinergic miotics such as pilocarpine. Increased trabecular outflow is not mannitol's effect. Reduced choroidal blood flow is not the mechanism. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 44, mannitol in reducing intraocular pressure); Katzung Ch. 15 (osmotic diuretics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mannitol lowers intracranial and intraocular pressure by osmotically pulling water from cells. COMLEX: avoid mannitol in anuria or heart failure because of plasma volume expansion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 4 — solutes confined to the ECF pull water out of cells","isHighYield":false}],"45":[{"id":"sdl45_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure has worsening pulmonary edema and needs a loop diuretic. He had a severe allergic reaction to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and is considered allergic to sulfonamide-containing drugs. Which diuretic is the most appropriate choice?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide","B":"Ethacrynic acid","C":"Acetazolamide","D":"Chlorthalidone","E":"Hydrochlorothiazide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The brick notes that furosemide, bumetanide, and torsemide are chemically similar because of their sulfonamide group, and it names ethacrynic acid as the alternative loop diuretic for patients with sulfa drug sensitivity. Katzung likewise describes ethacrynic acid as not a sulfonamide derivative, with allergic reactions much less common than with the other loop agents. The precise nature of sulfa allergies is described as controversial and murky. Acetazolamide, chlorthalidone and hydrochlorothiazide are not loop diuretics, and acetazolamide and the thiazides are themselves sulfa drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ethacrynic acid is a nonsulfonamide loop diuretic, and First Aid describes it as more ototoxic than furosemide. COMLEX: use ethacrynic acid only in patients who cannot tolerate other loop diuretics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at the NKCC2 cotransporter and its downstream effects on Na+ and divalent cation excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":1,"stem":"Which transport event generates the lumen-positive potential that drives Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"Na+ entry through apical ENaC channels","B":"Cl- entry through apical Cl-/HCO3- exchangers","C":"K+ back-diffusion through apical channels","D":"H+ secretion through apical H+ pumps","E":"Ca2+ entry through apical calcium channels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter moves K+ into the cell, and K+ then leaves through an apical K+ channel into the lumen. This movement of K+ gives the lumen a positive charge that drives paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption. Loop diuretics reduce this K+ gradient, so the lumen is less positive and Ca2+ and Mg2+ are lost in the urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that loops lose Ca2+, and loop diuretics cause hypomagnesemia. COMLEX: hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia from loop diuretics increase the risk of cardiac arrhythmia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at the NKCC2 cotransporter and its downstream effects on Na+ and divalent cation excretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":1,"stem":"By which mechanism do NSAIDs reduce the efficacy of loop diuretics?","choices":{"A":"Blocked prostaglandin synthesis needed for vasodilation","B":"Induced NKCC2 expression that overrides the drug block","C":"Increased tubular reabsorption of the drug back into blood","D":"Direct binding of the drug within the tubular fluid","E":"Stimulated aldosterone release that retains sodium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loop diuretics stimulate renal prostaglandin synthesis, and these prostaglandins dilate the afferent arterioles, increasing renal blood flow and helping sustain GFR and natriuresis. NSAIDs such as aspirin and ibuprofen inhibit the prostaglandins that mediate this vasodilatory effect, so they decrease the efficacy of loop diuretics and should not be used together. NSAIDs can also compete for weak acid secretion in the proximal tubule. NSAIDs do not induce NKCC2 or bind the diuretic in the tubular fluid; they interfere through prostaglandins and competition for proximal secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links the loop diuretic effect on the afferent arteriole to PGE and notes that NSAIDs inhibit it. COMLEX: avoid combining NSAIDs with loop diuretics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology at the NKCC2 cotransporter and its downstream effects on Na+ and divalent cation excretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"NSAIDs do not induce NKCC2 or "}},{"id":"sdl45_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter do thiazide diuretics inhibit?","choices":{"A":"Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter","B":"Epithelial Na+ channel","C":"Na+/H+ antiporter","D":"Na+/Cl- symporter","E":"Na+/K+ ATPase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thiazides block the Na+/Cl- symporter (NCC) in the luminal membrane of the distal convoluted tubule. The Na+/K+-ATPase keeps intracellular sodium low, and this drives sodium entry through the symporter. Thiazides are only moderately potent because approximately 90 percent of filtered sodium has already been reabsorbed before the fluid reaches the DCT. Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransport is the loop diuretic target, ENaC is blocked by amiloride and triamterene, and the Na+/H+ antiporter is the proximal exchanger tied to carbonic anhydrase-dependent bicarbonate reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes thiazides as inhibiting NaCl reabsorption in the early DCT. COMLEX: Gitelman syndrome, an inherited defect of the Na+/Cl- symporter, resembles thiazide use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — early distal convoluted tubule transport mechanics and the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransport is the"}},{"id":"sdl45_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":1,"stem":"Why are thiazide diuretics useful for preventing calcium oxalate kidney stones?","choices":{"A":"Competitive binding of calcium within the tubular fluid","B":"Increased reabsorption of calcium from the tubular fluid","C":"Decreased plasma calcium concentration from diuresis","D":"Decreased glomerular filtration of calcium","E":"Impaired transport of oxalate into the tubular fluid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiazides increase calcium reabsorption in the renal tubule, which lowers urinary calcium and reduces the recurrence of calcium-containing stones. The effect arises partly from mild volume depletion, which enhances proximal sodium and passive calcium reabsorption, and partly from enhanced Na+/Ca2+ exchange in the DCT. Thiazides tend to raise rather than lower plasma calcium. Thiazides act on calcium handling, not on oxalate transport.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists idiopathic hypercalciuria among thiazide uses and hypercalcemia among their adverse effects. COMLEX: thiazides may modestly reduce osteoporotic fracture risk but are not primary bone drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — early distal convoluted tubule transport mechanics and the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":1,"stem":"According to Katzung, thiazide diuretics are thought to be ineffective when GFR falls below which value?","choices":{"A":"Below 30 mL/min","B":"Below 10 mL/min","C":"Below 15 mL/min","D":"Below 20 mL/min","E":"Below 45 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung states that thiazide diuretics are thought to be ineffective when GFR falls below 30 mL/min, although the exact GFR at which they lose benefit is still debated and may be lower than previously imagined. Thiazides can still lower the loop diuretic dose needed in patients with a GFR of 5 to 15 mL/min, for example metolazone combined with furosemide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that thiazides potentiate loop diuretics in refractory volume overload. COMLEX: as GFR declines, loop diuretics rather than thiazides are used for edema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — early distal convoluted tubule transport mechanics and the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Which agent directly blocks epithelial Na+ channels in collecting duct principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Spironolactone","B":"Amiloride","C":"Eplerenone","D":"Acetazolamide","E":"Chlorthalidone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The two K+-sparing subclasses are ENaC blockers, amiloride and triamterene, and aldosterone receptor antagonists, spironolactone and eplerenone. ENaC blockers directly block sodium reabsorption through the luminal channel, without affecting the aldosterone receptor or the ATPase. Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase, and chlorthalidone is a thiazide-type blocker of the Na+/Cl- symporter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists amiloride and triamterene as Na+ channel blockers in the cortical collecting tubule, with amiloride used in nephrogenic DI. COMLEX: hyperkalemia is the main adverse effect of these drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — pharmacology of the potassium-sparing diuretic subclasses in the late distal nephron","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"How do ENaC blockers reduce K+ secretion in principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Direct blockade of apical K+ channels in principal cells","B":"Blockade of the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase pump","C":"Competitive inhibition of the mineralocorticoid receptor","D":"Inhibition of H+ ATPase in intercalated cells","E":"Less Na+ entry weakens the lumen-negative potential"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ENaC blockers stop sodium reabsorption through the luminal channel. This makes the cell more negatively charged and the lumen more positively charged, which lowers K+ loss through the potassium channels. They act without affecting the aldosterone receptor or the Na+/K+-ATPase, which are the targets of the aldosterone antagonists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced Na+ entry lowers the lumen-negative potential, so K+ is spared. COMLEX: amiloride or triamterene is often combined with a thiazide to cancel its K+-wasting effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — pharmacology of the potassium-sparing diuretic subclasses in the late distal nephron","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"They act without affecting the","C":"They act without affecting the"}},{"id":"sdl45_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Why does eplerenone cause less gynecomastia than spironolactone?","choices":{"A":"Higher affinity for the androgen receptor","B":"Additional blockade of epithelial Na+ channels","C":"Inhibition of adrenal aldosterone synthesis","D":"Greater selectivity for the mineralocorticoid receptor","E":"Greater potency at the thiazide-sensitive symporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spironolactone has antiandrogen and estrogen-like effects because of its structural similarity to sex steroid hormones, and in men it can decrease libido, cause sexual dysfunction, and cause gynecomastia. Eplerenone is much more selective than spironolactone for the mineralocorticoid receptor and is virtually inactive on androgen or progesterone receptors, so these effects are less severe. Eplerenone does not block ENaC, inhibit aldosterone synthesis or act on the thiazide-sensitive symporter; it is a competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists gynecomastia and antiandrogen effects among the adverse effects of spironolactone. COMLEX: switching from spironolactone to eplerenone is the option for a man with gynecomastia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — pharmacology of the potassium-sparing diuretic subclasses in the late distal nephron","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Eplerenone is much more select","B":"Eplerenone does not block ENaC"}},{"id":"sdl45_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of metabolic adverse effects is characteristic of thiazide diuretics?","choices":{"A":"Hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, and ototoxicity","B":"Hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and gynecomastia","C":"Hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, and hyperuricemia","D":"Metabolic acidosis, paresthesias, and tinnitus","E":"Volume depletion, pulmonary edema, and kidney injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazides are hyperGLUC: hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia. Hyperglycemia reflects reduced insulin secretion and altered glucose metabolism, and hyperuricemia reflects volume-related proximal uric acid reabsorption and competition for secretion. Loop diuretics cause hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, and ototoxicity, K+-sparing agents cause hyperkalemia and acidosis with gynecomastia from spironolactone, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause acidosis, paresthesias, and tinnitus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia as thiazide adverse effects. COMLEX: thiazides should be avoided in patients with gout or metabolic syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — the nephron site of diuretic action determines the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance each class produces","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Which electrolyte abnormality do thiazides cause by limiting maximal urinary dilution?","choices":{"A":"Hyponatremia","B":"Hypocalcemia","C":"Hyperkalemia","D":"Hypermagnesemia","E":"Hypophosphatemia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazides block sodium reabsorption in the DCT diluting segment, so the kidney cannot make maximally dilute urine. A patient who drinks a lot of water and eats little food then retains water and becomes hyponatremic, often without looking volume depleted. Thiazides tend to raise calcium and lower potassium, so hypocalcemia and hyperkalemia run opposite to their effects, and magnesium or phosphate changes do not come from the dilution defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides can cause hyponatremia yet treat the hypernatremia of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. COMLEX: poor food intake with high water intake increases the risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — the nephron site of diuretic action determines the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance each class produces","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Thiazides tend to raise calciu","E":"Thiazides tend to raise calciu"}},{"id":"sdl45_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Chronic hyperkalemia from potassium-sparing diuretics suppresses proximal tubule synthesis of ammonia. Which acid-base disorder results?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 RTA","B":"Type 2 RTA","C":"Lactic acidosis","D":"Ketoacidosis","E":"Type 4 RTA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic hyperkalemia from potassium-sparing diuretics may suppress proximal tubule synthesis of ammonia, the buffer needed to excrete acid. This produces a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with a high serum potassium, hyperkalemic type 4 RTA, most often seen with aldosterone antagonists. Ketoacidosis is an anion gap acidosis from accumulated organic acids, not a defect in ammonia synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis as adverse effects of K+-sparing diuretics. COMLEX: potassium levels should be checked regularly in patients taking these drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — the nephron site of diuretic action determines the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance each class produces","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with gout takes probenecid. Furosemide 40 mg daily, which previously controlled his edema, now produces little diuresis. Which mechanism best explains the reduced response?","choices":{"A":"Competition for organic acid secretion lowers luminal drug delivery","B":"Inhibited NKCC2 expression from uric acid retention","C":"Enhanced proximal reabsorption of filtered furosemide","D":"Faster hepatic conversion of furosemide to inactive metabolites","E":"Blocked afferent arteriolar dilation by tubuloglomerular feedback"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loop diuretics act on the luminal side of the tubule, so their diuretic activity correlates with their secretion by the proximal tubule. Probenecid, like NSAIDs, competes for weak acid secretion in the proximal tubule, which reduces the amount of furosemide reaching NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb. Tubuloglomerular feedback does not explain a probenecid interaction; the problem is less drug secreted into the lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics reach the lumen by proximal tubular organic acid secretion, and competing acids blunt their effect. COMLEX: reassess the diuretic dose when a patient starts probenecid or an NSAID.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — bidirectional proximal tubule handling of organic acids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman with heart failure and stage 5 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 12 mL/min/1.73 m2) still makes urine but has no diuretic response to oral furosemide 40 mg. Which adjustment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Convert furosemide to hydrochlorothiazide monotherapy","B":"Add spironolactone to potentiate diuresis","C":"Give intravenous mannitol to force diuresis","D":"Substantially raise the furosemide dose","E":"Switch to acetazolamide for bicarbonate diuresis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With severe renal insufficiency (GFR 5 to 15 mL/min), diuretics can still work, and high-dose loop diuretics, up to 500 mg/day of furosemide, may be needed. Loop diuretic activity depends on delivery to the lumen by proximal tubular secretion. Thiazides are thought to be ineffective below a GFR of 30 mL/min, potassium-sparing agents cause hyperkalemia, mannitol cannot be excreted when GFR is low, and acetazolamide can exacerbate acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics remain the agents for volume overload when GFR is low. COMLEX: loop diuretics should not be used in anuric patients, because they will not work and ototoxicity risk may increase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 1 — falling GFR limits drug clearance and tubular drug delivery","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Thiazides are thought to be in","B":"Thiazides are thought to be in","C":"Thiazides are thought to be in"}},{"id":"sdl45_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer has a serum calcium of 13.8 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL) and is volume depleted. After saline repletion she receives intravenous furosemide, and her urinary calcium excretion rises. Which mechanism best explains the calciuresis?","choices":{"A":"Blocked PTH-sensitive Ca2+ channels in the distal tubule","B":"Lost lumen-positive potential driving paracellular Ca2+ uptake","C":"Suppressed calcitriol synthesis in the proximal tubule","D":"Stimulated calcitonin release that raises urinary calcium excretion","E":"Inhibited basolateral Na+/Ca2+ exchange in proximal cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2, so K+ movement into the lumen through the potassium channel falls and the lumen becomes less positive. There is then less drive for paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption, and both are lost in the urine. This is why loop diuretics are used together with normal saline to treat acute hypercalcemia in patients with malignancy. The calciuria does not depend on calcitriol suppression or calcitonin; it follows the loss of the paracellular driving force.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loops lose Ca2+, whereas thiazides increase Ca2+ reabsorption. COMLEX: loop diuretics with normal saline treat acute hypercalcemia, and hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia from loop use are usually mild.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — renal calcium handling along the nephron","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The calciuria does not depend "}},{"id":"sdl45_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2) has peripheral edema and a blood pressure of 150/90 mm Hg. Which single diuretic is most likely to be effective at this level of kidney function?","choices":{"A":"Hydrochlorothiazide","B":"Acetazolamide","C":"Furosemide","D":"Amiloride","E":"Mannitol"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Katzung states that thiazides are thought to be ineffective when GFR falls below 30 mL/min, although the exact threshold remains debated, so by that threshold hydrochlorothiazide is unlikely to work at an eGFR of 22. Acetazolamide must usually be avoided in kidney disease because it causes NaHCO3 excretion and can exacerbate acidosis, potassium-sparing diuretics may cause hyperkalemia, and mannitol cannot be excreted when GFR is reduced. A loop diuretic is the appropriate agent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide efficacy falls as GFR declines, whereas loop diuretics keep working at low GFR. COMLEX: excessive diuresis can impair renal function, and the consequences are more serious in patients with underlying renal disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — loss of functioning nephron mass in chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Acetazolamide must usually be "}},{"id":"sdl45_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus has polyuria of 6 L/day and a serum Na+ of 148 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L). Her physician starts hydrochlorothiazide, and her daily urine output falls. Which mechanism, as now understood for lithium-induced disease, best explains the benefit?","choices":{"A":"Restored ADH receptor signaling in collecting duct cells","B":"Blocked ENaC entry of Li+ into collecting duct principal cells","C":"Higher papillary osmolality, partial aquaporin-2 recovery","D":"Direct insertion of aquaporin-2 channels into the DCT","E":"Blocked ADH release from the posterior pituitary"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In lithium-induced nephrogenic DI, Katzung Ch 15 (course SDL 44–46 supplement, p30) states that hydrochlorothiazide \"causes increased osmolality in the inner medulla (papilla)\" and partly corrects the lithium-induced fall in aquaporin-2 expression; it also increases Na+ transporter expression in the DCT and CCT, so the maximum volume of dilute urine falls. Katzung notes that the benefit \"was previously thought\" to come from volume contraction with a fall in GFR, increased proximal NaCl and water reabsorption and less delivery to the diluting segments — the explanation still given in the course thiazide brick (SDL 45 PDF p9), which reports urine volume reduced by up to 50 percent. So the proximal-reabsorption account is the older view; for lithium-induced disease Katzung now gives the papillary mechanism. Blocking Li+ entry into collecting duct cells through the epithelial Na+ channel is how amiloride (or triamterene) partly reverses lithium-induced polyuria (Katzung Ch 15); hydrochlorothiazide acts on the Na+–Cl− cotransporter of the DCT, not on that channel. Thiazides also do not restore ADH receptor signaling, insert aquaporin-2 into the DCT, or block ADH release. Diuretics can reduce lithium clearance, so serum Li+ must be monitored (Katzung Ch 15).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thiazides can cause hyponatremia yet treat hypernatremia in nephrogenic DI, and First Aid lists nephrogenic DI as an indication; in lithium-induced NDI, HCTZ raises papillary osmolality and partly restores aquaporin-2 (Katzung Ch 15), whereas the older explanation was volume contraction with increased proximal reabsorption (course thiazide brick). COMLEX: nephrogenic DI is usually due to chronic interstitial renal disease or drugs such as lithium; monitor Li+ levels when a thiazide is added.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — central versus nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and the response to exogenous ADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with heart failure has worsening dyspnea and bibasilar crackles. Hydrochlorothiazide is started, but his urine output rises only modestly, and the team switches to intravenous furosemide. Which property of the thiazide best explains its limited diuretic effect?","choices":{"A":"About 90% of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed upstream","B":"It is filtered poorly because it is protein bound","C":"It blocks a transporter in the collecting duct","D":"It is inactivated by proximal carbonic anhydrase","E":"It acts on a segment that is impermeable to Na+"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazides block the Na+/Cl- symporter in the distal convoluted tubule, and the thiazide brick explains their relatively weak diuretic effect: approximately 90 percent of the filtered sodium has already been reabsorbed before tubular fluid reaches the DCT. For this reason they are not used alone for the pulmonary edema of heart failure, where loop diuretics are preferred. Thiazides reach their luminal target by tubular secretion, they act in the DCT rather than the collecting duct, they are not inactivated by carbonic anhydrase, and the DCT actively reabsorbs sodium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: despite their milder natriuresis, thiazides have a stronger antihypertensive effect than loop agents, partly because they are also mild vasodilators. COMLEX: chlorthalidone has a longer half-life and larger volume of distribution than hydrochlorothiazide, which favors overnight blood pressure control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 1 — loop diuretic transport and high-ceiling natriuresis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Thiazides reach their luminal ","E":"Thiazides reach their luminal "}},{"id":"sdl45_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis and ascites is treated with spironolactone. Which cellular effect in collecting duct principal cells produces his natriuresis?","choices":{"A":"Direct plugging of the epithelial sodium channel pore","B":"Blocked aldosterone signaling that lowers ENaC activity","C":"Inhibition of the NKCC2 cotransporter in the luminal membrane","D":"Increased number of apical K+ channels for secretion","E":"Stimulation of basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spironolactone competitively inhibits the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor, so aldosterone can no longer increase ENaC activity, Na+/K+-ATPase activity, or the number of K+ channels. Sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion are blocked, which produces a sodium diuresis that pulls excess fluid from the peritoneum. Direct channel blockers such as amiloride act at ENaC itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hepatic ascites, heart failure, and hyperaldosteronism among the uses of spironolactone. COMLEX: patients with end-stage liver disease are often malnourished and prone to hypokalemia, so K+ sparing is useful.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — late distal principal cell transport and the kinetics of aldosterone action","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct channel blockers such a"}},{"id":"sdl45_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with hypertension takes hydrochlorothiazide, and his serum K+ is 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Amiloride is added. Which pattern best describes the effect of amiloride on urinary electrolytes and blood pH?","choices":{"A":"Higher Na+ and K+ excretion with alkalosis","B":"Higher Na+ and HCO3- excretion with acidosis","C":"Higher Na+ and lower K+ excretion with acidosis","D":"Higher Na+, K+, and Cl- excretion with unchanged pH","E":"Higher Na+ and lower Ca2+ excretion with alkalosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sodium channel inhibitors raise urinary Na+ and lower urinary K+ and tend to cause acidosis, because they inhibit H+ secretion in parallel with K+ secretion, and chronic hyperkalemia may also suppress proximal tubule ammonia synthesis. Loop diuretics raise Na+, K+, and Ca2+ and cause alkalosis, thiazides raise Na+ and K+, lower Ca2+, and cause alkalosis, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors raise HCO3- and cause acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: K+-sparing diuretics cause hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis, whereas loop and thiazide diuretics cause hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. COMLEX: a combined thiazide and ENaC blocker offsets the potassium loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — acid-base status and transcellular H+-K+ exchange","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loop diuretics raise Na+, K+, ","B":"Loop diuretics raise Na+, K+, "}},{"id":"sdl45_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with heart failure takes spironolactone, and his 58-year-old wife with resistant hypertension takes amiloride with hydrochlorothiazide. Both develop mild hyperkalemia. Which cellular effect do both potassium-sparing drugs share in collecting duct principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Reduced H+ ATPase activity on the apical membrane","B":"Reduced Na+/K+ ATPase activity on the basolateral membrane","C":"Reduced number of ENaCs in the basolateral membrane","D":"Increased number of ENaCs in the apical membrane","E":"Reduced Na+ influx from the tubular fluid into the cell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both subclasses reduce sodium entry from the tubular fluid into principal cells, amiloride by blocking ENaC directly and spironolactone by blocking the aldosterone effect that increases ENaC number and activity. Amiloride acts without affecting the aldosterone receptor or the ATPase, whereas spironolactone also reduces Na+/K+-ATPase activity. ENaC is an apical channel, and neither drug increases its number.","boardPrep":"Step 1: both classes cause hyperkalemia by reducing K+ secretion at the collecting duct. COMLEX: both classes are weak diuretics because most sodium is already reabsorbed by the time fluid reaches the collecting duct.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy (eGFR 34 mL/min/1.73 m2) and hypertension takes lisinopril. Spironolactone is added, and 10 days later his serum K+ is 6.4 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Na+ 135 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), Cl- 108 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which mechanism best explains his acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Inherited H+-ATPase defect in alpha-intercalated cells","B":"Impaired proximal HCO3- reabsorption from enzyme inhibition","C":"Gastrointestinal HCO3- loss from chronic diarrhea","D":"Suppressed proximal ammonia synthesis from hyperkalemia","E":"Accumulation of lactate from tissue hypoperfusion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic hyperkalemia from potassium-sparing diuretics, made worse by an ACE inhibitor and reduced GFR, may suppress proximal tubule ammonia synthesis, the buffer needed to excrete body acid. The result is a normal anion gap acidosis with high serum potassium, hyperkalemic type 4 RTA. His anion gap is 135 - 108 - 17 = 10 mEq/L, which is normal, so lactate is not the cause. K+-sparing diuretics can also inhibit H+ secretion in parallel with K+ secretion, but hyperkalemia suppressing ammonia synthesis is the main mechanism of the type 4 acidosis. An inherited H+-ATPase defect would produce a distal type 1 picture with hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia inhibits proximal ammoniagenesis and causes type 4 RTA. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and oral potassium supplements increase the hyperkalemia risk with potassium-sparing diuretics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — the pathophysiology of hyperkalemic (Type 4) RTA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"His anion gap is 135 - 108 - 1"}},{"id":"sdl45_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman who eats very little and drinks large volumes of fluid started hydrochlorothiazide two weeks ago for hypertension. She is confused, appears euvolemic, and has a serum Na+ of 122 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L). Which mechanism best explains her hyponatremia?","choices":{"A":"Impaired urine concentration from a lost medullary gradient","B":"Excess sodium loss from blocked thick limb transport","C":"Aldosterone-driven sodium retention with water dilution","D":"Autonomous ADH release from an occult lung tumor","E":"Impaired maximal urinary dilution with continued water intake"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Thiazides block sodium reabsorption in the DCT diluting segment, so the kidney cannot excrete fully diluted urine, and drinking a lot of water while eating little food leads to water retention and hyponatremia. Hypovolemia-induced ADH release and increased thirst also contribute, and these patients often do not look volume depleted. Elderly women are most prone to thiazide-induced hyponatremia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hyponatremia among the adverse effects of thiazides. COMLEX: thiazide-induced hyponatremia can be prevented by reducing the dose or limiting water intake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — SIADH versus diabetes insipidus and free water handling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl45_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man takes hydrochlorothiazide and nifedipine, and his blood pressure is 162/92 mm Hg. His history includes hyperlipidemia with elevated LDL cholesterol, obesity with a BMI of 37, and a GFR of 38 mL/min/1.73 m2. Amiloride is considered. Which feature most argues against adding it?","choices":{"A":"Limited renal capacity to excrete potassium","B":"Obesity with a body mass index of 37","C":"Hyperlipidemia with elevated LDL cholesterol","D":"Concurrent nifedipine calcium channel blockade","E":"Concurrent hydrochlorothiazide therapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Potassium-sparing diuretics can worsen underlying hyperkalemia, so they are contraindicated in significant acute or chronic renal impairment, and they should be used with caution in anyone prone to hyperkalemia such as patients with chronic kidney disease. Hydrochlorothiazide wastes potassium and is often paired with a potassium-sparing agent. Obesity, hyperlipidemia, and a calcium channel blocker are not listed contraindications.","boardPrep":"Step 1: K+-sparing diuretics carry a risk of hyperkalemia that rises when renal K+ excretion is impaired. COMLEX: significant renal impairment is a contraindication, and potassium should be monitored.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — segmental potassium handling along the nephron","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Obesity, hyperlipidemia, and a","C":"Obesity, hyperlipidemia, and a","E":"Hydrochlorothiazide wastes pot"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl45_q1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man in the intensive care unit receives high-dose intravenous furosemide and gentamicin. On day 3 he reports ringing in both ears and trouble following conversation. Which adverse effect of the diuretic best explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Rising serum calcium level","B":"Rising serum magnesium level","C":"Sensorineural hearing loss","D":"Rising serum potassium level","E":"Painful breast enlargement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loop diuretics can cause ototoxicity, especially at high intravenous doses or with other ototoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides. Rising serum calcium is wrong, because loop diuretics cause calciuria and lower serum calcium. Rising serum magnesium is wrong, because they cause magnesium wasting. Rising serum potassium is wrong, because they cause hypokalemia. Painful breast enlargement is an antiandrogenic effect of spironolactone. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 48, loop diuretic side effects including ototoxicity); Katzung Ch. 15 (ototoxicity of loop diuretics, increased with aminoglycosides).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretic adverse effects are ototoxicity, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypocalcemia, hyperuricemia, and volume depletion. COMLEX: avoid combining loop diuretics with aminoglycosides when possible.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 1 — aminoglycosides accumulate when GFR falls","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl45_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Ceiling Loop Diuretic Transport & Secretion","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with heart failure starts oral furosemide. Which property of furosemide explains why it depends on proximal tubular secretion to reach its luminal target?","choices":{"A":"Extensive hepatic drug metabolism","B":"Passive diffusion from the medulla","C":"Protein binding that limits filtration","D":"Large molecular size of the drug","E":"Reabsorption by the proximal tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loop diuretics are extensively bound to plasma albumin and are not freely filtered, so they reach the luminal NKCC2 target only after secretion into the proximal tubule by organic anion transporters (OAT1 and OAT3). Extensive hepatic drug metabolism is not why the drug depends on secretion. Passive diffusion from the medulla does not deliver the drug to the lumen. Reabsorption by the proximal tubule would lower, not provide, luminal delivery. Large molecular size is not the reason; protein binding is. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 45, secretory pharmacokinetics of loop diuretics); Katzung Ch. 15 (loop agents are secreted by the organic acid secretory system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics are protein-bound and reach the lumen by OAT secretion, so uremic acids that compete for OAT require higher doses. COMLEX: diuretic resistance in kidney disease often needs a higher dose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 1 — secreted drugs depend on proximal OAT and OCT transporters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl45_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Thiazide Transport, Calcium Sparing, & GFR Thresholds","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with heart failure and an estimated GFR of 22 mL/min/1.73 m2 (reference range: above 90 mL/min/1.73 m2) remains edematous on high-dose furosemide. His physician adds a thiazide-type diuretic for sequential nephron blockade. Which agent is classically chosen?","choices":{"A":"Mannitol","B":"Hydrochlorothiazide","C":"Acetazolamide","D":"Spironolactone","E":"Metolazone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Metolazone is the thiazide-like drug usually added to a loop diuretic for refractory edema at low GFR. Hydrochlorothiazide used alone loses much of its effect below a GFR of about 30 mL/min, so metolazone is the conventional choice for sequential nephron blockade. Acetazolamide is a weak diuretic whose effect is self-limited. Spironolactone carries a high hyperkalemia risk in advanced kidney disease. Mannitol is contraindicated in heart failure with low GFR because it expands plasma volume. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 45, GFR thresholds and the metolazone exception); Katzung Ch. 15 (metolazone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metolazone is the classic thiazide-like add-on to loop diuretics in renal insufficiency. COMLEX: loop plus metolazone overcomes diuretic resistance but needs close electrolyte monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 1 — loop diuretics need OAT secretion, which falls in renal failure","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Hydrochlorothiazide used alone"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl45_q4","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Collecting Duct Potassium-Sparing Mechanisms","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has hypertension and hypokalemia. Plasma renin and aldosterone are both suppressed, and genetic testing shows a gain-of-function mutation in the epithelial sodium channel. Which drug most directly corrects his disorder?","choices":{"A":"Eplerenone","B":"Furosemide","C":"Spironolactone","D":"Triamterene","E":"Chlorthalidone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With aldosterone already suppressed, the overactive sodium channel itself has to be blocked. Triamterene, like amiloride, directly blocks apical ENaC in principal cells, abolishing the lumen-negative potential that drives K+ secretion. Eplerenone and spironolactone block the mineralocorticoid receptor, which does little when aldosterone is low and the channel is constitutively active. Furosemide would worsen hypokalemia. Chlorthalidone would also worsen hypokalemia. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 45, ENaC blockers vs mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists); Katzung Ch. 15 (amiloride and triamterene block ENaC).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ENaC blockers (amiloride, triamterene) act directly on the channel, whereas MRAs work only when aldosterone is present. COMLEX: low renin and low aldosterone with hypertension and hypokalemia suggests a channel gain of function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct agents conserve K+ by removing the lumen-negative potential","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl45_q5","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman takes spironolactone for primary hyperaldosteronism. After 4 months she develops irregular menses and breast tenderness. Which mechanism best explains these effects?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of aromatase in adipose tissue","B":"Androgen and progesterone receptor binding","C":"Suppression of pituitary LH secretion","D":"Hyperkalemia disrupting normal ovarian function","E":"Direct blockade of estrogen receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spironolactone is non-selective and binds androgen and progesterone receptors, which causes gynecomastia, decreased libido, and menstrual irregularities. Eplerenone, which is selective for the mineralocorticoid receptor, avoids these effects. Inhibition of aromatase would lower estrogen, which is not this drug's action. Suppression of pituitary LH is not the described mechanism. Hyperkalemia does not explain menstrual and breast effects. Direct blockade of estrogen receptors is not how spironolactone acts. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 45, antiandrogenic side effects of spironolactone); Katzung Ch. 15 (spironolactone endocrine effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: spironolactone causes gynecomastia and menstrual changes through androgen and progesterone receptor binding, and eplerenone is the selective alternative. COMLEX: switch to eplerenone if these effects are intolerable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists act on principal cells","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Hyperkalemia does not explain "}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl45_q6","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man starts hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Three months later his fasting glucose is 128 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL) and his serum K+ is 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism best explains his higher glucose?","choices":{"A":"Hypokalemia impairing insulin release","B":"Volume loss raising cortisol levels","C":"Autoimmune destruction of beta cells","D":"Hypercalcemia impairing insulin action","E":"Hyperuricemia blocking glucose uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazide-induced hypokalemia impairs insulin release from pancreatic beta cells and lowers peripheral insulin sensitivity, producing the hyperglycemia of the HyperGLUC profile. Hypercalcemia is part of the profile but is not the stated cause of hyperglycemia. Volume loss raising cortisol is not the described mechanism. Autoimmune destruction of beta cells describes type 1 diabetes, not a drug effect. Hyperuricemia predisposes to gout, not to impaired glucose uptake. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 45, thiazide HyperGLUC profile); Katzung Ch. 15 (thiazide hyperglycemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide HyperGLUC means hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia. COMLEX: correcting hypokalemia can improve thiazide-related glucose intolerance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 2 — upstream diuretics cause potassium wasting","isHighYield":false},{"id":"grid_sdl45_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Metabolic Complications & Renal Impairment Limits","batch":4,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction comes to the clinic for follow-up 3 weeks after her dose of a diuretic that inhibits the Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter in the thick ascending limb was doubled for worsening leg edema. Since then she has lost 5 kg. Her only other medication is atorvastatin. Blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg, and pulse is 88/min. Mucous membranes are dry, the lungs are clear, and there is trace ankle edema. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum K⁺","Serum HCO₃⁻","Urinary Ca²⁺ excretion"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↓","↓"],"C":["↓","↓","↑"],"D":["↓","↔","↑"],"E":["↓","↑","↑"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Blocking NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb produces all three changes in the pattern (↓ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻, ↑ urinary Ca²⁺ excretion). More Na⁺ and fluid reach the collecting duct while volume contraction raises aldosterone, so principal cells secrete more K⁺ and serum K⁺ falls. Volume contraction also activates angiotensin II and aldosterone, increasing proximal Na⁺–H⁺ exchange and HCO₃⁻ reabsorption and distal H⁺ secretion, a contraction alkalosis. Finally, NKCC2 activity generates the +7 to +8 mV lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ reabsorption across the thick ascending limb, so blocking it raises urinary Ca²⁺ excretion (loops lose Ca²⁺). The pattern (↓ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻, ↓ urinary Ca²⁺ excretion) is the thiazide pattern, with the same hypokalemic alkalosis but reduced Ca²⁺ excretion. The pattern (↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻, ↑ urinary Ca²⁺ excretion) pairs hypokalemia with metabolic acidosis, the picture of a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, which wastes HCO₃⁻; loop diuretics raise serum bicarbonate. The pattern (↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻, ↓ urinary Ca²⁺ excretion) is hyperkalemia with metabolic acidosis, the effect of aldosterone blockade by K⁺-sparing diuretics, not of a loop agent. The pattern (↓ serum K⁺, ↔ serum HCO₃⁻, ↑ urinary Ca²⁺ excretion) gets the K⁺ and Ca²⁺ effects right but leaves out the contraction alkalosis; her 5-kg weight loss, dry mucous membranes, and low-normal blood pressure show volume contraction, which raises serum HCO₃⁻.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loops lose Ca²⁺ (used for hypercalcemia) while thiazides retain it (used for calcium stones and idiopathic hypercalciuria); both cause hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. COMLEX: after a loop dose increase, recheck K⁺ and Mg²⁺, since loop diuretics cause both hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — NKCC2 drives the lumen-positive potential behind paracellular Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb","isHighYield":true}],"46":[{"id":"sdl46_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug directly inhibits renin?","choices":{"A":"Lisinopril","B":"Losartan","C":"Candesartan","D":"Benazepril","E":"Aliskiren"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aliskiren binds directly to renin and inhibits its binding to angiotensinogen, which reduces cleavage of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I and lowers all downstream RAAS effectors. It was the first orally active nonpeptide renin inhibitor approved for hypertension. Lisinopril and benazepril are ACE inhibitors, and losartan and candesartan are angiotensin receptor blockers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aliskiren acts at the earliest RAAS step, blocking conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. COMLEX: it lowers blood pressure but has no proven outcome benefit, and combining it with an ACE inhibitor or ARB is not recommended.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators such as angiotensin II adjust renal arteriolar resistances and GFR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"Dilation of which vessel by ACE inhibitors and ARBs lowers glomerular hydrostatic pressure?","choices":{"A":"Afferent arteriole","B":"Interlobular artery","C":"Efferent arteriole","D":"Vasa recta","E":"Peritubular capillary"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Under normal circumstances angiotensin II raises GFR by constricting the efferent arteriole. Drugs that lower angiotensin II levels or block its effect dilate the efferent arteriole, which lowers glomerular hydrostatic pressure. This underlies both the early benefit in diabetic kidney disease and the risk of prerenal acute kidney injury in low-flow states. The interlobular artery lies upstream of the glomerulus, and the vasa recta and peritubular capillaries lie downstream of the efferent arteriole, so dilating them does not set glomerular pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs reduce intraglomerular pressure by dilating the efferent arteriole. COMLEX: expect an early rise in creatinine after starting these drugs, which is often acceptable if the GFR stabilizes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators such as angiotensin II adjust renal arteriolar resistances and GFR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The interlobular artery lies u","D":"The interlobular artery lies u","E":"The interlobular artery lies u"}},{"id":"sdl46_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":1,"stem":"A volume-depleted patient starts an ACE inhibitor, and the GFR falls within the first few days. What type of kidney injury is this?","choices":{"A":"Prerenal acute kidney injury","B":"Postrenal acute kidney injury","C":"Toxic acute tubular necrosis","D":"Acute interstitial nephritis","E":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Efferent arteriolar dilation acutely decreases GFR, especially in low-flow states such as dehydration or heart failure. Because the fall in GFR reflects reduced glomerular hydrostatic pressure rather than obstruction or tubular, interstitial, or glomerular injury, it is a form of prerenal acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RAAS inhibitors plus low renal perfusion cause prerenal azotemia through loss of efferent tone. COMLEX: if the GFR keeps falling, monitor closely and reduce the dose; a small early decline may be acceptable given the benefit in CKD and heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators such as angiotensin II adjust renal arteriolar resistances and GFR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Because the fall in GFR reflec","C":"Because the fall in GFR reflec","D":"Because the fall in GFR reflec","E":"Because the fall in GFR reflec"}},{"id":"sdl46_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":1,"stem":"Accumulation of which mediator is thought to cause the dry cough seen with ACE inhibitors?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin II","B":"Aldosterone","C":"Bradykinin","D":"Vasopressin","E":"Angiotensinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ACE also degrades bradykinin, an inflammatory mediator that causes vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and bronchial smooth muscle contraction. ACE inhibition leads to bradykinin accumulation, which is thought to cause the dry, hacking cough. Angiotensin II and aldosterone fall with these drugs. Vasopressin is not the mediator that ACE degrades.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cough and angioedema are both bradykinin-mediated ACE inhibitor effects. COMLEX: ARBs have a reduced incidence of cough, so they are the usual substitute when cough limits therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone pathway that ACE inhibition and receptor blockade act on","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":1,"stem":"Which route mainly clears ACE inhibitors from the body?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic metabolism","B":"Biliary secretion","C":"Pulmonary exhalation","D":"Renal excretion","E":"Fecal excretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ACE inhibitors are cleared predominantly by the kidney through glomerular filtration and tubular secretion, so the dosage should be lowered in patients with reduced GFR to prevent accumulation. ARBs, in contrast, are cleared mostly by hepatic metabolism and biliary excretion, so they depend less on renal function. ACE inhibitors are not cleared by exhalation; their elimination is renal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renally cleared drugs accumulate as GFR falls. COMLEX: dose adjustment of ACE inhibitors in reduced GFR is a standard prescribing safeguard.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone pathway that ACE inhibition and receptor blockade act on","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"ACE inhibitors are not cleared"}},{"id":"sdl46_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":1,"stem":"Which rare complication of ACE inhibitors can swell the lips and tongue and obstruct the upper airway?","choices":{"A":"Angioedema","B":"Dry cough","C":"Hyperkalemia","D":"Prerenal AKI","E":"Fetal renal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ACE inhibitor-associated angioedema results from bradykinin accumulation and commonly affects the upper airways, face, lips, and tongue. Airway obstruction is the most feared complication and can be fatal. Cough does not obstruct the airway, and hyperkalemia, prerenal AKI, and fetal renal injury are other adverse effects without airway swelling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor angioedema is bradykinin-mediated and contraindicated in C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency. COMLEX: ARBs have a much lower risk of angioedema and cough than ACE inhibitors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone pathway that ACE inhibition and receptor blockade act on","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug for male reproductive disorders inhibits both the type 1 and type 2 isoforms of 5-alpha-reductase?","choices":{"A":"Finasteride","B":"Dutasteride","C":"Tamsulosin","D":"Tadalafil","E":"Vardenafil"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dutasteride inhibits both isoforms and therefore suppresses DHT synthesis more completely than finasteride, which selectively inhibits the type 2 isoform. Serum DHT falls by roughly 70 percent with finasteride and by more than 90 percent with dutasteride. Tamsulosin is an alpha-1 blocker, and tadalafil and vardenafil are PDE5 inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT, and finasteride and dutasteride block that step upstream of the androgen receptor. COMLEX: both agents are approved for BPH, and finasteride at 1 mg is also used for androgenic alopecia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency is compared with pharmacologic 5-alpha-reductase inhibition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":1,"stem":"By roughly what percentage do 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors lower serum PSA?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 35 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"These drugs lower PSA by approximately 50 percent, which must be accounted for when screening for prostate cancer. The value of about 70 percent describes the fall in serum DHT with finasteride, not the fall in PSA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors halve PSA, so a measured value is interpreted at roughly double. COMLEX: PSA screening for prostate cancer still matters while a patient takes these agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency is compared with pharmacologic 5-alpha-reductase inhibition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":1,"stem":"Which adverse effect of 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors can persist after the drug is stopped, a phenomenon termed post-finasteride syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Orthostatic hypotension","B":"Visual disturbances","C":"Nasal congestion","D":"Sexual dysfunction","E":"Prolonged erection"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sexual dysfunction, including decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and ejaculatory disorders, occurs in a minority of patients and may persist after discontinuation. Visual disturbances and nasal congestion are PDE5 inhibitor effects, and priapism is linked to alpha-1 blockade and pro-erectile agents. Orthostatic hypotension is a first-dose effect of alpha-1 blockers, not the persistent effect of 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors cause decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and ejaculatory disorders. COMLEX: both agents are teratogenic to male fetuses and contraindicated in pregnancy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency is compared with pharmacologic 5-alpha-reductase inhibition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":1,"stem":"PDE5 inhibitors prolong the action of which second messenger in penile smooth muscle?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Diacylglycerol","D":"Calcium ions","E":"Inositol trisphosphate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PDE5 is the enzyme that degrades cGMP in penile tissue. Sildenafil, tadalafil, and vardenafil competitively block it, prolonging cGMP activity and sustaining smooth muscle relaxation, which enhances and prolongs the physiologic response to sexual stimulation. PDE5 degrades cGMP, so cAMP, diacylglycerol, calcium and IP3 are not the messenger whose action it prolongs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors block cGMP breakdown. COMLEX: they do not generate erections independently and depend on intact nitric oxide signaling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic control of penile erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"PDE5 degrades cGMP, so cAMP, d","E":"PDE5 degrades cGMP, so cAMP, d"}},{"id":"sdl46_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":1,"stem":"Which mediator released by nonadrenergic, noncholinergic nerve endings and endothelium in the corpus cavernosum activates guanylyl cyclase to start erection?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Endothelin","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Angiotensin II","E":"Vasopressin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sexual stimulation triggers release of nitric oxide from nonadrenergic, noncholinergic nerve endings and vascular endothelium. Nitric oxide activates guanylyl cyclase, which converts GTP to cGMP, and elevated cGMP relaxes smooth muscle in penile arterioles, increasing blood inflow. Norepinephrine promotes detumescence rather than erection. Angiotensin II and vasopressin are vasoconstrictors, not the NANC and endothelial mediator that activates guanylyl cyclase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the erection pathway runs NO, guanylyl cyclase, cGMP, then smooth muscle relaxation. COMLEX: parasympathetic input drives erection, while sympathetic norepinephrine drives detumescence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic control of penile erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug class is contraindicated with PDE5 inhibitors because of the risk of profound hypotension?","choices":{"A":"Statins","B":"Biguanides","C":"Anticoagulants","D":"Thyroid hormones","E":"Nitrates"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nitrates also elevate cGMP through nitric oxide-dependent pathways, so co-administration with a PDE5 inhibitor produces additive vasodilation and can cause life-threatening drops in blood pressure. The other classes do not act through the cGMP vasodilator pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a PDE5 inhibitor plus a nitrate is the classic contraindicated combination. COMLEX: ask every man with erectile dysfunction about nitrate use, including for angina, before prescribing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic control of penile erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":1,"stem":"A painful erection unrelated to sexual stimulation is termed priapism once it persists for how long?","choices":{"A":"Beyond 30 minutes","B":"Beyond 1 hour","C":"Beyond 2 hours","D":"Beyond 4 hours","E":"Beyond 6 hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Priapism is a prolonged, often painful erection persisting beyond four hours without sexual stimulation. It is a urologic emergency because sustained ischemia in the corpus cavernosum can cause irreversible fibrosis and permanent erectile dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic priapism is a painful sustained erection lasting more than 4 hours. COMLEX: treatment is typically needed within 4 to 6 hours to prevent irreversible smooth muscle damage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic tone governs both erection and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":1,"stem":"Which antidepressant with potent alpha-1 blocking activity is frequently cited as a cause of drug-induced priapism?","choices":{"A":"Trazodone","B":"Bupropion","C":"Fluoxetine","D":"Escitalopram","E":"Duloxetine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trazodone has potent alpha-1 adrenergic blocking activity and is one of the most frequently cited causes of drug-induced priapism. Blocking alpha-1 receptors removes the sympathetic norepinephrine signal that constricts cavernous smooth muscle and ends an erection. Bupropion, fluoxetine, escitalopram and duloxetine lack the potent alpha-1 blockade that makes trazodone a classic cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: trazodone is an antidepressant associated with priapism through alpha-1 blockade. COMLEX: review the medication list of any patient presenting with a prolonged erection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic tone governs both erection and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bupropion, fluoxetine, escital","C":"Bupropion, fluoxetine, escital","D":"Bupropion, fluoxetine, escital","E":"Bupropion, fluoxetine, escital"}},{"id":"sdl46_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":1,"stem":"Which abnormality underlies ischemic, or low-flow, priapism?","choices":{"A":"Excessive arterial inflow into the corpus cavernosum","B":"Impaired venous outflow from the corpus cavernosum","C":"Impaired lymphatic drainage of the corpus cavernosum","D":"Reduced nitric oxide release from cavernous nerves","E":"Increased sympathetic tone on cavernous smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most common form is ischemic, or low-flow, priapism, caused by impaired venous outflow from the corpus cavernosum. Blood becomes trapped, oxygen tension falls, and metabolic acidosis develops within the cavernous tissue. Reduced nitric oxide release and increased sympathetic tone would oppose an erection rather than sustain it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic priapism is low-flow with trapped, deoxygenated blood, and sickled red blood cells can block venous drainage of the corpus cavernosum. COMLEX: it is a urologic emergency treated immediately.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 8 — autonomic tone governs both erection and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":1,"stem":"THC suppresses GnRH pulsatility by activating which hypothalamic receptor?","choices":{"A":"Mu opioid receptor","B":"D2 dopamine receptor","C":"CB1 receptor","D":"GABA-B receptor","E":"5-HT2A receptor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"THC binds cannabinoid receptors expressed throughout the male reproductive axis. Activation of CB1 receptors in the hypothalamus suppresses GnRH pulsatility, lowering LH and FSH secretion and thereby decreasing testosterone production and impairing spermatogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: THC suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis through CB1 receptors. COMLEX: screen for recreational drug use in men with low libido, erectile dysfunction, or infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — chronic THC exposure and its reproductive, endothelial, and drug-metabolism consequences","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old man who has smoked marijuana daily for 8 years reports decreased libido. He takes no other drugs, and his testes are normal on examination. Which hormone pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low LH, low FSH, and low testosterone","B":"High LH, high FSH, and low testosterone","C":"Low LH, low FSH, and normal testosterone","D":"High LH, high FSH, and high testosterone","E":"Normal LH, normal FSH, and low testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Suppressed GnRH pulsatility reduces LH and FSH secretion, and the downstream consequence is decreased testosterone production and impaired spermatogenesis. A central mechanism therefore lowers all three hormones, whereas raised gonadotropins would indicate a testicular defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a central (hypogonadotropic) pattern has low LH, low FSH, and low testosterone. COMLEX: chronic users also show reduced sperm count, decreased motility, and abnormal morphology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — chronic THC exposure and its reproductive, endothelial, and drug-metabolism consequences","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"A central mechanism therefore ","D":"A central mechanism therefore "}},{"id":"sdl46_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which cytochrome P450 enzyme metabolizes sildenafil and also handles THC?","choices":{"A":"CYP2D6","B":"CYP2E1","C":"CYP1A2","D":"CYP3A4","E":"CYP2B6"},"correct":"D","explanation":"THC and cannabidiol are metabolized by hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes, particularly CYP3A4 and CYP2C9, and PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil are also CYP3A4 substrates. Concurrent marijuana use may therefore alter sildenafil plasma concentrations. The source names CYP3A4, with CYP2C9 for THC, not CYP2D6, 2E1, 1A2 or 2B6.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CYP3A4 substrates such as sildenafil are sensitive to other agents that share this pathway. COMLEX: marijuana can potentiate hypotension with PDE5 inhibitors, particularly in patients with cardiovascular disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — chronic THC exposure and its reproductive, endothelial, and drug-metabolism consequences","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The source names CYP3A4, with ","B":"The source names CYP3A4, with ","C":"The source names CYP3A4, with ","E":"The source names CYP3A4, with "}},{"id":"sdl46_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and hypertension has persistent microalbuminuria and a preserved GFR. His physician starts lisinopril to slow progression of his diabetic kidney disease. Which mechanism best explains the renal benefit of this drug?","choices":{"A":"Afferent arteriolar dilation that raises glomerular capillary pressure","B":"Reduced renal plasma flow that lowers the filtration fraction","C":"Efferent arteriolar dilation that lowers glomerular capillary pressure","D":"Direct restoration of negative charge on the basement membrane","E":"Blockade of tubular glucose reabsorption that lowers filtered glucose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In diabetic kidney disease, early use of ACE inhibitors reduces hydrostatic pressure in the glomerulus and decreases microalbuminuria. Angiotensin II normally constricts the efferent arteriole, so blocking its formation dilates the efferent arteriole and lowers glomerular capillary pressure, which is why ACE inhibitors and ARBs are used for chronic kidney disease with proteinuria. ACE inhibitors act through glomerular hemodynamics and do not restore basement membrane charge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs lower intraglomerular pressure and slow diabetic nephropathy. COMLEX: CKD with proteinuria and comorbid hypertension or heart failure favors an ACE inhibitor or ARB, whereas CKD without proteinuria does not specifically benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — how afferent versus efferent arteriolar resistance changes glomerular capillary pressure, plasma flow, GFR, and filtration fraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension and osteoarthritis has taken lisinopril for two years and started daily ibuprofen last week. She is mildly dehydrated from a viral illness, and her creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 1.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains her fall in GFR?","choices":{"A":"Afferent dilation from ibuprofen plus efferent constriction from lisinopril","B":"Obstruction of urine outflow that raises Bowman space hydrostatic pressure","C":"Immune complex deposition in the glomerulus with mesangial proliferation","D":"Loss of prostaglandin afferent dilation plus ACE inhibitor efferent dilation","E":"Direct tubular toxicity from ibuprofen that halts filtrate flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, and prostaglandins normally dilate the afferent arteriole. Blocking that afferent dilation while the ACE inhibitor dilates the efferent arteriole reduces glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR, especially in a patient already at risk such as one who is dehydrated. This is a form of prerenal acute kidney injury. The injury is hemodynamic, a prerenal fall in filtration pressure, not direct tubular toxicity halting filtrate flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAID plus ACE inhibitor plus volume depletion is a classic hemodynamic cause of prerenal AKI. COMLEX: RAAS inhibitors should not be used with chronic NSAIDs because of the risk of prerenal AKI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — local prostaglandins protect the afferent arteriole, and NSAIDs remove that buffer in hypoperfused states","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with hypertension has not reached her blood pressure goal on several other antihypertensive agents. Her physician adds a drug that lowers plasma angiotensin I along with all downstream RAAS effectors. At which step does this drug act?","choices":{"A":"Blocking ACE from converting angiotensin I to angiotensin II","B":"Blocking AT1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle","C":"Blocking mineralocorticoid receptors in principal cells","D":"Blocking aldosterone synthesis in the adrenal cortex","E":"Blocking renin from cleaving angiotensinogen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Direct renin inhibitors act at the earliest point in the RAAS: they bind renin and inhibit its binding to angiotensinogen, which reduces the formation of angiotensin I and every downstream effector. Aliskiren is indicated only for hypertension and only when other agents have failed. ACE inhibitors and ARBs act one and two steps further downstream. Mineralocorticoid receptor blockade and inhibition of aldosterone synthesis act at the end of the cascade and would not lower angiotensin I.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aliskiren blocks conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. COMLEX: it should not be combined with ACE inhibitors or ARBs because of additive hyperkalemia and AKI without added benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — renin-angiotensin-aldosterone effectors in renal sodium balance and blood pressure control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"ACE inhibitors and ARBs act on"}},{"id":"sdl46_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man who started ramipril three weeks ago presents with rapid swelling of the lips and tongue and difficulty swallowing. He has no urticaria or itching and no known allergies. Which mechanism best explains his condition?","choices":{"A":"Bradykinin buildup that increases vascular permeability","B":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation with histamine release","C":"Angiotensin II accumulation that constricts dermal venules","D":"Aldosterone excess that expands the interstitial fluid volume","E":"Complement activation by circulating immune complexes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ACE also degrades bradykinin, which causes vasodilation and increased vascular permeability. Inhibiting ACE lets bradykinin accumulate, producing angioedema of the face, lips, tongue, and upper airway. Airway obstruction is the most feared complication and can be fatal. Angiotensin II and aldosterone levels fall, rather than rise, with ACE inhibition. The absence of urticaria and itching argues against IgE-mediated histamine release, and the swelling is a drug effect on bradykinin, not immune complex disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor angioedema is bradykinin-mediated, not allergic, and it is contraindicated in C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency. COMLEX: airway obstruction is the most feared complication, and ARBs carry a much lower risk of angioedema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — extrinsic vasoactive mediators that adjust arteriolar tone in the kidney","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Angiotensin II and aldosterone"}},{"id":"sdl46_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with chronic kidney disease and an eGFR of 25 mL/min/1.73 m2 is prescribed enalapril for heart failure. The pharmacist recommends a lower dose than usual. Which property of the drug best justifies this recommendation?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic clearance that falls when renal perfusion declines","B":"Renal elimination that causes accumulation at a low GFR","C":"Pulmonary metabolism that saturates when GFR is reduced","D":"Active biliary secretion that stops when GFR is reduced","E":"Renal tubular toxicity that becomes dose-dependent at low GFR"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ACE inhibitors are cleared by the kidney, so the dosage should be lowered in patients with reduced GFR to avoid accumulation. ARBs, by contrast, are cleared mostly by the liver. Reduced clearance of a renally eliminated drug raises its systemic exposure. Dose reduction is to prevent accumulation, not because the drug becomes a tubular toxin at low GFR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renally eliminated drugs accumulate as GFR falls. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors are cleared by the kidney, whereas ARBs are largely hepatically cleared.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 1 — how falling GFR alters drug clearance and predicts systemic accumulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with heart failure takes enalapril and develops a persistent dry, hacking cough with a normal chest radiograph. His physician switches him to losartan, and the cough resolves within weeks. Which best explains the improvement?","choices":{"A":"Losartan inhibits renin so that less angiotensin I forms","B":"Losartan blocks bradykinin receptors on bronchial smooth muscle","C":"Receptor blockade leaves ACE-mediated bradykinin breakdown intact","D":"Losartan raises aldosterone, which relaxes bronchial muscle","E":"Losartan is cleared by the kidney and accumulates less"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ARBs act one step downstream of ACE inhibitors by blocking angiotensin II receptors instead of inhibiting ACE, so bradykinin degradation by ACE is unaffected. Bradykinin therefore does not accumulate, and the dry cough is much less common with ARBs. Aldosterone does not relax bronchial muscle, and blocking angiotensin II receptors lowers rather than raises aldosterone signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARBs do not increase bradykinin, so they are used for patients who cannot tolerate an ACE inhibitor because of cough or angioedema. COMLEX: the effect on angiotensin II signaling and the benefit in heart failure are similar to an ACE inhibitor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — renin-angiotensin-aldosterone effectors in renal sodium balance and blood pressure control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia asks how finasteride differs from the androgen receptor blocker his brother takes for metastatic prostate cancer. Which statement best describes the mechanism of finasteride?","choices":{"A":"It competitively blocks the androgen receptor in the prostate","B":"It suppresses pituitary LH release and testicular testosterone","C":"It antagonizes alpha-1 receptors in prostatic smooth muscle","D":"It increases peripheral aromatization of testosterone","E":"It limits DHT supply without blocking the androgen receptor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Finasteride inhibits type 2 5-alpha-reductase, the enzyme that converts testosterone to the more potent dihydrotestosterone within target tissues. The course brick stresses that 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors act upstream in androgen signaling: they do not block the androgen receptor itself but limit the availability of its most potent ligand, which shrinks the prostate and relieves obstruction. Receptor antagonism describes the brother's drug, LH suppression describes continuous GnRH agonists such as leuprolide, alpha-1 antagonism describes tamsulosin, and these inhibitors do not act by increasing aromatization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride lowers serum DHT by about 70 percent, whereas dutasteride lowers it by more than 90 percent. COMLEX: finasteride is also approved at a lower 1 mg dose for androgenic alopecia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — intracellular androgen receptor signaling","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Receptor antagonism describes ","B":"Receptor antagonism describes "}},{"id":"sdl46_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia has weak urinary stream. After six months of finasteride, imaging shows a smaller gland and his obstructive symptoms improve. Which effect best explains the response?","choices":{"A":"Blockade of androgen receptors in prostatic epithelium","B":"Lower intraprostatic DHT that shrinks the hyperplastic tissue","C":"Relaxation of prostatic smooth muscle by alpha-1 blockade","D":"Suppression of pituitary LH that removes testosterone drive","E":"Increased cGMP that relaxes the bladder neck musculature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase, lowering intraprostatic DHT and shrinking prostate volume, which relieves urinary obstruction in BPH. Its action is upstream in the androgen signaling cascade: it does not block the androgen receptor but limits the availability of the most potent androgen ligand. Finasteride does not suppress pituitary LH or raise cGMP; those describe GnRH-axis drugs and PDE5 inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DHT drives prostate growth, and 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors shrink the gland over months. COMLEX: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors lower PSA by about 50 percent and are teratogenic to male fetuses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — DHT-dependent transition zone hyperplasia and urethral compression in BPH","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Finasteride does not suppress "}},{"id":"sdl46_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man with androgenic alopecia starts finasteride 1 mg daily. Which action underlies the expected benefit to his hair follicles?","choices":{"A":"Decreased conversion of testosterone to DHT in target tissue","B":"Increased aromatization of testosterone to estradiol in hair follicles","C":"Stimulation of testosterone secretion by testicular Leydig cells","D":"Direct vasodilation of scalp arterioles through cGMP accumulation","E":"Suppression of pituitary gonadotropin release by negative feedback"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elevated DHT is implicated in androgenic alopecia. DHT is formed when 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone within target tissues, and finasteride, approved at the lower dose of 1 mg for androgenic alopecia, selectively inhibits the type 2 isoform to limit DHT synthesis. Finasteride does not raise cGMP or suppress gonadotropins; those describe PDE5 inhibitors and GnRH-axis drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: male pattern hair loss is DHT-dependent, and finasteride blocks its formation. COMLEX: counsel about decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and ejaculatory disorders, which can persist after discontinuation in a minority of patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — androgen actions that require 5-alpha-reductase conversion to DHT, including male pattern hair","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Finasteride does not raise cGM"}},{"id":"sdl46_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes and erectile dysfunction is prescribed sildenafil. He asks why the drug will not produce an erection if he takes it without any sexual arousal. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"It directly activates guanylyl cyclase in cavernous muscle","B":"It stimulates testosterone release that drives arousal","C":"It blocks alpha-1 receptors to relax cavernous smooth muscle","D":"It prolongs cGMP generated by arousal-driven nitric oxide release","E":"It releases nitric oxide from parasympathetic nerve endings"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PDE5 inhibitors block cGMP degradation and so prolong cGMP-mediated smooth muscle relaxation, but they do not generate erections independently. Sexual stimulation must first release nitric oxide, which activates guanylyl cyclase to make cGMP, so these drugs enhance rather than replace the physiologic response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors depend on endogenous NO signaling and amplify the response to stimulation. COMLEX: endothelial dysfunction from diabetes lowers NO production, which is a common organic cause of erectile dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man takes sildenafil for erectile dysfunction. He reports that for a few hours after each dose, white objects look tinted blue. Which mechanism best explains this visual effect?","choices":{"A":"Retinal vasoconstriction from a nitric oxide surplus","B":"Alpha-1 blockade of the iris dilator muscle","C":"Cross-inhibition of PDE6 in retinal photoreceptors","D":"Inhibition of PDE11 in the ciliary muscle","E":"Raised intraocular pressure from aqueous excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid lists cyanopia, or blue-tinted vision, as an adverse effect specific to sildenafil caused by inhibition of PDE6 in the retina, and the course brick attributes sildenafil's visual disturbances to its mild PDE6 inhibitory activity affecting retinal photoreceptors. Nitric oxide relaxes rather than constricts vascular smooth muscle, alpha-1 blockade of the iris dilator underlies floppy iris syndrome with tamsulosin, tadalafil's PDE11 inhibition is linked to myalgia, and raised intraocular pressure is not the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: other common PDE5 inhibitor effects are headache, flushing, nasal congestion, and dyspepsia. COMLEX: tadalafil's additional PDE11 inhibition may contribute to myalgia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 6 — ocular adverse effects of urologic and erectile drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":2,"stem":"In a healthy 30-year-old man, sexual arousal activates nonadrenergic, noncholinergic nerve endings that release nitric oxide, which activates soluble guanylyl cyclase in cavernous smooth muscle and raises cGMP. Which kinase does cGMP activate to relax the smooth muscle and produce erection?","choices":{"A":"Protein kinase G","B":"Protein kinase A","C":"Protein kinase C","D":"Janus kinase 2","E":"Src tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elevated cGMP activates protein kinase G, which opens potassium channels and acts on the myosin light chain kinase pathway, collectively producing smooth muscle relaxation, arterial dilation, and venous occlusion, the hemodynamic hallmarks of erection. PDE5 inhibitors act by prolonging this cGMP signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pathway is NO, soluble guanylyl cyclase, cGMP, PKG, then relaxation. COMLEX: this is the signaling axis that PDE5 inhibitors amplify and that NOS inhibition blunts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with schizophrenia started clozapine two weeks ago. He presents with a painful, rigid erection lasting six hours that is unrelated to sexual activity. He takes no erectile dysfunction medication. Which mechanism best explains this adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"PDE5 inhibition that prolongs cGMP-mediated relaxation","B":"Serotonin reuptake inhibition that raises spinal erectile drive","C":"Histamine H1 blockade that increases cavernous blood flow","D":"Elevated testosterone that sustains cavernous arterial inflow","E":"Alpha-1 blockade that impairs sympathetic detumescence"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Alpha-1 blockade is a shared property of many antipsychotics, including chlorpromazine and clozapine. Detumescence depends on sympathetic norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors of cavernous smooth muscle to cause vasoconstriction and venous outflow, so blocking these receptors prevents the contraction needed to end an erection. Clozapine is not a PDE5 inhibitor, and he takes no erectile dysfunction drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-1 blockade, as with trazodone, antipsychotics, and alpha-1 blockers, is a classic mechanism of drug-induced priapism. COMLEX: this is a urologic emergency, and ischemic priapism needs immediate treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — sympathetic and parasympathetic control of erection and detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who has trouble achieving erections self-injects alprostadil into the corpus cavernosum for the first time and develops a painful rigid erection lasting seven hours. Which outcome is most likely if the erection is not relieved?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune destruction of the cavernous nerves with penile anesthesia","B":"Fibrous plaque within the tunica albuginea with penile curvature","C":"Fibrosis of cavernous smooth muscle with permanent erectile dysfunction","D":"Atherosclerotic occlusion of the pudendal arteries with impotence","E":"Testicular atrophy from hypothalamic suppression of gonadotropins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Alprostadil is prostaglandin E1 given by intracavernosal injection and carries a dose-dependent priapism risk. Prolonged ischemic priapism traps blood, lowers oxygen tension, and produces acidosis in the cavernous tissue; if not treated promptly, typically within 4 to 6 hours, irreversible smooth muscle damage and fibrosis occur, causing permanent erectile dysfunction. Priapism damages cavernous tissue locally; it does not cause pudendal atherosclerosis or suppress gonadotropins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic priapism lasting over 4 hours is a urologic emergency because sustained ischemia damages the corpus cavernosum. COMLEX: treatment is immediate, for example corporal aspiration, intracavernosal phenylephrine, or surgical decompression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — mechanisms of penile erection and detumescence in the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia takes lisinopril, metformin, atorvastatin, omeprazole, and prazosin. He presents with a painful erection lasting five hours that began without sexual stimulation. Which drug class most likely contributed?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","B":"Alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist","C":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","D":"Biguanide antihyperglycemic","E":"Proton pump inhibitor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alpha-1 blockers such as prazosin and tamsulosin can precipitate priapism by blocking the sympathetic detumescence pathway: norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors normally constricts cavernous smooth muscle and drives venous outflow. None of his other medications acts on that pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-1 blockers, trazodone, antipsychotics, PDE5 inhibitors, and intracavernosal alprostadil are the drug classes linked to priapism. COMLEX: review the full medication list of any patient with a prolonged erection, since polypharmacy raises the risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — sympathetic alpha-1 tone and detumescence of the corpora cavernosa","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"None of his other medications ","C":"None of his other medications ","D":"None of his other medications ","E":"None of his other medications "}},{"id":"sdl46_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man who smokes marijuana daily presents with decreased libido and difficulty conceiving. His testosterone, LH, and FSH are all low, and semen analysis shows reduced sperm count and motility. He denies anabolic steroid use, and his testicular examination is normal. Which mechanism best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamic CB1 activation that suppresses GnRH pulses","B":"Primary testicular failure with loss of negative feedback","C":"Androgen receptor blockade in peripheral target tissues","D":"Inhibition of 5-alpha-reductase in the prostate and skin","E":"Congenital Leydig cell steroidogenic enzyme deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"THC activates CB1 receptors in the hypothalamus and suppresses GnRH pulsatility, reducing LH and FSH secretion. The downstream consequence is decreased testosterone production and impaired spermatogenesis, with reduced sperm count, decreased motility, and morphologic abnormalities. Primary testicular or Leydig cell defects would raise LH and FSH rather than lower them. 5-alpha-reductase inhibition would lower DHT without lowering LH, FSH and testosterone together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypogonadotropic hypogonadism has low LH, low FSH, and low testosterone. COMLEX: THC also impairs sperm capacitation and the acrosome reaction, so ask about recreational drug use in men with infertility or low libido.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — hormonal panels that separate primary from secondary hypogonadism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who smokes two packs of cigarettes daily has erectile dysfunction that responds poorly to tadalafil. He has no diabetes, and his testosterone level is normal. According to the course pharmacology brick, which mechanism best explains how his smoking impairs erectile function?","choices":{"A":"Nicotine stimulation of PDE5 synthesis in the corpora","B":"Reactive oxygen species that scavenge nitric oxide","C":"Hypothalamic CB1 activation that lowers GnRH pulses","D":"Cotinine antagonism of cavernosal muscarinic receptors","E":"Venous leak from fibrous plaques in the tunica"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course brick lists heavy tobacco use among factors that reduce nitric oxide bioavailability: smoking generates reactive oxygen species that scavenge NO, so less cGMP is produced in cavernous smooth muscle and erection is impaired. Because PDE5 inhibitors amplify rather than replace NO-cGMP signaling, they work poorly when NO is depleted. Nicotine is not described as inducing PDE5, CB1 suppression of GnRH is the effect of THC and would lower testosterone, cotinine is not a muscarinic antagonist, and fibrous tunical plaques cause penile curvature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: endothelial dysfunction in diabetes, hypertension, and atherosclerosis is among the most common organic causes of erectile dysfunction, which is often an early marker of cardiovascular disease. COMLEX: exercise-induced eNOS upregulation may modestly improve erectile function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 4 — NO, cGMP, and PKG signaling in penile erection","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl46_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man who smokes marijuana daily has erectile dysfunction that responds poorly to sildenafil. He has no diabetes or hypertension. Which proposed mechanism best explains the weak response?","choices":{"A":"Excess nitric oxide that exhausts cavernous cGMP stores","B":"Competitive antagonism of sildenafil by THC at PDE5","C":"Testosterone excess that desensitizes cavernous receptors","D":"Blunted eNOS activity that reduces nitric oxide bioavailability","E":"Enhanced sympathetic drive that shortens the erectile response"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The endocannabinoid system modulates nitric oxide signaling in vascular endothelium, and chronic THC exposure may blunt eNOS activity, reducing nitric oxide bioavailability. PDE5 inhibitors depend on intact NO-cGMP signaling and do not generate erections independently, so reduced nitric oxide can diminish their efficacy. Daily THC lowers testosterone rather than causing testosterone excess.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors require endogenous nitric oxide, so conditions that lower NO bioavailability weaken the response. COMLEX: THC also lowers testosterone through hypothalamic suppression, so evaluate libido and screen for recreational drug use.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 8 — penile vascular signaling","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl46_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy develops facial swelling on lisinopril. His physician switches him to valsartan to keep blocking the renin-angiotensin system. Which molecular target does valsartan block?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 angiotensin receptor","B":"Type 2 bradykinin receptor","C":"Mineralocorticoid receptor","D":"Type 1 angiotensin receptor","E":"Catalytic site of renin enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ARBs such as valsartan selectively block the type 1 angiotensin II (AT1) receptor on vascular smooth muscle, the adrenal cortex, and renal tubules. Because they do not inhibit ACE, bradykinin is still degraded. The type 2 angiotensin receptor is not the ARB target. The type 2 bradykinin receptor is not blocked by ARBs. The mineralocorticoid receptor is blocked by spironolactone and eplerenone. The renin catalytic site is blocked by aliskiren. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 46, sites of action in the RAAS cascade); Katzung Ch. 11 (angiotensin receptor blockers).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aliskiren blocks renin, ACE inhibitors block conversion of Ang I to Ang II, and ARBs block AT1 receptors. COMLEX: an ARB keeps the efferent dilation that protects diabetic kidneys without the bradykinin effects of ACE inhibitors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 2 — ACE inhibitors raise bradykinin, which causes cough and angioedema","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The type 2 angiotensin recepto"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl46_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ACE Inhibitor Clearance, Bradykinin, & Angioedema","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman started enalapril 6 weeks ago and now has a persistent dry, hacking cough. Her chest radiograph is clear, and she has no fever or wheezing. Which mechanism best explains her cough?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone-driven airway edema","B":"Angiotensin II constricting airways","C":"Histamine release from mast cells","D":"Eosinophilic airway inflammation","E":"Bradykinin sensitizing airway C-fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ACE is identical to kininase II, so ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown. Accumulated bradykinin stimulates prostaglandin production and sensitizes non-myelinated airway C-fibers, causing the characteristic dry cough. Angiotensin II constricting airways is wrong, because ACE inhibitors lower angiotensin II. Histamine release from mast cells is not the mechanism. Eosinophilic airway inflammation describes asthma. Aldosterone-driven airway edema is not a recognized effect. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 46, ACE inhibitor cough); Katzung Ch. 11 (bradykinin and substance P in ACE inhibitor cough).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor cough and angioedema come from bradykinin accumulation, and ARBs carry a lower risk. COMLEX: switch to an ARB if the cough is intolerable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 1 — ARBs block AT1 receptors and leave ACE active","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl46_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Androgen Pathway Inhibitors in BPH & Alopecia","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man takes finasteride for benign prostatic hyperplasia. His wife, who is planning a pregnancy, asks why she has been told not to handle crushed tablets. Which outcome is the concern?","choices":{"A":"Ambiguous genitalia in a male fetus","B":"Neural tube defect in any fetus","C":"Virilization of a female fetus","D":"Limb reduction defect in a fetus","E":"Cleft palate in any fetus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"5-alpha-reductase inhibitors block conversion of testosterone to DHT, which the male fetus needs to form the external genitalia, so exposure in pregnancy can cause ambiguous genitalia in a male fetus. Neural tube defects are linked to folate deficiency and some antiseizure drugs. Virilization of a female fetus would require excess androgen, not DHT blockade. Limb reduction defects are linked to thalidomide. Cleft palate is not the described risk. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 46, teratogenicity of 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors); Katzung Ch. 40 (finasteride).","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride and dutasteride are teratogenic to male fetuses because external genital development depends on DHT. COMLEX: women who are or may become pregnant should not handle crushed tablets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — DHT drives prostatic growth in BPH","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl46_q4","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — PDE5 Inhibitor Signaling & Vascular Dangers","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has erectile dysfunction and lower urinary tract symptoms from benign prostatic hyperplasia. He prefers one daily pill that treats both and allows spontaneous sexual activity. Which drug best fits?","choices":{"A":"Tadalafil","B":"Vardenafil","C":"Sildenafil","D":"Finasteride","E":"Alprostadil"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tadalafil has a long half-life (about 17.5 hours), which allows once-daily low-dose use for spontaneous sexual activity, and it is approved for BPH-related lower urinary tract symptoms. Sildenafil has a short half-life and is used on demand. Vardenafil also has a short half-life. Finasteride treats BPH but can worsen erectile function. Alprostadil is injected into the corpora and does not treat BPH symptoms. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 46, PDE5 inhibitor pharmacokinetic profiles); Katzung Ch. 12 (PDE5 inhibitors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors prolong cGMP-mediated cavernous smooth muscle relaxation, and tadalafil is long-acting and treats BPH symptoms. COMLEX: ask about nitrate use before prescribing any PDE5 inhibitor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 5 — excess pro-erectile signaling can cause ischemic priapism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl46_q5","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Pathophysiology & Pharmacology of Priapism","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man injects alprostadil into his corpora for erectile dysfunction and takes an extra dose. Five hours later he has a painful, rigid erection, and blood aspirated from the corpora shows hypoxia and acidosis. Which mechanism best explains his priapism?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-1 activation contracting arteries","B":"Venous thrombosis in the cavernosa","C":"Excess cAMP relaxing cavernous muscle","D":"Arterial fistula raising inflow","E":"Sickled cells occluding sinusoids"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Alprostadil is prostaglandin E1, which raises cAMP in cavernous smooth muscle. An overdose causes sustained relaxation that overrides sympathetic detumescence and traps deoxygenated blood, producing ischemic priapism. Alpha-1 activation contracting arteries causes detumescence, not priapism. Venous thrombosis is not the initiating mechanism of drug-induced priapism. An arterial fistula causes high-flow, non-ischemic priapism, which does not fit the hypoxic blood gas. Sickled cells occluding sinusoids cause priapism in sickle cell disease, which he does not have. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 46, excessive pro-erectile signaling in priapism); Katzung Ch. 18 (alprostadil).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic priapism lasts more than 4 hours, with hypoxic and acidotic cavernous blood, and follows alpha-1 blockade or excess cGMP or cAMP signaling. COMLEX: it is an emergency because fibrosis and lasting erectile dysfunction follow within hours.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 4 — PDE5 inhibitors preserve cGMP in cavernous smooth muscle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl46_q6","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recreational Drugs, Endothelial Health, & Male Pharmacotherapy","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man and his partner have not conceived after 14 months. He smokes cannabis heavily every day, and his semen analysis shows low sperm count and motility. In vitro testing also shows his sperm fail to penetrate the zona pellucida. Which direct effect of THC best explains this last finding?","choices":{"A":"Antisperm antibody coating","B":"Ejaculatory duct obstruction","C":"Impaired capacitation","D":"Varicocele heat injury","E":"Retrograde ejaculation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"THC binds cannabinoid receptors on mature spermatozoa and directly disrupts capacitation and the acrosome reaction, preventing zona pellucida penetration. Central effects lower GnRH, LH, FSH, and testosterone, which explains the low count. Antisperm antibody coating is an immune cause of infertility not linked to cannabis. Ejaculatory duct obstruction causes a low-volume ejaculate. Retrograde ejaculation sends semen into the bladder. Varicocele heat injury reflects dilated scrotal veins. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 46, THC effects on sperm capacitation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cannabis suppresses the HPG axis and directly impairs sperm capacitation and the acrosome reaction. COMLEX: counsel stopping cannabis in male infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — testosterone negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary","isHighYield":false},{"id":"grid_sdl46_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — RAAS Cascade Inhibition & Glomerular Hemodynamics","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension has a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 210 mg/g (reference range: <30 mg/g) on two occasions. His serum creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). He takes metformin. Lisinopril is started. Which of the following sets of changes in plasma values 2 weeks later, compared with before treatment, is most likely?","grid":{"headers":["Renin activity","Angio­tensin II","Aldos­terone","Brady­kinin"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↓","↔"],"B":["↑","↑","↓","↔"],"C":["↑","↓","↓","↑"],"D":["↑","↑","↑","↔"],"E":["↑","↑","↓","↑"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lisinopril inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme, producing the pattern (↑ renin activity, ↓ angiotensin II, ↓ aldosterone, ↑ bradykinin). Less angiotensin II is formed, so aldosterone secretion falls, and loss of angiotensin II negative feedback on the juxtaglomerular cells raises plasma renin activity. ACE is also kininase II, the enzyme that inactivates bradykinin, so bradykinin accumulates; this underlies the dry cough and angioedema of ACE inhibitors. The pattern (↓ renin activity, ↓ angiotensin II, ↓ aldosterone, ↔ bradykinin) is the direct renin inhibitor aliskiren, which suppresses plasma renin activity and does not raise bradykinin. The pattern (↑ renin activity, ↑ angiotensin II, ↓ aldosterone, ↔ bradykinin) is an angiotensin II receptor blocker such as losartan: AT₁ blockade removes the feedback on renin and lowers aldosterone, while circulating angiotensin II rises and bradykinin is unaffected. The pattern (↑ renin activity, ↑ angiotensin II, ↑ aldosterone, ↔ bradykinin) is a thiazide diuretic, whose volume contraction activates the whole renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis. The pattern (↑ renin activity, ↑ angiotensin II, ↓ aldosterone, ↑ bradykinin) is sacubitril–valsartan: valsartan acts as the angiotensin II receptor blocker does, and sacubitril inhibits neprilysin, which degrades bradykinin as well as the natriuretic peptides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: plasma renin activity rises with ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and diuretics but falls with aliskiren; of these RAAS drugs only ACE inhibitors raise bradykinin, which neprilysin inhibitors also do. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy and after any prior ACE-inhibitor angioedema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 2 — ACE (kininase II) inactivates bradykinin; its accumulation causes ACE-inhibitor cough and angioedema","isHighYield":true}],"47":[{"id":"sdl47_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"In which week of development does the metanephros, the permanent kidney, first appear?","choices":{"A":"Week 2","B":"Week 4","C":"Week 8","D":"Week 10","E":"Week 5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The metanephros, or permanent kidney, appears in week 5 of development, after the nonfunctional pronephros regresses (its indications disappear by the end of week 4) and the mesonephros serves as an interim kidney.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the three kidney systems form in a cranial-to-caudal sequence, pronephros then mesonephros then metanephros, and only the metanephros is permanent. COMLEX: the mesonephric duct persists in males to form the epididymis, ductus deferens, and seminal vesicles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the stepwise sequence of kidney development and the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling that builds the permanent kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic structure gives rise to the collecting ducts, calyces, renal pelvis, and ureter?","choices":{"A":"Metanephric blastema","B":"Ureteric bud","C":"Pronephric duct","D":"Urogenital sinus","E":"Allantois"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ureteric bud arises from the mesonephric duct and forms the collecting ducts, major and minor calyces, renal pelvis, and ureter. The metanephric blastema, or mesenchyme, instead forms the nephron from the glomerulus through the distal convoluted tubule. The urogenital sinus forms the bladder and urethra, and the allantois is obliterated into a fibrous cord, the urachus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ureteric bud makes the collecting system, and the metanephric mesenchyme makes the nephron. COMLEX: failure of bud branching or of bud-mesenchyme interaction produces renal agenesis or multicystic dysplastic kidney.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the stepwise sequence of kidney development and the reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling that builds the permanent kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":1,"stem":"Horseshoe kidney is associated with which chromosomal disorder?","choices":{"A":"Klinefelter syndrome","B":"Fragile X syndrome","C":"Turner syndrome","D":"Williams syndrome","E":"Marfan syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The SDL 47 review lists Turner syndrome (45,XO) and trisomies 13, 18, and 21 as associations of horseshoe kidney, in which the lower poles fuse across the midline and the isthmus is trapped beneath the inferior mesenteric artery during ascent. Klinefelter syndrome, fragile X syndrome, Williams syndrome, and Marfan syndrome are not listed associations of renal fusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the altered ureteral drainage angle predisposes to urinary stasis, recurrent pyelonephritis, and nephrolithiasis. COMLEX: Turner syndrome also features coarctation of the aorta and bicuspid aortic valve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — kidney ascent and fusion anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the renal filtration barrier contains the heparan sulfate that repels anionic albumin?","choices":{"A":"Fenestrated endothelium","B":"Glomerular basement membrane","C":"Podocyte slit diaphragm","D":"Mesangial cell layer","E":"Parietal epithelium of Bowman capsule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The glomerular basement membrane carries a negative charge from heparan sulfate proteoglycans, which repels negatively charged proteins such as albumin. The fenestrated endothelium lacks diaphragms and blocks blood cells by size, and the slit diaphragm adds size selectivity. Mesangial cells sit between the capillary loops and the parietal epithelium lines the outer wall of Bowman capsule, so neither is a layer of the filtration barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the three layers are fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte foot processes with slit diaphragms. COMLEX: loss of the charge barrier allows albumin into the urine in nephrotic states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the layers of the glomerular filtration barrier and segment-specific tubular histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature accounts for the intense eosinophilic staining of proximal convoluted tubule cells?","choices":{"A":"Abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Dense apical glycocalyx","C":"Stored lipid droplets","D":"Numerous basal mitochondria","E":"Prominent lysosomal granules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The SDL 47 review describes proximal convoluted tubule cells as tall cuboidal cells with a dense PAS-positive brush border, packed with basal mitochondria that give intense eosinophilia and generate ATP for the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase. Abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum produces basophilia rather than eosinophilia, the apical glycocalyx of the brush border is what stains with PAS, and lipid droplets and lysosomes are not the source of the eosinophilic staining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal tubule reabsorbs about 67 percent of filtered water, Na+, and Cl- and all filtered glucose and amino acids. COMLEX: its high metabolic load makes it the primary site of ischemic tubular necrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — segment-specific tubular histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":1,"stem":"Ischemic acute tubular necrosis after prolonged hypotension preferentially injures which nephron segment?","choices":{"A":"Cortical collecting duct","B":"Thin descending limb","C":"Proximal tubule","D":"Glomerulus","E":"Bowman capsule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs 67 percent of filtered water, sodium, and chloride and essentially all glucose and amino acids, so its high metabolic load makes it the primary site of ischemic tubular necrosis after hypotension or shock. Robbins adds that in the ischemic pattern the proximal straight tubule and the ascending limbs of the loop of Henle are the most vulnerable segments, whereas the lumens of the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct contain casts rather than the main injury. The thin descending limb is not among the vulnerable segments, and the glomerulus and Bowman capsule are not the site of tubular necrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in ischemic acute tubular injury the proximal straight tubule and the thick ascending limb are the most vulnerable segments, and toxic injury is most obvious in the proximal convoluted tubule. COMLEX: contrast dye and aminoglycosides are typical toxic triggers, and hypotension or shock is the typical ischemic trigger.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 and 2 — the layers of the glomerular filtration barrier and segment-specific tubular histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins adds that in the ische"}},{"id":"sdl47_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy volunteer has an inulin clearance of 150 mL/min and a para-aminohippuric acid clearance of 600 mL/min. What is his filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"About 0.10","B":"About 0.15","C":"About 0.20","D":"About 0.25","E":"About 0.40"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Filtration fraction is GFR divided by renal plasma flow. Inulin clearance measures GFR (150 mL/min), and PAH clearance measures effective renal plasma flow (600 mL/min), so the filtration fraction is 150 / 600 = 0.25, above the usual value of about 0.20. Values of 0.10 to 0.20 would require a lower GFR or a higher plasma flow, and 0.40 overstates the ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: filtration fraction rises with efferent arteriolar constriction and falls when plasma protein concentration or Bowman space pressure rises. COMLEX: renal blood flow equals renal plasma flow divided by (1 minus hematocrit).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — quantitative hemodynamic metrics","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Values of 0.10 to 0.20 would r"}},{"id":"sdl47_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":1,"stem":"A patient's GFR falls abruptly to 50 percent of normal and then stays there, while his creatinine production is unchanged. Once a new steady state is reached, how does his plasma creatinine compare with its original value?","choices":{"A":"About twice normal","B":"About half normal","C":"Unchanged from normal","D":"About four times normal","E":"About 1.25 times normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton explains that when GFR falls, creatinine excretion drops transiently and plasma creatinine rises until the filtered load (plasma creatinine × GFR) again equals daily production; with production constant, a 50 percent fall in GFR doubles plasma creatinine at steady state. The relationship is inverse, so plasma creatinine does not fall or stay unchanged, a 1.25-fold rise would follow a much smaller loss of GFR, and a fourfold rise would require GFR to fall to about 25 percent of normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: creatinine clearance slightly overestimates GFR because a small amount of creatinine is secreted by the proximal tubule. COMLEX: once the new steady state is reached, urinary creatinine excretion again equals production even though plasma creatinine stays high.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — inulin and creatinine as markers of GFR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The relationship is inverse, s","C":"The relationship is inverse, s","D":"The relationship is inverse, s"}},{"id":"sdl47_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":1,"stem":"What is the normal filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 60 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The filtration fraction is GFR divided by renal plasma flow, and it is normally about 0.20, or 20 percent. The other 80 percent of plasma flow is not filtered and leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arterioles to become peritubular capillary blood flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FF equals GFR over RPF and is normally about 20 percent. COMLEX: efferent constriction raises the filtration fraction, and afferent constriction leaves it about unchanged.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3, SDL 8 -> Obj 4, and SDL 9 -> Obj 4 — clearance-based measures of renal function and how arteriolar resistance changes reshape glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":1,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with untreated primary adrenal insufficiency has hyposmotic volume contraction from renal NaCl loss. According to Costanzo, how do her plasma protein concentration and hematocrit change?","choices":{"A":"Protein down, hematocrit down","B":"Protein up, hematocrit unchanged","C":"Protein up, hematocrit up","D":"Protein down, hematocrit unchanged","E":"Protein up, hematocrit down"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In hyposmotic volume contraction, NaCl is lost in excess of water, ECF osmolarity falls, and water shifts into cells, so ECF volume falls and ICF volume rises. Costanzo explains that both plasma protein concentration and hematocrit increase: protein is concentrated in the smaller ECF volume, and hematocrit rises both from the smaller ECF volume and because water entering red cells increases their volume. A rise in protein with an unchanged hematocrit is the hyperosmotic contraction pattern of sweating or water deprivation, and falling protein values accompany volume expansion states.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in isosmotic contraction from diarrhea, protein and hematocrit also rise, from hemoconcentration without any water shift. COMLEX: aldosterone deficiency causes the renal NaCl loss that gives adrenal insufficiency its hyposmotic pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — clinical volume contraction states","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A rise in protein with an unch","D":"A rise in protein with an unch"}},{"id":"sdl47_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":1,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with psychogenic polydipsia drinks large volumes of water, and her serum Na+ falls to 126 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Which change in her intracellular fluid is expected at steady state?","choices":{"A":"ICF volume up, ICF osmolarity down","B":"ICF volume down, ICF osmolarity up","C":"ICF volume and osmolarity unchanged","D":"ICF volume up, ICF osmolarity unchanged","E":"ICF volume down, ICF osmolarity down"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess water lowers ECF osmolarity, so water moves down its osmotic gradient into cells until intracellular osmolarity falls to match; ICF volume therefore rises and ICF osmolarity falls while the ECF also expands. The SDL 47 review lists D5W infusion, psychogenic polydipsia, and SIADH as causes. A fall in ICF volume with a rise in osmolarity is the hyperosmotic pattern, an unchanged ICF follows isosmotic disturbances, and a water gain cannot raise cell volume without diluting the cell or shrink the cell while diluting it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the SDL 47 review states that hematocrit decreases from hemodilution in hyposmotic expansion, whereas Costanzo lists hematocrit as unchanged. COMLEX: both sources agree that ECF osmolarity and plasma protein concentration fall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — clinical volume expansion states","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A fall in ICF volume with a ri","E":"A fall in ICF volume with a ri"}},{"id":"sdl47_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":1,"stem":"Which volume disturbance follows an infusion of normal saline?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion","B":"Hyposmotic volume expansion","C":"Isosmotic volume contraction","D":"Hyposmotic volume contraction","E":"Isosmotic volume expansion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Isotonic saline is added entirely to the ECF and does not change ECF osmolarity, so no water shifts between compartments. ECF volume increases while ICF volume and osmolarity stay unchanged, and hematocrit and plasma protein concentration fall by dilution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: isotonic fluid stays in the ECF and expands it without changing osmolarity. COMLEX: hyposmotic expansion occurs with SIADH and hyperosmotic expansion with high salt intake.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 2 and 3 — isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic volume contraction and expansion and the resulting water shifts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"According to the SDL 47 review, Winter's formula should be used to judge respiratory compensation in which primary acid-base disorder?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic alkalosis","B":"Metabolic acidosis","C":"Acute respiratory acidosis","D":"Chronic respiratory acidosis","E":"Respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The SDL 47 review lists the scope of Winter's formula as a board trap: it calculates expected respiratory compensation only during a primary metabolic acidosis, predicting PCO2 as 1.5 times serum HCO3- plus 8, plus or minus 2. A measured PCO2 above the predicted range signals a concomitant respiratory acidosis, and one below it signals a concomitant respiratory alkalosis. Metabolic alkalosis and the primary respiratory disorders are assessed with their own compensation rules rather than Winter's formula.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the delta ratio, (anion gap minus 12) divided by (24 minus HCO3-), screens for a second metabolic disorder. COMLEX: a measured PCO2 within 2 mm Hg of the prediction indicates appropriate compensation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — calculating expected compensation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Metabolic alkalosis and the pr","D":"Metabolic alkalosis and the pr","E":"Metabolic alkalosis and the pr"}},{"id":"sdl47_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"Which expression calculates the serum anion gap?","choices":{"A":"Sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate","B":"Sodium plus chloride minus bicarbonate","C":"Chloride minus the sum of sodium and bicarbonate","D":"Bicarbonate minus the sum of sodium and chloride","E":"Sodium plus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anion gap equals the sodium concentration minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate concentrations. It represents unmeasured anions, so it rises when an organic anion such as a ketoacid or lactate replaces bicarbonate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an elevated gap points to ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, salicylate, methanol, ethylene glycol, or renal failure, while a normal gap points to bicarbonate loss. COMLEX: calculate the gap first in any metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 and 3 — high versus normal anion gap acidoses and the formulas for expected compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"Which condition produces a metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap?","choices":{"A":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","B":"Methanol poisoning","C":"Profuse diarrhea","D":"Salicylate poisoning","E":"Lactic acidosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diarrhea causes gastrointestinal loss of bicarbonate, and chloride replaces it, giving a hyperchloremic acidosis with a normal anion gap. Diabetic ketoacidosis, methanol poisoning, salicylate poisoning, and lactic acidosis each add an unmeasured anion and raise the gap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea and renal tubular acidoses cause normal anion gap acidosis with hyperchloremia. COMLEX: use the anion gap to separate these from the causes of high gap acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 and 3 — high versus normal anion gap acidoses and the formulas for expected compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic fibers from spinal levels T10 to T11 supply which organs?","choices":{"A":"Heart and great vessels","B":"Stomach and gallbladder","C":"Prostate and lower gut","D":"Lungs and bronchi","E":"Kidney and upper ureter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The kidney and upper ureter receive sympathetic input from T10 to T11, which causes vasoconstriction and altered GFR. T1 to T4 corresponds to cardiac, T5 to T9 to the upper gastrointestinal organs (stomach, liver, gallbladder), and T12 to L2 to the prostate and lower gut. The course review assigns T10 to T11 to the kidney and upper ureter, not to the lungs and bronchi.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal sympathetic tone alters vascular resistance and GFR. COMLEX: somatic dysfunction at T10 to T11 supports a renal origin, so the level helps separate renal disease from other visceral sources.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 6 — mapping autonomic innervation levels to the kidneys, ureters, bladder, prostate, and gonads","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"T1 to T4 corresponds to cardia","D":"The course review assigns T10 "}},{"id":"sdl47_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve provides the parasympathetic supply to the kidney and upper ureter?","choices":{"A":"Pudendal nerve","B":"Hypogastric nerve","C":"Phrenic nerve","D":"Vagus nerve","E":"Genitofemoral nerve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The parasympathetic supply of the kidney and upper ureter comes from the vagus nerve, cranial nerve X. The bladder, lower ureter, and prostate instead receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus supplies parasympathetic fibers to the kidney, whereas pelvic organs use S2 to S4. COMLEX: in the OPP convention of the review guide, the testes and ovaries also receive vagal parasympathetic input with T10 to T11 sympathetic input.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 -> Obj 6 — mapping autonomic innervation levels to the kidneys, ureters, bladder, prostate, and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":1,"stem":"According to the SDL 47 review, sympathetic innervation of the prostate arises from which spinal levels?","choices":{"A":"T12 to L2","B":"T1 to T4","C":"T5 to T9","D":"T10 to T11","E":"S2 to S4"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The SDL 47 OPP summary maps prostate sympathetics to T12 to L2, with parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4. In the same review, T1 to T4 represents cardiac viscera, T5 to T9 the stomach, liver, and gallbladder, and T10 to T11 the kidney, upper ureter, and gonads, while S2 to S4 carries parasympathetic rather than sympathetic fibers to the pelvic organs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bladder sympathetics arise from T11 to L2, and its parasympathetics travel in the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4. COMLEX: somatic dysfunction at T10 to T11 is the level that points to a renal parenchymal origin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 6 — viscerosomatic levels of the urinary and genital organs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":2,"stem":"A term newborn has a flattened nose, low-set ears, a receding chin, and bilateral clubbed feet. He remains cyanotic and cannot be adequately ventilated despite intubation. Prenatal ultrasound showed severely reduced amniotic fluid and no visible bladder. Which mechanism best explains his respiratory failure?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant deficiency from delivery well before the due date","B":"Aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid during delivery","C":"Tracheoesophageal fistula with aspiration of gastric contents","D":"Absent fetal urine that left no fluid to stent developing airways","E":"Ascending intrauterine infection with congenital pneumonia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bilateral renal agenesis, ARPKD, or posterior urethral valves prevent fetal urine excretion, causing severe oligohydramnios. Fetal compression produces the characteristic facies and limb deformities, and without inhaled amniotic fluid to stent the airways, alveolar expansion halts, so pulmonary hypoplasia is the main cause of neonatal death. Prematurity with surfactant deficiency, tracheoesophageal fistula and congenital pneumonia do not explain oligohydramnios with an absent bladder and compression deformities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Potter sequence is caused by oligohydramnios from absent fetal urine and is fatal because of pulmonary hypoplasia. COMLEX: fetal renal failure or obstruction, including maternal ACE inhibitor use, can trigger it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — Potter sequence in bilateral renal agenesis and CAKUT","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":2,"stem":"A boy is born at 29 weeks' gestation. His parents ask whether his kidneys had finished forming nephrons before delivery. According to the SDL 47 review, which statement is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Functional nephrogenesis was still under way at birth","B":"Nephrogenesis ended when the metanephros appeared in week 5","C":"All of his nephrons were complete by the end of week 12","D":"His mesonephros was still serving as his main kidney","E":"His nephrons will arise from the ureteric bud after birth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The SDL 47 review states that the metanephros appears in week 5 as the permanent kidney and that functional nephrogenesis continues through week 36 of gestation, so an infant born at 29 weeks arrives before nephron formation is complete. Week 5 marks the start of the metanephros rather than the end of nephrogenesis, nephron formation is not finished by week 12 even though the metanephros has begun to produce urine, the mesonephros serves only as the interim first-trimester kidney, and nephrons arise from the metanephric mesenchyme rather than the ureteric bud.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ureteric bud forms the collecting ducts, calyces, pelvis, and ureter, while the metanephric blastema forms the glomerulus through the distal convoluted tubule. COMLEX: the pronephros appears in week 4, never functions, and regresses completely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — timing of the pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Week 5 marks the start of the "}},{"id":"sdl47_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has leaked urine continuously since toilet training, soaking her underwear all day, yet she also voids normally at regular intervals. Ultrasound shows a duplicated collecting system on one side. Which embryologic mechanism best explains her incontinence?","choices":{"A":"Failure of the allantoic lumen to obliterate into the median umbilical ligament","B":"Accessory ureteric bud carried caudally to open below the sphincter","C":"Incomplete urorectal septum partitioning of the cloaca","D":"Failure of ventral body wall closure exposing the bladder","E":"Loss of coordinated bladder emptying from a spinal cord lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A second ureteric bud can move caudally with the mesonephric duct and open at a low, abnormal site. In girls the ectopic ureter can drain into the urethra, vestibule, or vagina below the external urethral sphincter, so urine drips continuously while normal periodic voiding from the bladder persists. A persistent allantoic lumen causes a urachal fistula draining at the umbilicus, and faulty urorectal septation causes anorectal malformations, not continuous dribbling with normal voiding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an ectopic ureter arises from a duplicated ureteric bud that inserts abnormally low, and the upper pole ureter is the one that is ectopic. COMLEX: continuous dribbling with normal voiding in a girl is the classic clue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — congenital lower urinary tract anomalies and the anatomic basis of their presentations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A persistent allantoic lumen c"}},{"id":"sdl47_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn of Finnish ancestry develops massive edema and proteinuria in the first weeks of life. Kidney biopsy shows a minimal change disease–like glomerulopathy with extensive podocyte foot process effacement. A mutation in the gene encoding which protein is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Podocin","B":"Alpha-actinin 4","C":"Nephrin","D":"Polycystin-1","E":"Fibrocystin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins notes that NPHS1 encodes nephrin, a key slit diaphragm protein, and that its mutations produce congenital nephrotic syndrome of the Finnish type, a minimal change disease–like glomerulopathy with extensive foot process effacement. NPHS2 mutations in podocin cause autosomal recessive, steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome of childhood onset, alpha-actinin 4 mutations cause some autosomal dominant FSGS, polycystin-1 is mutated in adult polycystic kidney disease, and fibrocystin is the PKHD1 product mutated in the recessive form.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrin molecules from neighboring foot processes dimerize across the filtration slit and link through podocin and CD2AP to the actin cytoskeleton. COMLEX: loss of slit diaphragms is a key event in the proteinuria of most forms of glomerular injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — primary nephrotic podocytopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man dies 3 days after septic shock with prolonged hypotension. Renal cortical sections at autopsy show necrosis of tubule profiles with intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm and sloughed cells, while adjacent profiles with lower cuboidal cells and open lumens are spared. Why are the injured cells especially vulnerable to ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Sparse mitochondria that limit oxidative capacity","B":"High ATP demand from bulk reabsorption of filtered solute","C":"Low reabsorptive workload that leaves reserves untrained","D":"Direct exposure to circulating antibodies in the lumen","E":"Lack of a brush border that exposes the apical membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The injured segment is the proximal tubule, packed with basal mitochondria that supply ATP for Na+/K+-ATPase as it reabsorbs 67 percent of filtered water, sodium, and chloride plus essentially all glucose and amino acids. Robbins notes that tubular epithelial cells are particularly sensitive to ischemia because of their high rate of metabolism and oxygen consumption, and this high metabolic load is why the proximal tubule is the primary site of ischemic acute tubular necrosis. The spared distal convoluted tubule has lower cuboidal cells, lacks a brush border, and does less bulk reabsorption. Antibodies play no part in ischemic tubular injury, which follows hypoperfusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proximal tubular epithelium is highly energy dependent and therefore highly sensitive to hypoxia. COMLEX: hypotension or shock is the typical ischemic trigger, and contrast dye or aminoglycosides are typical toxic triggers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — segment-specific epithelial features of the proximal versus distal convoluted tubule under light microscopy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Antibodies play no part in isc"}},{"id":"sdl47_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman loses about 1.2 L of blood from a postpartum hemorrhage, and sodium and chloride delivery to her macula densa falls. Which cell type responds by releasing renin?","choices":{"A":"Macula densa cells","B":"Alpha-intercalated cells","C":"Principal cells","D":"Proximal tubule cells","E":"Juxtaglomerular granular cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The macula densa are chemoreceptor cells in the early distal tubule that sense luminal sodium and chloride. The juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that contain renin granules, so they are the source of renin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renin comes from juxtaglomerular cells in the afferent arteriole, and the macula densa is the sensor. COMLEX: the macula densa senses luminal sodium and chloride and signals the juxtaglomerular cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — the macula densa as a luminal solute sensor in tubuloglomerular feedback","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with multiple myeloma has a markedly elevated total serum protein concentration from a circulating paraprotein. Considering only the effect of the raised plasma protein concentration on glomerular Starling forces, which set of changes in GFR, renal plasma flow, and filtration fraction is expected?","choices":{"A":"GFR up, RPF unchanged, FF up","B":"GFR down, RPF down, FF unchanged","C":"GFR down, RPF up, FF down","D":"GFR down, RPF unchanged, FF down","E":"GFR up, RPF down, FF up"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The SDL 47 review notes that increased plasma protein concentration, as in dehydration or multiple myeloma, raises glomerular capillary oncotic pressure, which opposes filtration. GFR falls while renal plasma flow is unchanged, so filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) decreases. A rise in GFR and FF follows a fall in plasma protein, proportional falls in GFR and RPF with unchanged FF follow afferent constriction, a falling GFR with a rising RPF follows efferent dilation by an ARB, and a rising GFR with a falling RPF follows efferent constriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ureteral obstruction gives the same pattern by raising Bowman space hydrostatic pressure. COMLEX: in myeloma the kidney is also injured by Bence-Jones light-chain cast nephropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 1 — Starling forces and glomerular hemodynamics","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A rise in GFR and FF follows a","B":"A rise in GFR and FF follows a","C":"A rise in GFR and FF follows a","E":"A rise in GFR and FF follows a"}},{"id":"sdl47_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":2,"stem":"An investigational organic anion, drug X, is freely filtered and not bound to plasma proteins. In a volunteer with a GFR of 120 mL/min, the plasma drug X concentration is 0.02 mg/mL, the urine drug X concentration is 12 mg/mL, and urine flow is 1.0 mL/min. What is the net rate of tubular secretion of drug X?","choices":{"A":"2.4 mg/min","B":"12.0 mg/min","C":"9.6 mg/min","D":"14.4 mg/min","E":"4.8 mg/min"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Net secretion equals the excretion rate minus the filtered load. The filtered load is GFR × plasma concentration = 120 mL/min × 0.02 mg/mL = 2.4 mg/min, and the excretion rate is urine concentration × urine flow = 12 mg/mL × 1.0 mL/min = 12 mg/min, so net secretion is 12 − 2.4 = 9.6 mg/min. The value 2.4 mg/min is the filtered load, 12.0 mg/min is the excretion rate, 14.4 mg/min adds the two, and 4.8 mg/min doubles the filtered load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Costanzo applies the same subtraction to PAH, concluding that an excretion rate greater than the filtered load proves net secretion. COMLEX: the clearance of drug X is 600 mL/min, five times the GFR, which also indicates net secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — deducing tubular handling from clearance data","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The value 2.4 mg/min is the fi"}},{"id":"sdl47_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":2,"stem":"During an inulin infusion study, a 26-year-old healthy man has a plasma inulin concentration of 1 mg/mL. At a urine flow of 1 mL/min his urine inulin concentration is 120 mg/mL. After he drinks a large water load, urine flow rises to 2 mL/min and urine inulin concentration falls to 60 mg/mL. What is his inulin clearance during the water diuresis?","choices":{"A":"120 mL/min","B":"240 mL/min","C":"60 mL/min","D":"30 mL/min","E":"180 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration. Before the water load it is 120 times 1 divided by 1, and during diuresis it is 60 times 2 divided by 1, so both equal 120 mL/min. Urine flow doubles, but urine inulin concentration falls in proportion, so inulin clearance, which measures GFR, does not depend on urine flow rate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diuresis changes urine flow and urine concentration in opposite directions, leaving clearance of a freely filtered marker unchanged. COMLEX: GFR is a property of the glomeruli, not of urine flow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — how changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect clearance of glomerular markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man collapses at the end of a marathon after losing a large volume of sweat. He is hypotensive and tachycardic, his plasma protein concentration and osmolarity are elevated, and his hematocrit is the same as before the race. Which mechanism best explains the unchanged hematocrit?","choices":{"A":"Isosmotic loss of ECF spares red cell concentration and volume","B":"Fluid loss concentrates red cells while high ECF osmolarity shrinks them","C":"Water entry into red cells from low ECF osmolarity offsets fluid loss","D":"Sweat is hyposmotic, so the drop in ECF osmolarity dilutes red cells","E":"Red cell mass falls through hemolysis in proportion to fluid loss"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sweat is hyposmotic, so its loss lowers ECF volume and raises ECF osmolarity, which draws water out of cells. Loss of ECF fluid alone would raise the hematocrit, but the osmotic shift shrinks the red cells, and the two opposite effects offset each other so the hematocrit is unchanged while plasma protein concentration rises.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic volume contraction has a lower ECF volume, a lower ICF volume, a higher osmolarity, and an unchanged hematocrit. COMLEX: contrast this with diarrhea, in which the hematocrit rises.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — how isosmotic, hyperosmotic, and hyposmotic contraction alter ECF and ICF volume, plasma protein, and hematocrit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with psychogenic polydipsia drinks about 6 liters of water over a few hours and develops headache and confusion. Her plasma sodium concentration is 122 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L). Which type of volume disturbance does she have?","choices":{"A":"Hyperosmotic volume expansion","B":"Isosmotic volume expansion","C":"Hyposmotic volume contraction","D":"Hyperosmotic volume contraction","E":"Hyposmotic volume expansion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pure water added to the ECF lowers ECF osmolarity, so water shifts down its gradient into cells. Both ECF and ICF volumes increase, in proportion to their original sizes, and osmolarity falls in both compartments, which is hyposmotic volume expansion, as in SIADH. Plasma protein concentration falls by dilution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: water excess gives hyposmotic volume expansion with a higher ECF volume, a higher ICF volume, and a lower osmolarity. COMLEX: the same pattern occurs with SIADH and with excess D5W infusion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — clinical volume expansion states and their effects on ECF and ICF volume and osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has an ICF volume of 28 L, an ECF volume of 14 L, and a plasma osmolarity of 290 mOsm/L. While stranded without fresh water, he eats salted food containing 200 mmol of NaCl, all of which enters the ECF. Assume the osmotic coefficient of NaCl is 2 and that he takes in no water and makes no urine during equilibration. What is his ECF volume in the new steady state?","choices":{"A":"14.0 L","B":"13.1 L","C":"15.4 L","D":"14.9 L","E":"16.4 L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The added solute is 200 mmol times 2, or 400 mOsm. With no water taken in or excreted, total body water stays at 42 L, so new osmolarity is (290 times 42 plus 400) divided by 42, about 299.5 mOsm/L. New ECF volume is (290 times 14 plus 400) divided by 299.5, about 14.9 L, so roughly 0.9 L of water moved from the ICF to the ECF. This is hyperosmotic volume expansion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high NaCl intake raises ECF osmolarity, pulls water out of cells, and expands the ECF. COMLEX: solve these by conserving osmoles, using the new common osmolarity to find the new ECF volume.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — hyperosmotic volume expansion from high sodium intake and the osmole-based calculation of water shifts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with type 1 diabetes is lethargic with shallow breathing. Glucose is 610 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and urine ketones are positive. Arterial pH is 7.12 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PCO2 is 38 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), and serum HCO3- is 12 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Using Winter's formula, which best describes his acid-base status?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory acidosis","B":"Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation","C":"Metabolic acidosis with concomitant respiratory alkalosis","D":"Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","E":"Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The low pH and low bicarbonate indicate metabolic acidosis from ketoacidosis. Winter's formula predicts PCO2 as 1.5 times 12 plus 8, which is 26 with a range of 24 to 28 mm Hg. His measured PCO2 of 38 is above the predicted range, so the lungs are not compensating adequately and a concomitant respiratory acidosis is present.","boardPrep":"Step 1: measured PCO2 above the Winter's prediction means a concomitant respiratory acidosis. COMLEX: inadequate ventilation in a severe metabolic acidosis is a warning sign of impending respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — applying Winter's formula to judge whether respiratory compensation is appropriate","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"His measured PCO2 of 38 is abo"}},{"id":"sdl47_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has 3 days of vomiting and polyuria. Glucose is 550 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and ketones are positive. Serum Na+ is 142 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), Cl- is 92 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- is 18 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and her PCO2 matches the value predicted by Winter's formula. Using delta ratio = (anion gap minus 12) divided by (24 minus HCO3-), which best describes her metabolic status?","choices":{"A":"Isolated high anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","B":"High anion gap acidosis with a concurrent normal anion gap acidosis","C":"High anion gap acidosis with a concurrent metabolic alkalosis","D":"Isolated normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","E":"Primary respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Her anion gap is 142 minus (92 plus 18), or 32 mEq/L, so she has a high anion gap acidosis. The delta ratio is (32 minus 12) divided by (24 minus 18), or 20 divided by 6, about 3.3. A ratio above 2 means the bicarbonate fell far less than the gap rose, indicating a coexisting metabolic alkalosis, here from vomiting; in Costanzo's terms the fall in HCO3 is less than the rise in the anion gap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: with the delta ratio defined as in the question, 1.0 to 2.0 indicates a pure high anion gap acidosis, above 2.0 a coexisting metabolic alkalosis or a preexisting chronic respiratory acidosis, and a low ratio, about 0.4 to 0.8 in the SDL 47 guide (below 1.0 in the Exam 1 notes), a combined high and normal gap acidosis. COMLEX: DKA with vomiting is the classic example of the high ratio.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — calculating expected compensation and the delta ratio to find a hidden second metabolic disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman who is malnourished has taken large daily doses of acetaminophen for chronic back pain for months. She now has fatigue and rapid breathing. HCO3- is 12 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and Cl- 102 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). Serum lactate, ketones, salicylate, and creatinine are normal, and there is no osmolar gap. Accumulation of which acid best explains her disorder?","choices":{"A":"Glycolic acid","B":"Formic acid","C":"D-lactic acid","D":"Beta-hydroxybutyric acid","E":"5-Oxoproline"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Her anion gap is 138 − (102 + 12) = 24 mEq/L, a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. In the GOLDMARK mnemonic, the O stands for oxoproline from chronic acetaminophen use, which fits a malnourished patient taking large daily doses once lactate, ketones, salicylate, and renal failure are excluded. Glycolic acid from ethylene glycol and formic acid from methanol would come with an osmolar gap, D-lactic acid is exogenous or bacterial lactate, and beta-hydroxybutyric acid would appear as ketones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GOLDMARK stands for glycols, oxoproline, L-lactate, D-lactate, methanol, aspirin, renal failure, and ketones. COMLEX: the SDL 47 review's GOLD MARK list also includes rifampin and isoniazid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — causes of high anion gap metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has right flank discomfort. On osteopathic examination there is somatic dysfunction at T10 to T11, tenderness about 1 inch superior and 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus, and a posterior tender point between the T12 and L1 spinous and transverse processes. Which organ is most likely the source of the viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Kidney","B":"Stomach","C":"Bladder","D":"Prostate","E":"Heart"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The kidney and upper ureter have sympathetic input at T10 to T11, an anterior Chapman point 1 inch superior and 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus, and a posterior Chapman point between the T12 and L1 spinous and transverse processes. The stomach maps to T5 to T9 and the heart to T1 to T4, while the bladder and prostate have lower sympathetic levels and different Chapman points.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal sympathetic tone alters vascular resistance and GFR. COMLEX: T10 to T11 dysfunction plus the kidney Chapman points supports a renal parenchymal source of the reflex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 6 — mapping sympathetic levels and Chapman reflex points to the kidney, ureter, bladder, prostate, and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with urinary hesitancy has somatic dysfunction at T12 to L2. Tenderness is found at the lateral margin of the pubic symphysis anteriorly and between the PSIS and the L5 transverse process posteriorly. Which organ is the most likely source of the viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Testis","B":"Prostate","C":"Stomach","D":"Urinary bladder","E":"Upper ureter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The prostate has sympathetic input from T12 to L2, parasympathetic input from the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4, an anterior Chapman point at the lateral margin of the pubic symphysis, and a posterior point between the PSIS and the L5 transverse process. The kidney and testis are T10 to T11 organs, and the stomach maps to T5 to T9. The urinary bladder also has sympathetic input in the T11 to L2 range, but its Chapman points are periumbilical and at the superior border of the pubic symphysis anteriorly and at the upper edge of the L2 transverse process posteriorly, which do not match the findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pelvic organs use S2 to S4 parasympathetic outflow. COMLEX: matching the somatic level and the Chapman points to the organ is how these viscerosomatic questions are solved.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 6 — matching somatic dysfunction levels and Chapman reflex points to the organ of origin, from kidney to prostate and gonads","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has flank discomfort. Palpation shows somatic dysfunction at T5 to T9 with no findings at T10 to T11. Which origin of his discomfort is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Renal parenchymal disease","B":"Upper ureteral obstruction","C":"Stomach or liver disease","D":"Testicular vascular disease","E":"Coronary artery disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"T5 to T9 represents the upper gastrointestinal organs, including the stomach, liver, and gallbladder. Findings at T10 to T11 must be present to confirm a renal parenchymal origin, and the upper ureter and testis share that level, whereas cardiac disease maps to T1 to T4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: viscerosomatic levels distinguish organ systems, with T1 to T4 cardiac, T5 to T9 upper gastrointestinal, and T10 to T11 renal. COMLEX: absence of T10 to T11 findings argues against renal parenchymal origin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 6 — using the level of somatic dysfunction to separate cardiac, upper gastrointestinal, renal, and pelvic sources","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Findings at T10 to T11 must be","E":"Findings at T10 to T11 must be"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl47_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Renal Organogenesis, Induction & Congenital Anomalies","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has recurrent febrile urinary tract infections. Imaging shows a duplicated collecting system on the left with two ureters. Which complication typifies the ureter draining the lower pole?","choices":{"A":"Megaureter from atresia","B":"Ureterocele obstruction","C":"Ectopic urethral insertion","D":"Ureteropelvic stenosis","E":"Vesicoureteral reflux"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By the Weigert-Meyer law, the ureter draining the lower pole inserts in the normal position but is prone to vesicoureteral reflux, whereas the upper-pole ureter inserts lower and is prone to obstruction. Ureterocele obstruction is the upper-pole pattern. Ectopic urethral insertion is also the upper-pole pattern and causes continuous dribbling in girls. Ureteropelvic stenosis is a separate cause of hydronephrosis. Megaureter from atresia is not the lower-pole pattern. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 47, ectopic ureter and Weigert-Meyer law).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Weigert-Meyer, the upper-pole ureter is obstructed or ectopic, and the lower-pole ureter refluxes. COMLEX: febrile UTIs in a child warrant evaluation for reflux.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 2 — upper-pole obstruction, by contrast, causes hydronephrosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl47_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Glomerular Barrier & Tubular Histology","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has hypotension for 2 hours during surgery and then develops oliguria and muddy brown casts. Biopsy shows the most severe necrosis in the straight portion of the proximal tubule. Which factor best explains why this segment is most vulnerable?","choices":{"A":"Low oxygen tension in outer medulla","B":"High filtered protein load in cortex","C":"Absent brush border in the segment","D":"Low mitochondrial content of the cells","E":"Direct exposure to concentrated urine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The straight proximal tubule (S3) lies in the outer medulla, which has low oxygen tension at baseline because of countercurrent vasa recta flow, and its cells have high ATP demand, so ischemia injures it first. High filtered protein load in the cortex does not explain medullary vulnerability. An absent brush border is wrong, because proximal cells have a prominent brush border. Low mitochondrial content is wrong, because proximal cells are packed with mitochondria. Direct exposure to concentrated urine is not the cause of ischemic injury. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 47, PCT vulnerability to ischemic ATN); Robbins Ch. 20 (acute tubular injury).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the S3 segment and the medullary thick ascending limb are most vulnerable to ischemia because of high ATP demand in a hypoxic outer medulla. COMLEX: muddy brown casts after hypotension suggest ischemic ATN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 4 — ATN phases are initiation, maintenance, and recovery","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl47_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clearance, GFR Markers & Filtration Fraction","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with hypertension starts losartan. Considering only its effect on the efferent arteriole, which change in glomerular hemodynamics is expected?","choices":{"A":"GFR up, RPF down, FF up","B":"GFR down, RPF same, FF down","C":"GFR same, RPF up, FF up","D":"GFR up, RPF up, FF same","E":"GFR down, RPF up, FF down"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Losartan blocks angiotensin II-mediated efferent constriction, so the efferent arteriole dilates. Glomerular capillary pressure falls, lowering GFR, while outflow resistance falls and renal plasma flow rises, so filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) falls. GFR up, RPF down, FF up is the pattern of efferent constriction by angiotensin II. GFR down with unchanged RPF fits higher plasma protein or ureteral obstruction. GFR up, RPF up, FF same fits afferent dilation. GFR unchanged with RPF and FF both up is not internally consistent. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 47, hemodynamic shifts from arteriolar manipulation); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (effects of arteriolar constriction on GFR and RPF).","boardPrep":"Step 1: efferent dilation (ACE inhibitors, ARBs) lowers GFR, raises RPF, and lowers FF; efferent constriction does the reverse. COMLEX: a small creatinine rise after starting an ARB is expected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 1 — ARBs dilate the efferent arteriole","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"GFR up, RPF down, FF up is the","B":"GFR down with unchanged RPF fi","C":"GFR unchanged with RPF and FF ","D":"GFR up, RPF up, FF same fits a"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl47_q4","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Darrow-Yannet Fluid Compartment Shifts","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought in after his caregiver mistakenly used salt instead of sugar when preparing several of his feedings. Serum Na+ is 162 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Assume steady state before any renal correction. Which pattern of body fluid change is expected?","choices":{"A":"ECF up, ICF up, osmolarity down","B":"ECF down, ICF up, osmolarity down","C":"ECF up, ICF down, osmolarity up","D":"ECF up, ICF unchanged, osmolarity unchanged","E":"ECF down, ICF down, osmolarity up"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo classifies high NaCl intake as hyperosmotic volume expansion: the added solute stays in the ECF and raises its osmolarity, so water shifts out of cells. ECF volume rises while ICF volume falls, and at steady state both compartments share the same higher osmolarity. Both volumes rising with low osmolarity is hyposmotic expansion as in SIADH, a falling ECF with a rising ICF is hyposmotic contraction as in adrenal insufficiency, an isolated ECF rise is isotonic saline infusion, and both volumes falling with high osmolarity is hyperosmotic contraction as in water deprivation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Costanzo lists hematocrit and plasma protein concentration as decreased in hyperosmotic volume expansion. COMLEX: in every Darrow-Yannet steady state, ECF and ICF osmolarities end up equal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — clinical volume expansion states","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Both volumes rising with low o","B":"Both volumes rising with low o","D":"Both volumes rising with low o","E":"Both volumes rising with low o"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl47_q5","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Winter's Formula, Anion Gap & Delta Ratio","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man with type 1 diabetes has severe diarrhea. Labs show Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L), Cl- 108 mEq/L (reference range: 98–106 mEq/L), HCO3- 10 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and PaCO2 23 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg). Use 12 as the normal anion gap and 24 as the normal HCO3-. Which interpretation is correct?","choices":{"A":"Pure high anion gap acidosis","B":"High anion gap acidosis with alkalosis","C":"Pure normal anion gap acidosis","D":"High and normal anion gap acidoses","E":"Normal anion gap acidosis with alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The anion gap is 138 minus (108 plus 10), or 20, so the gap rose by 8 while HCO3- fell by 14, giving a delta ratio of about 0.6. A ratio below 0.8 (SDL 47 guide; below 1.0 in the Exam 1 notes) means HCO3- fell more than the gap explains, so a normal anion gap acidosis (here from diarrhea) coexists with the high anion gap ketoacidosis. Winter's formula predicts a PaCO2 of 23 plus or minus 2, so compensation is appropriate. Pure high anion gap acidosis would give a ratio of 1 to 2. High anion gap acidosis with alkalosis would give a ratio above 2. Pure normal anion gap acidosis ignores the raised gap. Normal anion gap acidosis with alkalosis fits neither the gap nor the ratio. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 47, anion gap and delta ratio); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (anion gap).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a delta ratio below 0.8 (below 1.0 in the Exam 1 notes) means a mixed high and normal anion gap acidosis, and above 2 means an added metabolic alkalosis. COMLEX: DKA with diarrhea is a classic mixed picture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 -> Obj 3 — RTAs are another cause of normal anion gap acidosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"High anion gap acidosis with a","E":"Normal anion gap acidosis with"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl47_q6","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — OPP Innervation Levels & Chapman Reflex Points","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has dysuria, urinary frequency, and suprapubic discomfort from acute cystitis. During an osteopathic examination, the physician palpates for the anterior Chapman reflex points of the bladder. Where are these points located?","choices":{"A":"1 inch above and 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus","B":"At the tip of the twelfth rib","C":"Intercostal spaces 8 to 10 near the costal cartilages","D":"Around the umbilicus and on the pubic symphysis","E":"Along the anterior iliotibial band of the thigh"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The SDL 47 OPP summary places the anterior Chapman reflex points of the bladder periumbilically and at the superior border of the pubic symphysis, and the course Chapman chart likewise describes them at the umbilicus and on the pubic symphysis, with the posterior point at the superior edge of the L2 transverse process. Points 1 inch above and 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus belong to the kidney, the tip of the twelfth rib to the appendix, intercostal spaces 8 to 10 to the small intestine, and the iliotibial band to the large intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bladder sympathetics arise from T11 to L2, and its parasympathetics come from the pelvic splanchnic nerves at S2 to S4. COMLEX: the course chart also lists an inhibitory pubic symphysis point for enuresis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 6 — Chapman reflex points of the urinary tract","isHighYield":false}],"48":[{"id":"sdl48_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature is more characteristic of primary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis than of minimal change disease?","choices":{"A":"Highly selective loss of albumin in the urine","B":"Poor response to corticosteroid therapy","C":"Normal glomeruli on light microscopy","D":"Remission in over 90 percent of treated children","E":"Absence of hypertension and hematuria"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary FSGS is a diffuse podocytopathy in the same spectrum as minimal change disease, but sclerosis involves part of the tuft in a subset of glomeruli. Compared with minimal change disease it has a higher incidence of hematuria, reduced GFR, and hypertension, more nonselective proteinuria, and a poor response to corticosteroids, and at least 50 percent of patients reach ESKD within 10 years. Minimal change disease shows normal glomeruli by light microscopy, highly selective albuminuria, and a response to steroids in more than 90 percent of children.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults in the United States, particularly in patients of Hispanic and African descent, and high-risk APOL1 alleles are a recognized cause. COMLEX: NPHS2 podocin mutations are one cause of steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome of childhood.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 and SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and ultrastructure separate the primary nephrotic podocytopathies and the crescentic glomerulonephritides","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Compared with minimal change d"}},{"id":"sdl48_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":1,"stem":"Which ultrastructural finding characterizes membranous nephropathy?","choices":{"A":"Subepithelial deposits with basement membrane spikes","B":"Subepithelial humps with luminal neutrophils","C":"Basket-weave lamination of the lamina densa","D":"Electron-dense deposits confined to the mesangium","E":"Diffuse foot process effacement with no deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In membranous nephropathy electron microscopy shows irregular electron-dense immune complex deposits between the basement membrane and the overlying epithelial cells, and podocytes lay down basement membrane material between the deposits, which appears as spikes on silver stain. Discrete humps with neutrophils describe poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, basket-weave lamination describes Alport syndrome, mesangial deposits describe IgA nephropathy, and effacement without deposits describes minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PLA2R is the target antigen in about 60 percent of membranous nephropathy, and IgG4 is the principal immunoglobulin deposited. COMLEX: rule out secondary causes such as malignancy, infection, and SLE, and expect nonselective proteinuria that responds poorly to corticosteroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 -> Obj 3 and SDL 32 -> Obj 2 — light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and ultrastructure separate the primary nephrotic podocytopathies and the crescentic glomerulonephritides","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Discrete humps with neutrophil"}},{"id":"sdl48_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which statement best describes the clinical course of idiopathic focal segmental glomerulosclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Spontaneous remission occurs in most adults","B":"Most patients respond fully to corticosteroids","C":"Children generally fare better than adults","D":"Renal function stays stable despite proteinuria","E":"Most patients reach renal failure within a year"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins states that spontaneous remission is rare in idiopathic FSGS, that responses to corticosteroid therapy are variable, and that children in general have a better prognosis than adults. Progression to renal failure occurs at variable rates; about 20 percent of patients follow an unusually rapid course with intractable massive proteinuria that ends in renal failure within 2 years. Stable renal function despite massive proteinuria is the typical course of minimal change disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the degree of proteinuria, the degree of renal insufficiency at diagnosis, and the collapsing variant predict rapid progression. COMLEX: FSGS recurs in 25 to 50 percent of kidney transplant recipients, sometimes within 24 hours, pointing to a circulating factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — primary nephrotic podocytopathies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":1,"stem":"According to Robbins, which disorder is the most common cause of acute kidney injury, accounting for about half of cases in hospitalized patients?","choices":{"A":"Acute tubular injury","B":"Acute interstitial nephritis","C":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","D":"Papillary necrosis","E":"Bilateral urinary tract obstruction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins describes acute tubular injury as the most common cause of acute kidney injury, accounting for some 50 percent of cases in hospitalized patients; it is generally reversible and arises in ischemic settings such as shock or with nephrotoxins such as gentamicin and radiographic contrast. Acute interstitial nephritis, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, papillary necrosis, and bilateral obstruction all cause acute kidney injury but far less often.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pathologists prefer the term acute tubular injury because necrosis may be absent despite acute renal failure. COMLEX: combined ischemic and nephrotoxic injury occurs with hemoglobinuria from mismatched transfusions and myoglobinuria from muscle injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — ischemic versus nephrotoxic acute tubular injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops fever, rash, and a rising creatinine after starting methicillin. Which clinical feature of this kidney injury best supports an immune mechanism rather than direct drug toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Severity that rises steadily with cumulative dose","B":"Recurrence after re-exposure to a related drug","C":"Necrosis confined to proximal tubules","D":"Granular casts of sloughed tubular cells","E":"Crystal deposits within collecting ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins lists the clinical evidence for an immune mechanism: the latent period, eosinophilia and rash, onset of nephropathy that is not dose related, and recurrence of the clinical and pathologic findings after re-exposure to the same or a chemically related drug. Dose-dependent severity, necrosis confined to proximal tubules, granular casts of sloughed cells, and intratubular crystals point to direct toxic or obstructive tubular injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: increased IgE and IgE-containing plasma cells suggest a type I late-phase reaction, whereas granulomas and positive drug skin tests suggest type IV hypersensitivity. COMLEX: withdrawal of the drug is followed by recovery, although it may take several months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — hapten-mediated immunopathology of drug-induced interstitial nephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":1,"stem":"According to First Aid, which urinary finding is expected in acute kidney injury from acute tubular necrosis, in contrast to prerenal azotemia?","choices":{"A":"Urine osmolality above 500 mOsm/kg","B":"Urine Na+ below 20 mEq/L","C":"Serum BUN/creatinine ratio above 20","D":"Fractional excretion of Na+ above 2%","E":"Fractional excretion of Na+ below 1%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In intrinsic renal failure such as acute tubular necrosis, damaged tubules cannot reabsorb sodium or concentrate urine, so First Aid lists a fractional excretion of sodium above 2 percent, urine sodium above 40 mEq/L, urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg, and a BUN/creatinine ratio below 15. Urine osmolality above 500 mOsm/kg, urine sodium below 20 mEq/L, a BUN/creatinine ratio above 20, and a fractional excretion of sodium below 1 percent are the pattern of prerenal azotemia, in which intact tubules avidly reabsorb sodium, water, and urea.","boardPrep":"Step 1: muddy brown granular casts in the urine also point to acute tubular necrosis. COMLEX: in postrenal azotemia from bilateral outflow obstruction, these indices vary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — BUN/creatinine ratio in prerenal and intrinsic azotemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Urine osmolality above 500 mOs","E":"Urine osmolality above 500 mOs"}},{"id":"sdl48_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug can cause hyperkalemic (type 4) renal tubular acidosis by reducing aldosterone production?","choices":{"A":"Amphotericin B","B":"Acetazolamide","C":"Hydrochlorothiazide","D":"Furosemide","E":"Heparin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"First Aid lists causes of type 4 RTA as either decreased aldosterone production, including diabetic hyporeninism, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs, heparin, cyclosporine, and adrenal insufficiency, or aldosterone resistance from potassium-sparing diuretics, obstructive nephropathy, and TMP-SMX. Amphotericin B toxicity causes distal (type 1) RTA, acetazolamide causes proximal (type 2) RTA, and hydrochlorothiazide and furosemide cause hypokalemia rather than a hyperkalemic acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in type 4 RTA, hyperkalemia suppresses proximal ammonia synthesis, so urine can still be acidified but total acid excretion is low. COMLEX: urine pH is variable in type 4 RTA, unlike the consistently high pH of type 1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — hypoaldosteronism and hyperkalemia in type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":1,"stem":"Which urine pH finding is characteristic of untreated type 1 renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Variable, depending on the plasma bicarbonate level","B":"Below 4.4 whenever plasma bicarbonate is low","C":"Between 4.4 and 5.0 with a positive urinary anion gap","D":"Above 5.5 even when plasma bicarbonate is low","E":"Near 7.4 with a negative urinary anion gap"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the alpha-intercalated cells cannot secrete H+ adequately, so the urine cannot be acidified below about 5.5 even during systemic acidemia. A variable pH that depends on plasma bicarbonate is the type 2 pattern, in which urine acidifies once plasma bicarbonate falls below the lowered threshold. A pH near 4.4 is the physiologic minimum, and an acidic urine with a positive urinary anion gap fits type 4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA is associated with amphotericin B, autoimmune disease such as SLE, and obstruction, and with calcium phosphate stones. COMLEX: hypokalemia and a normal anion gap accompany the persistently alkaline urine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 to 4 — the defect mechanism, urine pH, and serum potassium separate the renal tubular acidoses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with HIV is hospitalized for cryptococcal meningitis and receives several drugs. Two weeks later, serum Na+ is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 114 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Urine pH is 6.6 on repeated samples. Which drug most likely caused his acid-base disorder?","choices":{"A":"Amphotericin B","B":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","C":"Unfractionated heparin","D":"Fluconazole","E":"Spironolactone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"He has a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (138 − [114 + 16] = 8 mEq/L) with hypokalemia and a urine pH persistently above 5.5, the pattern of distal (type 1) RTA, and First Aid lists amphotericin B toxicity as a cause. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, heparin, and spironolactone cause hyperkalemic type 4 RTA by aldosterone resistance or reduced aldosterone production, so they would raise rather than lower potassium. Fluconazole is not a recognized cause of RTA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 1 RTA raises the risk of calcium phosphate stones because of the high urine pH and increased bone turnover from buffering. COMLEX: other First Aid causes of type 1 RTA include analgesic nephropathy, congenital urinary tract obstruction, and autoimmune diseases such as SLE.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell failure in type 1 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding is most often associated with calcium oxalate stones?","choices":{"A":"Hyperuricemia with persistently acidic urine","B":"Hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia","C":"Genetic loss of renal amino acid reabsorption","D":"Elevated urinary citrate with low urinary oxalate","E":"Persistently dilute urine with a high daily volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 55 percent of calcium oxalate stones are associated with hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia, from absorptive hypercalciuria, renal hypercalciuria, or idiopathic fasting hypercalciuria with normal parathyroid function. Hypercalcemia from hyperparathyroidism and similar states accounts for about 5 percent. Hyperuricemia with acidic urine favors uric acid stones and inherited amino acid loss produces cystine stones, while citrate and a high urine volume are protective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most important determinant of stone formation is supersaturation of urine with the stone constituents, and low urine volume promotes it. COMLEX: First Aid describes calcium oxalate crystals as envelope-shaped or dumbbell-shaped, and stones are radiopaque.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — crystal chemistry, urinary pH, and radiodensity define the major renal stone types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Hyperuricemia with acidic urin"}},{"id":"sdl48_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":1,"stem":"In a patient with cystinuria, which drug is added when hydration, sodium restriction, and urine alkalinization fail to prevent new stones?","choices":{"A":"Allopurinol","B":"Hydrochlorothiazide","C":"Probenecid","D":"Losartan","E":"Tiopronin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"First Aid lists treatment of cystine stones as a low-sodium diet and alkalinization of the urine, with chelating agents such as tiopronin or penicillamine if the stones are refractory; chelation forms a more soluble complex with cystine. Allopurinol lowers urate production for uric acid stones, hydrochlorothiazide lowers urinary calcium for calcium stones, probenecid is a uricosuric agent that would not help, and losartan is an angiotensin receptor blocker with no role in stone prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria is an autosomal recessive defect of the PCT transporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine, and cystine forms hexagonal crystals. COMLEX: cystine stones are faintly radiopaque, can form staghorn calculi, and are detected with the sodium cyanide-nitroprusside test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — cystine stone chemistry and urine pH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":1,"stem":"According to First Aid, which exposure predisposes to calcium oxalate kidney stones?","choices":{"A":"Urease-producing urinary infection","B":"Tumor lysis during chemotherapy","C":"High-dose vitamin C supplementation","D":"Inherited loss of a dibasic amino acid transporter","E":"Persistently acidic urine from gout"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid lists ethylene glycol ingestion, vitamin C overuse, hypocitraturia, and malabsorption such as Crohn disease as causes of calcium oxalate stones; excess vitamin C is metabolized to oxalate. Urease-producing infections cause struvite stones, tumor lysis and acidic urine with gout favor uric acid stones, and loss of the transporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine causes cystine stones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium oxalate crystals are envelope or dumbbell shaped, and treatment includes thiazides, citrate, and a low-sodium diet. COMLEX: Robbins notes that enteric hyperoxaluria from intestinal overabsorption is more common than hereditary primary oxaluria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — calcium stone chemistry and metabolic causes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal cell carcinoma subtype arises from intercalated cells of the collecting duct, shows multiple chromosome losses with extreme hypodiploidy, and carries an excellent prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Clear cell carcinoma","B":"Papillary carcinoma","C":"Collecting duct carcinoma","D":"Xp11 translocation carcinoma","E":"Chromophobe carcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins describes chromophobe renal cell carcinoma as about 5 percent of renal cell cancers, composed of cells with prominent membranes and pale eosinophilic cytoplasm with perinuclear halos, showing multiple chromosome losses and extreme hypodiploidy, thought to arise from intercalated cells, and having an excellent prognosis compared with clear cell and papillary cancers. Clear cell carcinoma shows 3p loss, papillary carcinoma shows trisomies 7 and 17, collecting duct carcinoma arises from medullary collecting duct cells without a distinct pattern, and Xp11 tumors carry TFE3 translocations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: histologic distinction of chromophobe carcinoma from oncocytoma can be difficult because both arise from intercalated cells. COMLEX: oncocytoma is benign, with eosinophilic cells packed with mitochondria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — molecular and histologic subtypes of renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Clear cell carcinoma shows 3p ","C":"Clear cell carcinoma shows 3p "}},{"id":"sdl48_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal cell carcinoma subtype often occurs in young patients and is defined by rearrangements that cause overexpression of the transcription factor TFE3?","choices":{"A":"Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","B":"Xp11 translocation carcinoma","C":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma","D":"Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","E":"Collecting duct carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins describes Xp11 translocation renal cell carcinoma as a genetically distinct subtype that often occurs in young patients and is defined by translocations of the TFE3 gene at Xp11.2 with several partner genes, all of which overexpress the TFE3 transcription factor; its morphology varies with the partner gene. Clear cell carcinoma is driven by VHL loss on 3p, papillary carcinoma by trisomies 7 and 17 and MET, chromophobe carcinoma by multiple chromosome losses, and collecting duct carcinoma has no distinct cytogenetic pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the growth patterns of TFE3-rearranged carcinoma include nested, solid, and tubulopapillary forms. COMLEX: most renal cancers are sporadic, and familial forms make up only about 4 percent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — molecular and histologic subtypes of renal cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":1,"stem":"Which cytogenetic alteration is found in essentially all invasive testicular germ cell tumors, regardless of histologic type?","choices":{"A":"Deletion of chromosome 3p","B":"Trisomy of chromosomes 7 and 17","C":"Isochromosome 12p","D":"Gain of 9q","E":"Translocation of Xp11.2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins states that progression to full-blown germ cell tumors is strongly associated with reduplication of the short arm of chromosome 12, isochromosome 12p, which is invariably found in invasive germ cell tumors regardless of histologic type. Gain of 9q characterizes spermatocytic tumor, which lacks isochromosome 12p and germ cell neoplasia in situ. Deletion of 3p, trisomies 7 and 17, and Xp11.2 translocations are renal cell carcinoma alterations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: germ cell neoplasia in situ, the precursor lesion, is found in about 90 percent of testes with germ cell tumors and retains OCT3/4 and NANOG expression. COMLEX: about 70 percent of individuals with germ cell neoplasia in situ develop invasive tumors within 5 to 7 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 3 — classification of testicular germ cell tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Gain of 9q characterizes sperm"}},{"id":"sdl48_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter is the site of action of loop diuretics?","choices":{"A":"Na+-Cl- symporter of the distal convoluted tubule","B":"Epithelial sodium channel of the collecting duct","C":"Carbonic anhydrase of the proximal tubule","D":"Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the ascending limb","E":"Mineralocorticoid receptor of the principal cell"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter in the luminal membrane of the thick ascending limb. Blocking it also reduces the lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption, so both are lost in the urine. Thiazides block the Na+/Cl- symporter, amiloride and triamterene block the epithelial sodium channel, acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase, and spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics are first-line for pulmonary edema and severe volume overload, and cause hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, ototoxicity, and hypocalcemia. COMLEX: NSAIDs blunt their effect by inhibiting the prostaglandins that mediate renal vasodilation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 -> Obj 1 and 3 and SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — the site and mechanism of action of each diuretic class and of the androgen pathway inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":1,"stem":"By which mechanism do thiazide diuretics raise serum uric acid?","choices":{"A":"Competing with urate for tubular secretion","B":"Increasing hepatic purine synthesis","C":"Blocking urate excretion in the distal tubule","D":"Activating xanthine oxidase in the liver","E":"Inhibiting uricase in the collecting duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thiazide brick gives two mechanisms for hyperuricemia: volume contraction increases proximal reabsorption of uric acid, and because thiazides are secreted by the same transporter as uric acid, they competitively block urate secretion. Thiazides do not increase hepatic purine synthesis, act on urate in the distal tubule, activate xanthine oxidase, or inhibit uricase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides are hyperGLUC: hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia. COMLEX: the rise in uric acid can increase gout flares, so thiazides should be avoided in patients with gout.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 4 — metabolic complications of tubular diuretics","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Thiazides do not increase hepa"}},{"id":"sdl48_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":1,"stem":"Chronic acetazolamide therapy increases the risk of which type of kidney stone?","choices":{"A":"Uric acid","B":"Cystine","C":"Calcium phosphate","D":"Xanthine","E":"Magnesium ammonium phosphate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor brick explains that the bicarbonaturia these drugs cause alkalinizes the urine, which may lead to formation of calcium phosphate stones; the drugs also produce a proximal (type 2) RTA with hypokalemia. Uric acid and cystine stones form in acidic urine, xanthine stones are not described with these drugs, and magnesium ammonium phosphate stones require infection with urease-producing bacteria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: other adverse effects of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors include paresthesias and tinnitus. COMLEX: these drugs contain a sulfonamide group and are avoided in patients with a sulfa allergy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — proximal carbonic anhydrase inhibition and acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man develops hypertension, edema, and a rising BUN 2 weeks after a streptococcal skin infection. Antistreptococcal antibody titers are high, serum C3 is low, and biopsy shows diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis with subepithelial humps. His case is sporadic rather than part of an outbreak. According to Robbins, which statement about his prognosis is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"More than 95 percent recover, as children do","B":"About 60 percent of such cases recover promptly","C":"Nearly all progress to end-stage disease within a year","D":"His outlook matches that of epidemic childhood cases","E":"Most develop nephrotic-range membranous disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins states that poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is less benign in adults: although the overall prognosis in epidemics is good, only about 60 percent of sporadic cases recover promptly, and in the rest the glomerular lesions fail to resolve quickly, with persistent proteinuria, hematuria, and hypertension. Some of these eventually clear, but others develop chronic glomerulonephritis or even RPGN. Recovery in more than 95 percent describes affected children, so his outlook is worse than that of epidemic childhood cases; rapid progression to end-stage disease is not the rule, and membranous disease is not the expected outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults the onset is more likely to be atypical, such as the sudden appearance of hypertension or edema with an elevated BUN. COMLEX: prolonged, persistent heavy proteinuria and an abnormal GFR mark an unfavorable prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — nephritic pathophysiology and poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has edema and proteinuria of 6 g/day. He has had chronic hepatitis B infection for 10 years. Biopsy shows diffuse capillary wall thickening with subepithelial deposits and basement membrane spikes, and serum antibodies to the phospholipase A2 receptor are negative. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Primary membranous nephropathy from PLA2R autoimmunity","B":"Minimal change disease from podocyte cytokine injury","C":"Membranous nephropathy secondary to hepatitis B","D":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis with humps","E":"Collapsing FSGS from APOL1 risk alleles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diffuse capillary wall thickening with subepithelial deposits and spikes is membranous nephropathy, and Robbins lists infections, including chronic hepatitis B, hepatitis C, syphilis, schistosomiasis, and malaria, among its secondary causes, along with malignant tumors, SLE, and other autoimmune disorders. Negative PLA2R antibodies argue against the primary autoimmune form. Minimal change disease shows normal light microscopy, poststreptococcal disease is a nephritic illness with low C3, and collapsing FSGS shows collapse of the tuft rather than subepithelial deposits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 10 to 15 percent of glomerulonephritis in SLE is of the membranous type. COMLEX: carcinomas of the lung and colon and melanoma are present in as many as 5 to 10 percent of adults with membranous nephropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — primary and secondary membranous nephropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy has recurrent hematuria and sensorineural hearing loss. His maternal uncle began dialysis at age 27, and slit-lamp examination shows lens dislocation. Which molecular abnormality is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen","B":"Circulating autoantibodies against nephrin","C":"Defective galactosylation of IgA1 hinge glycans","D":"Mutation of the alpha-5 chain of type IV collagen","E":"Autoantibodies against the phospholipase A2 receptor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hematuria with sensorineural deafness, lens dislocation, and an affected maternal uncle indicates X-linked Alport syndrome, present in about 85 percent of cases. Mutations affect the collagen IV alpha chains, and the X-linked form involves COL4A5, which impairs assembly of the GBM, lens, and cochlear collagen network. Alpha-3 antibodies cause anti-GBM disease, anti-nephrin antibodies cause some minimal change disease, abnormal IgA1 glycosylation causes IgA nephropathy, and PLA2R antibodies cause membranous nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: electron microscopy shows GBM thickening with splitting and lamination of the lamina densa, a basket-weave appearance. COMLEX: about 90 percent of affected males reach ESRD by age 40, while female heterozygotes typically present with hematuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — type IV collagen defects and their renal, ocular, and cochlear phenotypes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Alpha-3 antibodies cause anti-","E":"Alpha-3 antibodies cause anti-"}},{"id":"sdl48_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man is on postoperative day 2 after emergency repair of a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm complicated by 40 minutes of intraoperative hypotension. Urine output has fallen to 15 mL/h, and creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 3.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) with a BUN of 38 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL). Urine sodium is 58 mEq/L (reference range: varies with intake), FENa is 3 percent, and urine osmolality is 300 mOsm/kg (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/kg). Urinalysis shows muddy brown granular casts. Which mechanism most directly sustains his reduced GFR?","choices":{"A":"Sloughed tubular cells obstruct lumens while filtrate leaks into the interstitium","B":"Mesangial immune complexes that activate complement and recruit neutrophils","C":"Fibrin thrombi in glomerular capillaries from endothelial injury","D":"Ischemic necrosis of papillary tips from compression of medullary vessels","E":"Hapten-modified tubular antigens that provoke an eosinophil-rich infiltrate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Postoperative hypotension followed by oliguria, muddy brown casts, high urine sodium, FENa above 2 percent, and urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg is ischemic acute tubular injury. Injured cells detach from the basement membranes and obstruct lumens, raising intratubular pressure, and glomerular filtrate leaks back into the interstitium, causing edema and further ischemia; increased distal sodium delivery also constricts the afferent arteriole through tubuloglomerular feedback. The other options describe glomerulonephritis, thrombotic microangiopathy, papillary necrosis, and interstitial nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in ATN First Aid gives urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg, urine sodium above 40 mEq/L, and FENa above 2 percent, compared with prerenal azotemia. COMLEX: initiation lasts about 36 hours, maintenance urine output is 40 to 400 mL per day, and recovery can reach 3 L per day with electrolyte loss and susceptibility to infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 3 — intrarenal loops that maintain a depressed filtration rate after tubular injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The other options describe glo","C":"The other options describe glo","D":"The other options describe glo","E":"The other options describe glo"}},{"id":"sdl48_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman started ampicillin 12 days ago for a skin infection. She now has fever, a diffuse maculopapular rash, and a creatinine that has risen from 0.9 to 2.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Urinalysis shows leukocytes with eosinophils, a few red cells, and mild proteinuria, without red cell casts. Which mechanism best explains her kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Direct dose-dependent cytotoxicity to proximal tubular epithelium","B":"Prolonged hypoperfusion that causes ischemic tubular necrosis","C":"Subepithelial immune complex deposition that activates complement","D":"Compression of medullary vessels that causes papillary infarction","E":"Drug-modified tubular antigens that provoke an immune response"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fever, rash, eosinophiluria, and rising creatinine days to weeks after starting a synthetic penicillin indicate drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis. Drugs bind to tubular cell membrane or extracellular components to create modified self antigens that evoke an IgE-mediated or T cell-mediated response against tubular cells and their basement membranes. Onset is not dose related, unlike direct toxic injury, and glomeruli are normal, which excludes immune complex glomerulonephritis. Compression of medullary vessels causes papillary necrosis, which does not produce fever, rash and eosinophiluria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: synthetic penicillins such as methicillin and ampicillin, rifampin, thiazides, NSAIDs, and allopurinol are recognized causes, and a rash occurs in about 25 percent of patients. COMLEX: rising creatinine or oliguria develops in about 50 percent, and stopping the drug is followed by recovery that may take months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — drug-associated interstitial inflammation and its clinical markers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes has flank pain and gross hematuria. He passes a fragment of tan tissue in his urine, and pathology shows coagulative necrosis with preserved tubule outlines. Which mechanism best explains the lesion?","choices":{"A":"Direct dose-dependent toxicity to proximal convoluted tubular cells","B":"Ischemia from compression or obstruction of medullary blood vessels","C":"Immune complex deposition in the papillary interstitium with complement activation","D":"Hypersensitivity reaction with eosinophils in the medullary interstitium","E":"Supersaturation and crystal precipitation within the papillary collecting ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Passage of necrotic papillae with gross hematuria in a diabetic patient indicates renal papillary necrosis, which shows ischemic coagulative necrosis with preserved outlines of tubules. In all cases it is caused by ischemia from compression or obstruction of small vessels in the medulla, and in diabetes vascular compromise arises from microvascular disease. Direct toxicity, immune complexes, hypersensitivity infiltrates, and crystal precipitation do not produce this lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: papillary necrosis is associated with diabetes, sickle cell disease or trait, urinary tract obstruction, analgesic nephropathy, and pyelonephritis. COMLEX: necrotic papillae can obstruct the ureter and cause renal colic, and interstitial edema in obstruction or inflammation compresses medullary vessels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 3 — medullary pyramid susceptibility in metabolic, hematologic, and obstructive disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct toxicity, immune comple","D":"Direct toxicity, immune comple"}},{"id":"sdl48_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has fatigue and muscle weakness. Labs show Na+ 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 115 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Urine pH is 6.5 on repeated samples, and urinalysis shows no glucose or protein. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 renal tubular acidosis","B":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","C":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","D":"Diarrhea-related bicarbonate loss","E":"Type 1 renal tubular acidosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"She has a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (139 − [115 + 15] = 9 mEq/L) with hypokalemia and a urine pH that stays above 5.5 despite acidemia, the pattern of distal (type 1) RTA from failed H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells, and First Aid lists autoimmune diseases such as SLE among its causes. In type 2 RTA the urine pH falls below 5.5 once plasma HCO3- drops under the lowered threshold, and glucosuria often accompanies Fanconi syndrome; type 4 RTA causes hyperkalemia, diabetic ketoacidosis raises the anion gap, and diarrhea leaves the kidney able to acidify the urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 1 RTA raises the risk of calcium phosphate stones because of the high urine pH and increased bone turnover. COMLEX: the urinary anion gap is positive in type 1 RTA because NH4+ excretion is impaired.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — diagnostic algorithm for RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy takes lisinopril and spironolactone. Labs show Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 5.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 110 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Urine pH is 5.1. Which mechanism best explains his impaired renal acid excretion despite this urine pH?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemia that suppresses proximal ammonium production","B":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cells to secrete protons into the lumen","C":"A lowered proximal threshold that wastes filtered bicarbonate","D":"Overproduction of organic acids that raises the anion gap","E":"Excess mineralocorticoid effect that drives urinary potassium loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His anion gap is 138 - 127 = 11 mEq/L, which is normal, and hyperkalemia with an acidic urine in a patient with diabetic nephropathy taking lisinopril and spironolactone identifies type 4 RTA. Hyperkalemia moves K+ into proximal cells and H+ out, raising intracellular pH and inhibiting glutaminase, so NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion fall. Urine pH of 5.1 shows the urine can still be acidified below 5.5, which argues against type 1, so the limit is the ammonium available to carry acid rather than proton secretion, and bicarbonate wasting describes type 2. Excess mineralocorticoid effect would drive K+ loss and hypokalemia, not his hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetic nephropathy with hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism, ACE inhibitors, and potassium-sparing diuretics are common causes of type 4 RTA. COMLEX: correcting the hyperkalemia lifts the glutaminase inhibition and improves acid excretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — how potassium balance and aldosterone status shape renal acid excretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Urine pH of 5.1 shows the urin"}},{"id":"sdl48_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has muscle weakness. Labs show Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 113 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 13 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Urine pH is 6.8 despite the acidemia. Which long-term complication is she at greatest risk for if the disorder is untreated?","choices":{"A":"Hypophosphatemic rickets","B":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest","C":"Medullary nephrocalcinosis","D":"Uric acid nephrolithiasis","E":"Cystine nephrolithiasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypokalemia, a normal anion gap of 138 - 126 = 12 mEq/L, and an alkaline urine despite acidemia identify type 1 RTA, and autoimmune disease such as SLE is a recognized cause. The persistently alkaline urine and acidosis-related loss of citrate favor calcium phosphate stones and nephrocalcinosis. Hypophosphatemic rickets is linked to Fanconi syndrome, hyperkalemia is not a feature, and uric acid and cystine stones form in acidic urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid associates type 1 RTA with calcium phosphate kidney stones, and amphotericin B, obstruction, and analgesic nephropathy are also causes. COMLEX: alkali therapy replaces the unexcreted acid load and reduces stone risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell function and its secondary complications","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Hypophosphatemic rickets is li"}},{"id":"sdl48_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has colicky right flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a 5 mm distal ureteral stone, and a plain abdominal radiograph shows a radiopaque density. Serum calcium and PTH are normal, urinalysis shows no bacteria, and 24-hour urinary calcium excretion is elevated. Which mechanism most likely explains his stone?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial urease activity that alkalinizes the urine","B":"Hypercalciuria that causes supersaturation of the urine","C":"Acidic urine that reduces uric acid solubility","D":"Inherited failure of tubular amino acid reabsorption","E":"Rapid cell turnover that raises uric acid production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A radiopaque stone with normal serum calcium and PTH but elevated urinary calcium reflects calcium oxalate stone disease from hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia, which accounts for about 55 percent of such stones. The key determinant of stone formation is supersaturation of the urine with stone constituents. Urease activity produces struvite stones, acidic urine and rapid cell turnover produce uric acid stones, and inherited amino acid loss produces cystine stones; uric acid stones are also radiolucent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: calcium stones are radiopaque, and First Aid lists thiazides, citrate, and a low-sodium diet as prevention. COMLEX: causes of calcium oxalate stones include hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia, and hyperuricosuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — supersaturation, urinary calcium handling, and calcium stone chemistry","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has had four episodes of kidney stones since age 8, and his brother has had similar episodes. Urine testing shows markedly increased cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine. Which defect is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"Excessive intestinal absorption of dietary calcium","B":"Overproduction of uric acid from rapid cell turnover","C":"Inherited loss of a proximal tubular transporter","D":"Chronic infection with urease-producing bacteria","E":"Deficient urinary citrate excretion from chronic diarrhea"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Childhood onset, a similarly affected sibling, and excess urinary cystine with the dibasic amino acids ornithine, lysine, and arginine indicate cystinuria, an inherited defect of the proximal tubular transporter that reabsorbs these amino acids. Cystine is poorly soluble in urine, so stones form, and they also form at low urinary pH. The other options describe the mechanisms of calcium, uric acid, struvite, and calcium stone formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria is autosomal recessive and the mnemonic is COLA for Cystine, Ornithine, Lysine, and Arginine. COMLEX: First Aid describes hexagonal crystals and a positive sodium cyanide-nitroprusside test, with treatment by hydration, urinary alkalinization, and chelators if refractory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — inherited tubular transport defects as a cause of stone formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other options describe the","D":"The other options describe the","E":"The other options describe the"}},{"id":"sdl48_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a long-term indwelling bladder catheter after a spinal cord injury has recurrent urinary infections, flank pain, and fever. Urine pH is 8.2. Imaging shows a large branching stone filling the renal pelvis and calyces. Which organism is most likely to be cultured?","choices":{"A":"Escherichia coli","B":"Enterococcus faecalis","C":"Streptococcus agalactiae","D":"Proteus mirabilis","E":"Bacteroides fragilis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A staghorn stone in alkaline urine after recurrent infection is a magnesium ammonium phosphate, or struvite, stone. Robbins names Proteus and some staphylococci as the urea-splitting bacteria that convert urea to ammonia, alkalinize the urine, and precipitate magnesium ammonium phosphate salts, forming some of the largest stones. The other organisms are not the urea-splitting organisms named in the source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists urease-positive organisms such as Proteus mirabilis, and coffin lid crystals. COMLEX: treatment requires eradicating the infection and surgically removing the stone, since stones predispose to superimposed infection and obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — infectious triggers and urinary pH in stone formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other organisms are not th","B":"The other organisms are not th","C":"The other organisms are not th","E":"The other organisms are not th"}},{"id":"sdl48_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man who smokes has 3 months of intermittent gross hematuria, left flank discomfort, and an 8 kg weight loss. Hemoglobin is 18.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and serum calcium is 12.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). CT shows a 10 cm left renal mass with tumor extending into the renal vein and inferior vena cava. Which pair of tumor-derived products best explains his laboratory abnormalities?","choices":{"A":"EPO and PTHrP","B":"ACTH and renin","C":"hCG and calcitonin","D":"TSH and ADH","E":"GH and prolactin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A large renal mass with renal vein extension and inferior vena cava involvement is clear cell renal cell carcinoma, which Robbins calls one of the great mimics because of hormone-related syndromes including polycythemia and hypercalcemia. Erythropoietin explains the erythrocytosis and parathyroid hormone-related peptide explains the hypercalcemia. ACTH and renin can also be produced by renal cell carcinoma but would cause Cushing syndrome and hypertension. hCG with calcitonin and GH with prolactin would not raise hemoglobin and calcium, and calcitonin lowers calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives the paraneoplastic mnemonic PEAR, for PTHrP, EPO, ACTH, and Renin. COMLEX: the most reliable clue is hematuria, and the tumor is often silent until large, with the lungs and bones the most common metastatic sites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — clear cell RCC paraneoplastic syndromes and renal vein invasion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has a large left flank mass. Nephrectomy shows a Wilms tumor with blastemal, stromal, and epithelial elements, and some areas contain cells with large, hyperchromatic, pleomorphic nuclei and abnormal mitoses. Which molecular alteration is most associated with this histologic feature?","choices":{"A":"Germline WT1 deletion on 11p13","B":"Loss of imprinting at 11p15","C":"TP53 loss of function","D":"MET activation","E":"Biallelic VHL inactivation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"About 5 percent of Wilms tumors are anaplastic, with large, hyperchromatic, pleomorphic nuclei and abnormal mitoses, and Robbins links anaplasia to TP53 mutations and resistance to chemotherapy; loss of p53-driven apoptosis may explain the poor response to cytotoxic drugs. Anaplastic histology is perhaps the most critical determinant of adverse prognosis. WT1 deletion underlies the WAGR syndrome, loss of imprinting at 11p15 underlies Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, and MET and VHL alterations drive renal cell carcinomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephrogenic rests, putative precursor lesions, lie next to 25 to 40 percent of unilateral tumors and nearly 100 percent of bilateral tumors. COMLEX: even anaplasia confined to the kidney increases the risk of recurrence and death.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 3 — pediatric nephroblastoma histology and genetics","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"WT1 deletion underlies the WAG"}},{"id":"sdl48_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man has a painless, firm right testicular mass. Radical orchiectomy shows a homogeneous, gray-white, lobulated mass without hemorrhage or necrosis. Microscopy shows sheets of uniform round cells with clear cytoplasm, distinct borders, and prominent central nucleoli, divided by fibrous septa infiltrated by lymphocytes. Serum AFP is normal and hCG is minimally elevated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Embryonal carcinoma","B":"Classic seminoma","C":"Yolk sac tumor","D":"Choriocarcinoma","E":"Postpubertal teratoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A homogeneous gray-white mass without hemorrhage or necrosis, with uniform cells, clear glycogen-rich cytoplasm, prominent nucleoli, and lymphocytic septa, is seminoma, the most common germ cell tumor. About 15 percent of seminomas contain syncytiotrophoblasts and cause a minimal rise in hCG. Embryonal carcinoma is variegated and hemorrhagic, yolk sac tumor raises AFP, choriocarcinoma causes marked hCG elevation, and teratoma has mixed tissue elements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma cells are positive for KIT, OCT3/4, and podoplanin and negative for cytokeratin. COMLEX: seminoma is radiosensitive and chemosensitive with the best prognosis, and more than 95 percent of stage I and II patients are cured.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 3 — histologic patterns and serum markers that separate germ cell tumor types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis and a history of altitude sickness plans a trek to 4,000 m and asks for acetazolamide. Which concern makes acetazolamide inadvisable for him?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemia from distal potassium retention","B":"Metabolic alkalosis from bicarbonate retention","C":"Ototoxicity from high peak drug levels","D":"Hypoglycemia from impaired gluconeogenesis","E":"Precipitation of hepatic encephalopathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor brick advises avoiding these drugs in patients with cirrhosis because they may precipitate hepatic encephalopathy; they are also contraindicated with a sulfa allergy and used with caution in pregnancy. Acetazolamide causes hypokalemia rather than hyperkalemia and a metabolic acidosis rather than an alkalosis, ototoxicity is the classic hazard of loop diuretics, and hypoglycemia is not a described effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide prevents altitude sickness by causing bicarbonaturia and a metabolic acidosis that drives hyperventilation. COMLEX: tinnitus and paresthesias are other recognized adverse effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 4 — extrarenal uses of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Acetazolamide causes hypokalem","C":"Acetazolamide causes hypokalem"}},{"id":"sdl48_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old postmenopausal woman has hypertension, osteopenia, and recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones with high 24-hour urinary calcium excretion. Which diuretic would best address both her blood pressure and stone recurrence?","choices":{"A":"Ethacrynic acid","B":"Acetazolamide","C":"Spironolactone","D":"Chlorthalidone","E":"Bumetanide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thiazides such as chlorthalidone block the Na+/Cl- symporter in the distal convoluted tubule, lower blood pressure, and increase renal calcium reabsorption, which lowers urinary calcium, reduces stone recurrence, and may help maintain bone density. Loop diuretics such as ethacrynic acid and bumetanide increase calcium and magnesium excretion, acetazolamide alkalinizes the urine and raises the risk of calcium phosphate stones, and spironolactone acts on the collecting duct and is not used to prevent calcium stones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides cause the hyperGLUC profile, for hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia, and hypercalcemia, and can cause hyponatremia and hypokalemic alkalosis. COMLEX: they are sulfonamides, should be avoided in gout, and are less potent than loop diuretics for volume overload.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — diuretic effects on urinary calcium and glomerular filtration thresholds","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man takes trazodone for depression and sildenafil for erectile dysfunction. He presents with a painful erection lasting 5 hours that is unrelated to sexual stimulation. Which mechanistic combination best explains the persistent erection?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-1 blockade plus prolonged cGMP-mediated relaxation","B":"Serotonin reuptake inhibition plus increased testosterone synthesis","C":"Dopamine receptor blockade plus reduced nitric oxide synthase activity","D":"Beta-2 stimulation plus inhibition of 5-alpha reductase","E":"Nitric oxide synthase inhibition plus increased venous outflow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Detumescence depends on sympathetic norepinephrine acting at alpha-1 receptors on cavernous smooth muscle to cause vasoconstriction and venous outflow. Trazodone has potent alpha-1 blocking activity, and sildenafil, a PDE5 inhibitor, prolongs cGMP-mediated smooth muscle relaxation, so the combination overwhelms detumescence and produces ischemic priapism. The other pairings describe mechanisms that would reduce erectile function or do not act on penile smooth muscle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDE5 inhibitors are contraindicated with nitrates because both raise cGMP and cause profound hypotension. COMLEX: ischemic priapism is a urologic emergency requiring treatment within 4 to 6 hours to prevent fibrosis, and alpha-1 blockers such as prazosin and tamsulosin are also implicated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 5 — how sympathetic tone and cGMP signaling regulate detumescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex4t_sdl48_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Podocytopathies & Glomerulonephritides","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old Black man with high-risk APOL1 alleles has nephrotic-range proteinuria, hypertension, and hematuria that do not respond to corticosteroids. Biopsy shows sclerosis in segments of some glomeruli. Which immunofluorescence finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Granular IgG and C3 along capillary loops","B":"Mesangial IgA with C3 deposits","C":"Linear IgG along the basement membrane","D":"IgM and C3 trapped in sclerotic areas","E":"Full-house staining with C1q"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome with segmental sclerosis in a patient with APOL1 risk alleles indicates focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. Immunofluorescence shows nonspecific entrapment of IgM and C3 in the sclerotic segments, not true immune complex deposition. Granular IgG and C3 along capillary loops fit membranous nephropathy or poststreptococcal disease. Linear IgG along the basement membrane fits anti-GBM disease. Mesangial IgA with C3 fits IgA nephropathy. Full-house staining with C1q fits lupus nephritis. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 43 and SDL 48, FSGS LM, IF, and EM); Robbins Ch. 20 (FSGS).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSGS shows segmental sclerosis, nonspecific IgM and C3 trapping, and diffuse effacement with podocyte detachment. COMLEX: FSGS is the most common primary nephrotic syndrome in Black adults and responds poorly to steroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 1 — podocytopathies differ in steroid response","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl48_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — ATN, AIN & Papillary Necrosis","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman develops fever, a maculopapular rash, and a creatinine of 2.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) 12 days after starting a proton pump inhibitor. Her eosinophil count is elevated. Which urinalysis finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Muddy brown granular casts","B":"WBC casts with urinary eosinophils","C":"RBC casts with dysmorphic RBCs","D":"Fatty casts with oval fat bodies","E":"Broad waxy casts with crystals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fever, rash, and eosinophilia after a new drug indicate acute interstitial nephritis, and its urine shows WBC casts, leukocyturia with urinary eosinophils, hematuria, and mild proteinuria. Muddy brown granular casts indicate acute tubular necrosis. RBC casts with dysmorphic RBCs indicate glomerulonephritis. Fatty casts with oval fat bodies indicate nephrotic syndrome. Broad waxy casts indicate advanced chronic kidney disease. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 48, ATN vs AIN laboratory findings); Robbins Ch. 20 (drug-induced interstitial nephritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: AIN means fever, rash, eosinophilia, WBC casts, and urinary eosinophils 2 to 40 days after a drug; ATN means muddy brown casts. COMLEX: stop the offending drug first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 -> Obj 4 — AIN histology shows interstitial eosinophils and tubulitis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl48_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Tubular Acidoses","batch":3,"stem":"A 49-year-old man who works at a battery-recycling plant has bone pain and glucosuria with normal serum glucose and calcium. His serum HCO3- is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) with a normal anion gap, and his serum phosphorus is 1.9 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL). Which bone complication best explains his pain?","choices":{"A":"Osteoporosis from estrogen loss","B":"Osteonecrosis from steroid use","C":"Osteoblastic metastatic deposits","D":"Osteitis from high PTH levels","E":"Osteomalacia from phosphate wasting"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lead exposure with glucosuria, hypophosphatemia, and a normal anion gap acidosis indicates Fanconi syndrome with type 2 RTA. Urinary phosphate wasting causes osteomalacia, or rickets in children. Osteoporosis from estrogen loss does not fit a middle-aged man with these labs. Osteonecrosis from steroid use is unsupported, because no steroid use is reported. Osteoblastic metastatic deposits fit prostate cancer, not tubular dysfunction. Osteitis from high PTH levels fits hyperparathyroidism with hypercalcemia, and his calcium is normal. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 48, type 2 RTA etiologies and osteomalacia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 2 RTA comes from Fanconi syndrome, acetazolamide, myeloma, or heavy metals, and phosphate wasting causes osteomalacia. COMLEX: ask about occupational lead or cadmium exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 -> Obj 3 — acetazolamide causes a drug-induced type 2 RTA","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl48_q4","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Urolithiasis Classification","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has her third calcium oxalate stone. Serum calcium is 11.9 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL), phosphate is 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 2.5–4.5 mg/dL), PTH is elevated, and 24-hour urine calcium is high. Which mechanism best explains her stones?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal overabsorption of oxalate from fat malabsorption","B":"Urease-producing infection alkalinizing the urine","C":"Low urine citrate from distal tubular acidosis","D":"Hypercalcemia raising the filtered calcium load","E":"Uric acid crystals nucleating calcium oxalate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins notes that about 5 percent of calcium oxalate stones occur with hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria, as in hyperparathyroidism, diffuse bone disease, sarcoidosis, and other hypercalcemic states. Her high calcium, low phosphate, and elevated PTH point to hyperparathyroidism, and the hypercalcemia raises the filtered calcium load enough to produce hypercalciuria and supersaturation. Enteric hyperoxaluria, urease-producing infection, hypocitraturia, and nucleation by uric acid crystals are other stone mechanisms, but none accounts for her hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 55 percent of calcium oxalate stone formers have hypercalciuria without hypercalcemia, from absorptive, renal, or idiopathic fasting hypercalciuria. COMLEX: PTH raises distal calcium reabsorption but blocks proximal phosphate reabsorption, giving high serum calcium with low phosphate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — metabolic causes of calcium stones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Enteric hyperoxaluria, urease-","C":"Enteric hyperoxaluria, urease-"}},{"id":"ex4t_sdl48_q5","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — RCC, Wilms Tumor & Germ Cell Tumors","batch":3,"stem":"A 32-year-old man has a testicular mass removed by radical orchiectomy. The tumor shows sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm and lymphocyte-rich septa. Which immunohistochemical profile best supports the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"KIT negative, cytokeratin positive","B":"OCT3/4 positive, cytokeratin positive","C":"AFP positive, KIT negative","D":"Beta-hCG positive, KIT negative","E":"KIT positive, OCT3/4 positive"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Uniform clear cells with lymphocyte-rich septa indicate seminoma, which is KIT (CD117) positive, OCT3/4 positive, podoplanin positive, and cytokeratin negative. OCT3/4 positive with cytokeratin positive fits embryonal carcinoma. AFP positive with KIT negative fits yolk sac tumor. Beta-hCG positive with KIT negative fits choriocarcinoma. KIT negative with cytokeratin positive fits a non-seminomatous or non-germ-cell tumor, not seminoma. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 40, immunohistochemistry of seminoma vs NSGCT); Robbins Ch. 21 (seminoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: seminoma is KIT positive, OCT3/4 positive, and cytokeratin negative; embryonal carcinoma is OCT3/4 positive, cytokeratin positive, and KIT negative. COMLEX: seminoma is very radiosensitive.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 3 — tumor markers in germ cell tumors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex4t_sdl48_q6","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Diuretics & Male Reproductive Therapeutics","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man takes tamsulosin for benign prostatic hyperplasia and is scheduled for cataract extraction. Which complication should his surgeon anticipate?","choices":{"A":"Acute angle-closure glaucoma","B":"Postoperative endophthalmitis","C":"Floppy iris during surgery","D":"Ischemic optic neuropathy","E":"Corneal endothelial loss"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Alpha-1 antagonists, especially tamsulosin, cause intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery, with a flaccid, billowing iris that tends to prolapse. Acute angle-closure glaucoma is not the classic tamsulosin risk. Postoperative endophthalmitis is an infection unrelated to alpha-1 blockade. Ischemic optic neuropathy is not a tamsulosin effect. Corneal endothelial loss is not linked to the drug. Sources: Exam 4 lecture notes (SDL 48, alpha-1 antagonist side effects); Katzung Ch. 10 (tamsulosin and intraoperative floppy iris syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-1 antagonists cause orthostatic hypotension, dizziness, retrograde ejaculation, and floppy iris syndrome. COMLEX: tell the ophthalmologist about tamsulosin before cataract surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 4 — alpha-1 blockers add to PDE5 inhibitor hypotension","isHighYield":false}]};
